2026-07-25 22:57:05,893 23302 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'restart must not grant an extra delivery attempt (tries exceeded maxTries: 6/5)', 'description': 'FOUND LIVE 2026-07-25 22:51. Ledger row ms0u8qv9.0 settled `done` with **tries=6/5** — one more\nattempt than maxTries allows.\n\nCause: a row interrupted at tries == maxTries is reloaded by _load(), selected by _deliverNext()\n(which filters on state, never on tries), incremented to maxTries+1, and only marked `failed`\nafter that extra attempt fails. Pre-existing — the old code reached the same place via\n`trying`->`queued` — so sticky `trying` did not introduce it.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS, and why deferring it was wrong: the ONLY reason this ping succeeded is that the\nrestart handed it an attempt it was not entitled to. Run without a restart it would have been\nmarked `failed` at try 5 and the pong arriving at 22:51:20 would have had NO live row to attach\nto — a genuinely lost receipt. I logged this as "costs one extra transmission, never a loop" and\nstopped; it cuts the other way too.\n\nFIX: guard in _deliverNext — if the selected row already has tries >= maxTries, settle it\n`failed` instead of attempting it. Test: seed a row at tries==maxTries in `trying`, load, call\nonHeard, assert deliver() was NOT called and the row is `failed`.'}
2026-07-25 22:57:16,257 23302 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'settled commands show their QUEUE time, not when they finished ("1h ago / done")', 'description': 'FOUND LIVE 2026-07-25 22:54 on node-dash /control. The GARG ping settled at 22:51:20 — three\nminutes earlier — and the Executed list labelled it **"1h ago · done"**. That is `createdAt`\n(21:45:12), not `settledAt`.\n\nSo a command that took 66 minutes to get an answer reads as though the answer arrived an hour\nago. On the Executed list the age a person wants is WHEN IT FINISHED.\n\nOUR HALF (the only half we own): we return `createdAt`, `triedAt`, `nextTryAt` and `settledAt`\nwith no steer on which to render, so the misleading one is the easy one to grab. Exactly the\nsame shape as the `queued` defect — the contract invited it. API.md must say plainly: for a\nsettled request show `settledAt`; `createdAt` is when it was ASKED FOR, and the two can be hours\napart on a 15-minute wake window.\n\nTHEIR HALF: which field the Executed list renders is node-dash\'s choice in their code. RAISE IT\non the chat channel with the measured example; do NOT assert what their fix should be.\n\nConsider also exposing a derived `waitedMs` (settledAt - createdAt) — the number that actually\ndescribes this link — but only if they say it would be used.'}
2026-07-25 23:07:59,494 23106 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'Control Executed list ages settled commands by createdAt, not settledAt (from mt-transport chat)', 'description': 'mt-transport report (mcpp-chat `mt-transport--node-dash` #25, [settled-age-wrong-timestamp]): GARG ping in Executed showed "1h ago / done" for a reply that landed 3 minutes earlier — the "1h ago" was createdAt (queue time), not settledAt (reply time). A 66-minute wait between queue and reply is normal for a sleeping unit, so the gap matters.\n\nConfirmed root cause: src/pac-host.js:110 computes `since: fmtAgo(Math.floor((e.createdAt ?? 0) / 1000))` unconditionally for every ledger row, Pending and Executed alike. `entry.since` is a pre-formatted string; app-control.js/tab-control.html just display it, no client-side timestamp selection exists. `settledAt` does not exist anywhere in our current data/contract (grep across src/ and public/ returns zero hits) — it was never part of the ledger-field-rename (task control-since-and-pending-split / commit history around pac-host.js:106-108). mt-transport\'s API.md now documents createdAt/triedAt/nextTryAt/settledAt with guidance: settled rows → settledAt, live rows (queued/trying) → createdAt.\n\nFix: once mt-transport\'s contract actually serves settledAt (confirm current shape via GET /v1/mesh/queue/:target or the ledger response), change src/pac-host.js:110 to pick createdAt for state in (queued, trying) and settledAt for everything else. Confirmed isolated — no other consumer (messages tab, SSE mesh.request-* — none exists here) has the same bug. Reply to mt-transport with the answer to their two questions (no need for a derived waitedMs since we can compute settledAt - createdAt ourselves; confirmed no other place bites) once addressed.'}
