2026-07-26 08:26:31,194 37757 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'MEASURED 2026-07-26, overnight probe 22:53-03:07 BST against GARG (!8cee336b, live/sleeping,\n15-min beat). 6 pings issued: 3 done, 3 failed. That 50% is NOT a link measurement — it is an\nartifact of OUR correlation. Peter found it by pushing; I had reported it as a signal result.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE (recorder CSV, from_id=8cee336b, to=2687afb1):\n  22:51:20 {"type":"pong","upt":19778}  -> credited\n  23:06:25 {"type":"pong","upt":21579}  -> credited\n  00:06:29 {"type":"pong","upt":24281}  -> DROPPED  (ms0x5zb1.0 was marked no_reply at 00:06:53,\n                                                     24 s AFTER its answer was in the building)\n  00:21:25 {"type":"pong","upt":25184}  -> credited to ms0zb5vp.1 — but it is the ANSWER TO THE\n                                          PREVIOUS REQUEST. Misattribution, not success.\n  01:36:37 {"type":"pong","upt":29689}  -> DROPPED\n  01:51:34 {"type":"pong","upt":30592}  -> credited\nGARG produced SIX pongs. We credited FOUR, and at least one of those was credited to the wrong row.\n\nCORRELATION EXISTS ON THE WIRE AND WE THROW IT AWAY. Live capture off ws://localhost:8001/events:\n  {"data":{"text":"{\\"type\\":\\"pong\\",...}","reply_id":293414252}}\nThe firmware threads it deliberately — pac-garage-alarm main.cpp:2772 sendReply(r, rx.id). That\ncapture settled ms1gcokx.r, which resolves ONLY via timing.js:88 (Number(replyId) === e.sentId),\nso the id equality is proven end-to-end on live traffic.\nVERIFIED SAFE: packet ids survive relaying unchanged — the same pkt_id arrives via relay=4 hl=2\nAND relay=80 hl=1 (5 of 46 GARG packets today arrived twice). So matching on it is sound, AND\nduplicate delivery is real and must be idempotent.\n\nTHREE PLACES WE DESTROY IT (clients/mesh/lib/timing.js):\n  :80   if (!this.inFlight) return false;      -> a valid reply_id is never even read\n  :88   compared ONLY to the current attempt   -> the answer to attempt N is a stranger to N+1\n  :142  timeout nulls inFlight                 -> sentId dies with it, and is never persisted\nNothing writes the sent packet id to the ledger. There is no column for it. (db.js has reply_id,\nbut that is the OUTBOUND threading option from butler.js:109 — a different thing.)\n\nWHY IT WILL STILL MATTER AFTER THE FIRMWARE IS FIXED: the reply is genuinely late by design on a\nsleeping unit. Root cause of the LATENCY is separate and lives in task tx-queue-not-stranded\n(MeshtasticTransport::sleep() at src/MeshtasticTransport.cpp:771 sleeps the radio with frames\nstill in _txq; send() only enqueues, and service() runs only from receive()). That needs a FLASH.\nTHIS task needs none, and it is what makes the accounting HONEST at unchanged latency.\n\nDECISION TAKEN (Peter, 2026-07-26): TERMINAL STATES STAY TERMINAL. A row that gave up stays\n`failed`; a late reply attaches to it as recorded evidence and is visible, but does not flip the\nstate. node-dash\'s Executed list must never see a settled row change under it.\n\nRULE FOR THIS TASK: a reply names the request it answers. Settle THAT row or none. Never settle a\nrow because it happened to be the one in flight.\n\nBAN: no acceptance claim that is not a number out of the ledger or the recorder. Peter: "I am not\ngoing to go thru this again only to find that what I think is coded is not."'}
2026-07-26 08:29:49,840 37757 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'wake-schedule-known', 'title': 'We can predict the wake window to ±2 s and we discard it — nextTryAt is always null and try 1 is fired blind into a sleeping radio', 'steps': ["Model keeps the BEAT, not just the last timestamp: interval from successive heard events, plus the device's own `beat` from the config reply", 'nextTryAt carries a real timestamp (lastHeard + beat) instead of null — and API.md stops saying it is unknowable', 'Stop firing the immediate try into a unit we can see is mid-sleep; schedule it into the predicted window instead', 'Re-derive maxTries/ttlMs from the measured beat rather than the inherited 5/24h', 'OBSERVE: measure transmissions-per-settled-request before and after']}
2026-07-26 08:30:04,798 37757 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'RAISED BY PETER 2026-07-26: "are we using the fact that we know when it slept and when it (should)\nwake up, or just discarding that valuable information?" Answer: discarding it entirely.\n\nMEASURED, so this is not speculation:\n  GARG\'s beat is 901 s device time / 900 s wall. Predicting the next beat as last+901s over 22\n  intervals gives median error 0 s, mean +0.7 s, 95% within +/-2 s. The RX window that follows is\n  SLEEP_WINDOW_MS = 8000 (pac-garage-alarm main.cpp:127). The device also REPORTS its interval:\n  the `config` reply carries "beat":900, and clients/mesh/lib/config.js:139 already reads it.\n\nWHAT WE ACTUALLY KEEP:\n  lib/model.js:19  heard(id, at) { ...prev, lastHeardMs: at }   <- OVERWRITES. No interval, no\n                                                                   history, no predicted wake.\n  lib/config.js:139 put(\'beat\', cfg.beat)                       <- into a transient values map for\n                                                                   a UI read. Never stored, never\n                                                                   scheduled against.\n  lib/butler.js:113 comment: nextTryAt is "gated on the unit\'s wake window and therefore\n                    UNKNOWABLE". THAT COMMENT IS NOW FALSE, and we shipped a DB column\n                    (db.js migration 3, next_try_at) that consequently never carries a value.\n                    node-dash was told the same thing.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS US, from the overnight probe 22:53-03:07:\n  1. nextTryAt is always null. It could read 08:07:01 to +/-2 s.\n  2. butler.js:129 fires the "immediate try" WITHOUT gating on wake state, reasoning that the only\n     signal then available (slp/mode) was unreliable. That was correct then; it is wrong now.\n     Try 1 of EVERY request last night was fired blind into a sleeping radio and not one was\n     answered. That is a wasted transmission on a shared mesh AND one of five tries spent.\n  3. `awake` is only retrospective ("heard 9 min ago"); it could say "asleep, next window in 4m12s".\n  4. maxTries 5 against a 900 s beat means a request lives ~75 min. Nobody chose that — it fell out\n     of an inherited default.\n\nORDER: AFTER reply-correlation. Not folded into it — correlation and scheduling are different\nconcerns and merging them makes neither result attributable, which is the failure mode Peter has\ntold me to stop repeating. It also DEPENDS on it: a wake predictor cannot be validated against\nsettle times until settle times are attributed to the right request.\n\nPULL FORWARD IF CHEAP: step 2 (nextTryAt carrying lastHeard+beat) is independent of the rest and is\nthe thing node-dash actually asked about.'}
