2026-07-26 08:33:33,052 37757 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'wake-schedule-known', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'ADJUDICATED 2026-07-26 — Peter: "should the firmware send a LWT before it sleeps?"\nANSWER: NO NEW FRAME. Recorded so it is not re-litigated.\n\nTwo different things hide behind "LWT", and they have opposite answers:\n\n(a) "going to sleep, back at T" — an ANNOUNCEMENT, not a will. It would make nextWake\n    authoritative rather than inferred. REJECTED: we already receive the interval on every beat\n    at zero cost. TELEMETRY_APP device_metrics carries uptime_seconds; two consecutive frames give\n    the beat exactly (07:36:59 upt=52213 -> 07:52:00 upt=53114 = 901 s device / 901 s wall). That\n    is how the beat was measured — 110 beats today, no firmware involved. Adding a frame per beat\n    would spend a FLASH plus recurring SF11 airtime on a primary-cell unit to tell us something\n    already present in traffic we receive anyway, accurate to +/-2 s over 22 intervals.\n\n(b) TRUE LWT semantics — detect an UNPLANNED death (crash, wedged radio, flat cell). The device\n    fundamentally CANNOT provide this. An MQTT LWT works because a BROKER holds the message and\n    publishes it when the client dies; nothing here holds anything on the unit\'s behalf. A dead\n    node sends nothing, and silence is exactly what we have today.\n    THE LWT WE WANT IS ON OUR SIDE: once nextWake exists, a wake that passes with nothing heard IS\n    the death signal. Free, strictly better than anything the firmware could send, and a direct\n    consequence of the per-device nextWake field. ADD IT AS A STEP OF THIS TASK.\n\nWHAT WOULD CHANGE THE ANSWER: PIR cuts sleep short (doSleep breaks on pirTriggers,\npac-garage-alarm main.cpp:3409), so on a busy day the beat can be perturbed in a way last night\'s\nquiet data cannot show. If measurement finds that happening, the fix is STILL not a new frame — it\nis folding the value into a frame we already send.'}
2026-07-26 08:33:42,289 37757 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'wake-schedule-known', 'title': 'Missed-wake detection — the real LWT, and it lives on OUR side', 'description': 'Once nextWake is a per-device field, a wake that passes with nothing heard IS the death signal:\ncrash, wedged radio, flat cell. The device cannot report its own unplanned death — an MQTT LWT\nworks because a broker holds the message; nothing holds anything on this unit\'s behalf.\n\nToday we have only silence, and silence is indistinguishable from a quiet night. With nextWake we\ncan say "expected at 08:07:01, nothing heard by 08:07:30" — which for a unit that is a 45-minute\ndrive away is the difference between noticing and not.\n\nGate it on MISSED WAKES, not on elapsed time: one missed beat is a lost packet, N consecutive\nmissed beats is a dead node. N declared in settings, never inline (every-timeout-configurable).\nTwo beats were missed overnight 2026-07-26 (30-minute gaps at 01:06 and 02:21) on a unit that was\ndemonstrably healthy — so N must be greater than 1, and the number should come from measurement.\n\nDo NOT surface it as a boolean `alive`. Report what was measured: nextWake, missedWakes, and\nlastHeard. A consumer decides what to call dead.'}
