2026-07-26 13:48:25,980 70497 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': "CONFIRMED: signal_history dedup (num,packet_id) discards the second radio's independent measurement of the same packet", 'description': '**Status:** CONFIRMED · **Found during:** signal-provenance-mixed-source Phase 1 (2026-07-26)\n\n`src/db.js:612-615` — `INSERT OR IGNORE INTO signal_history`, with\n`CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_sig_dedup ON signal_history(num, packet_id) WHERE packet_id IS NOT NULL`.\n\n`src/persist.js:92-93` states the intent outright: *"Deduped by (num, packet_id) so N gateway\nradios hearing one broadcast yield one row."*\n\n**Why it is a defect:** with two gateway radios (OMNI + YAGI) this deliberately throws away a\nreal, independent measurement. When both hear the same broadcast, the two RSSI/SNR readings are\nthe *most* valuable data we get — they are a diversity comparison of the two antennas on an\nidentical packet, which is exactly what antenna work needs. Instead the first row to arrive wins\nand the second is silently dropped, and *which* radio wins is arbitrary (arrival order).\n\nThe table has no radio-attribution column at all — schema is\n`(id, ts, num, packet_id, rssi, snr, hops)`. So even the surviving row cannot say which antenna\nmeasured it.\n\n**Not yet known:** whether mesh-gw emits one event or two when both radios hear a packet\n(asked as xsession item [inbound-relay-attribution], unanswered). If it is one event carrying\nboth, this is a decode gap rather than a dedup gap.\n\n**Relation to current task:** overlaps fix 2 (per-radio signal storage). Recorded separately\nbecause it may outlive that fix — the dedup index would still need dropping/re-keying even after\n`nodes.rssi` is de-scalarised.'}
2026-07-26 13:48:34,455 70497 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'CONFIRMED: hopsField() treats a traceroute of unbounded age as authoritative "verified" hops', 'description': '**Status:** CONFIRMED · **Found during:** signal-provenance-mixed-source Phase 1 (2026-07-26)\n\n`src/node-status.js:53-71` — `hopsField()` prefers a stored traceroute\'s `route.length` over the\nlive reported value, with no age bound whatsoever. Verified always wins when present.\n\n**Evidence (live DB):** node 2364420971 (GARG) —\n\n    nodeinfo.last_traceroute = {"route":[], ..., "ts":1783965703876}\n\n`ts` is 2026-07-12, i.e. **~14 days old**. `route:[]` → length 0 → the panel renders **HOPS 0**,\nwhile the same node\'s live reported value in `nodes.hops` is **1**. This is the hops half of the\nmixed-source row Peter saw.\n\n**Mitigation already present:** the field is labelled `verified <stamp>` with the traceroute\'s\ntimestamp, so the age is not hidden. That is why this is recorded rather than treated as the\nprimary defect.\n\n**Why it is still a defect:** a two-week-old route is presented in the same field list as\nsecond-fresh signal data, and the value it produces (0) actively contradicts the live value (1).\nMesh topology is not stable over 14 days — [hops-away-semantics] already establishes that\nreported hops is unreliable, but "verified once, a fortnight ago" is not obviously better.\n\n**What is NOT known:** what the right bound is. Needs a decision, not a guess — a traceroute is\nexpensive and rate-limited, so expiring it aggressively means the field goes empty often.'}
