2026-07-26 14:59:57,392 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'D2 — S2 LEAD TIME set to 10 s (butler.wakeLeadMs), the LOW end of Peter\'s "10-15 seconds". Reasoning: a lead that is too long spends a transmission on a device still asleep, and a Meshtastic packet only lives ~31 s. 10 s covers the HTTP call to mesh-gw, the BLE write, ~2.2 s of SF11 airtime and 2 hops to the garage. CAVEAT PETER SHOULD KNOW: it is timed against a window NOBODY HAS MEASURED — mode.windowMs is 10000 with windowSource \'assumed\', and device-behaviour.md §7 lists the RX window as assumed. So this is an estimate against an estimate. It is configurable for exactly that reason, and S1 now provides the evidence to tune it (delivered vs unconfirmed per packet, against how early we sent). TO CHANGE: one value in host/host.config.json modules.mesh.butler.wakeLeadMs, no code change, no restart risk.'}
2026-07-26 15:00:06,894 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "D3 — S2 PRE-FIRES ONLY ON A DEVICE-REPORTED BEAT, not a measured one. THIS IS THE DECISION PETER IS MOST LIKELY TO DISAGREE WITH. A wrong wake prediction spends a transmission on a sleeping radio, and measured beats have been wrong in production within the last day: BNCH measured 335061 against a true 900000, GARG measured 1830694 — both before B2 fixed the seeding. A device-reported beat comes straight off a status/config reply, IS the setting, and cannot drift. So the butler gets an injected wakeTrusted(unit) that is true only when beatSource === 'device'; everything else falls back to today's reactive onHeard, which still works. CONSERVATIVE ON PURPOSE: it trades some of the benefit to guarantee the change never makes delivery worse than it is today. PETER MAY WANT IT LOOSER now that B2 makes measurement much harder to get wrong (two agreeing gaps to seed, three agreeing rejects to re-seed). TO CHANGE: one line — index.js's wakeTrusted predicate, allow beatSource 'measured' as well. NOTE the interaction with B18: the model is in-memory, so after a restart NO unit has a device-reported beat until it answers a status, and S2 stays dormant that whole time."}
2026-07-26 15:00:20,694 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'D4 — S2 uses ONE periodic interval (butler.wakeTickMs = 2000), not a setTimeout per unit. A per-unit timer is more precise but must be cancelled and rescheduled on every beat change, settle and cancel — three leak paths. One tick has one timer and survives beat changes for free. B1 was a live instance of getting timers wrong (setInterval(undefined) at ~920/s), and recorder.js:94 records the rule: leave NOTHING running. 2000 ms is 0.5 wakeups/s, negligible against the 39-tick baseline we just measured. TO CHANGE: host.config.json modules.mesh.butler.wakeTickMs.'}
2026-07-26 15:00:23,972 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'D5 — the Butler gains stop(), a lifecycle method it has NEVER had, and Mesh.close() now calls it. Required by D4: the butler previously had no stop/close at all, and Mesh.close() only closed the gateway, so an interval added there would have had nowhere to be cleared — exactly the trap recorder.js:94 warns about. host-module.js:272 already calls mesh.close(), so the service path is covered. This is a small API addition to a class other code constructs; a test that builds a bare Butler and never stops it is unaffected because the tick only starts when a wakeTick config value is present.'}
