2026-07-26 15:31:03,386 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': "Spec: docs/history/spec-hash-reader.md (attached). Commits 2370984 (services spec) + 6bbdd09 (mt-alarm, the tool).\n\nrecorded_hashes(fm) reads plain, colon and inline forms and returns (mapping, declared). An unreadable block is UNPARSEABLE with rc 2. A recorded key not in scope: is STRAY. --check-all prints coverage.\n\nMEASURED: 5/45 -> 51/53 specs read; drift 8 files/1 spec -> 167 files/40 specs; the five already-readable specs keep IDENTICAL verdicts (12/5/6/1/2). 167 vs my hand-counted 132 because eight specs written today have since had their files touched — checked, not assumed away, because the spec made a divergent number a finding in its own right.\n\nTHREE MORE BUGS CAME OUT OF FIXING THE ONE:\n1. MINE — `source_hash: ~` initially treated as unparseable. It is the explicit unset marker every new spec is born with; it flagged six proposed specs as corrupt, including the one being written at the time. Caught by the acceptance check.\n2. --check-all did `rc |= 1 if process(...) == 1 else 0`, discarding every rc 2 — the headline bug's exact shape, sitting one level above it.\n3. The single-spec path had the identical line.\n\nNOTHING RE-HASHED, deliberately. mt-transport's instruction and (2) is why it mattered: re-hashing in the same change would have written into a tool still swallowing its own errors.\n\nUNBLOCKS spec-drift-adjudication — 167 drifted files across 40 specs now need adjudicating, and mt-transport's 7/1 attribution is NOT usable (verified wrong from git, see B10 ledger entry). B17 stands: the device baseline is unmeasured, not zero."}
