2026-07-26 17:59:59,739 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "*** F1 IS WRONG — RETRACTED. I reported that GARG 'received our ping and did not answer it', put it in ../docs/roadmap/README.md as the answer to the project's top open question, told mt-transport, and used it to eliminate the downlink as a suspect. IT RESTS ON A PARTIAL READ OF ONE LOG LINE. Full sequence for packet 2363693060: 14:37:31 send_text_message sent; 14:37:31 queued; 14:37:54 routing_ack FAILED error=MAX_RETRANSMIT (the mesh gave up); 14:52:40 routing_ack DELIVERED (unknown id) from=!8cee336b — 14m46s later, ONE WAKE CYCLE. So the mesh declared failure, and GARG's ACKNOWLEDGEMENT arrived a full cycle late. That is the stranded-TX-queue signature applied to the ACK, not evidence that GARG stayed silent after receiving. I saw only the 'delivered' line because I grepped for delivered. THE ROADMAP AND device-behaviour.md NOW CARRY A CLAIM I CANNOT SUPPORT (commit 4292272) and must be corrected. Also told mt-transport (msg #27 on mt-transport--services). METHOD FAILURE: I grepped for the result I expected instead of reading the packet's whole history, then built a finding, a doc change and a cross-session announcement on it."}
2026-07-26 18:00:09,135 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "B24 CONFIRMED/OPEN — mesh-gw does not recognise 72% of the routing acks it receives. MEASURED today: 815 of 1139 routing_ack log lines carry '(unknown id)'. mesh-gw emits that when an ack arrives for a packet whose tracking it has already discarded. Two consequences, neither investigated: (a) a large fraction of delivery evidence arrives too late for mesh-gw to attribute, so whether it still reaches us as a message_status event with the right packet_id is UNKNOWN and must be established, not assumed; (b) if '(unknown id)' acks are NOT emitted as message_status, then our ackState is populated only by the subset mesh-gw still recognises, and every conclusion drawn from ackState today is drawn from a biased sample. The 15-minute delay on GARG's acks (see the F1 retraction) is very likely why so many are unknown: mesh-gw's tracking window is shorter than a wake cycle. THIS IS THE SINGLE MOST IMPORTANT UNANSWERED QUESTION about our delivery data and I noticed the '(unknown id)' string hours ago and did not chase it."}
2026-07-26 18:00:22,628 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "B25 CONFIRMED/OPEN — a packet can receive MORE THAN ONE terminal status, and our idempotency rule silently discards all but the first. MEASURED: 3 packets today carry two terminal statuses in mesh-gw's log. Packet 2363693060 got 'failed error=MAX_RETRANSMIT' at 14:37:54 and 'delivered' at 14:52:40. My noteAck (butler.js) is deliberately idempotent — 'one terminal per packet, later ones ignored' — so the FIRST wins and the second is dropped with no record. That is wrong on this evidence: a later 'delivered' after an earlier 'failed' is not noise, it is the true outcome arriving after the mesh gave up waiting, and it is exactly the fact worth keeping on a link this slow. WORSE, THERE IS AN INCONSISTENCY I CANNOT YET EXPLAIN: our ledger records acks[9]='delivered' for that packet, but 'failed' was emitted 15 minutes EARLIER, so idempotency should have kept 'failed'. Either the failed status never reached us as a message_status event, or the ordering assumption is wrong. pac-host has restarted 10 times today (current instance started 15:37:34) so the 14:37 events were handled by an earlier instance. UNRESOLVED — do not trust ackState until this is explained. My S1 spec asserted 'TERMINAL, exactly one per packet' as mesh-gw's contract; that is now contradicted by measurement."}
2026-07-26 18:00:35,174 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "B26 UNVERIFIED/OPEN — three things I noticed in passing today and explained away without checking. Recording them as UNVERIFIED, because each was stated in chat as though understood. (a) 'butler: message_status <status> for packet N matches no request' appears repeatedly in the pac-host log. I asserted it was dev-mode BNCH traffic whose packet ids are never recorded via noteSent — PLAUSIBLE, NEVER MEASURED. If it is instead our own butler traffic failing to match, ackState is broken for those packets and the warning is the symptom. Count it, attribute it. (b) The recorder holds 62 rows from our gateway with an EMPTY port field (from_id=2687afb1, to_id=ffffffff, port=''). Never looked at. An unparsed portnum could be anything, including our own suppressed traffic leaking through under a different shape. (c) mesh-gw's GET /{gwId}/messages returned only 7 messages while the recorder holds 19,870 rows for the same day. I noted the discrepancy and moved on. Peter says mesh-gw is a bridge with no database, which would make it a small live buffer — but I have not established what it actually is, and I cited it as an authority twice."}
2026-07-26 18:00:46,369 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "B27 CONFIRMED/OPEN — the ledger's reply_id column is NULL even on a settled row, and we record no signal quality per reply. Measured on ms1v060j.1: state=done, result present, reply_id=None. To answer 'which packet did this reply answer' I had to query mesh-gw, which DOES carry reply_id (its store showed reply_id=33971216 for GARG's pong). That question is the ONLY way to tell an immediate reply from one stranded a wake cycle — the exact discriminator for the TX-queue theory — and our own ledger cannot answer it. reply-correlation added the reply_id column and settleByReplyId matches on it; something is not persisting it. SEPARATELY: mesh-gw's message record carries rx_snr/rx_rssi per reply and we keep none of it. The 17:52 pong arrived at rx_snr -19.5, BELOW the ~-17.5 SF11 demodulation floor, while its payload reported GARG hearing US at snr -7.2. That asymmetry — good downlink, marginal uplink — is measurable from data we already receive and discard, and it is a better candidate explanation for GARG's behaviour than anything I asserted today. Both gaps make our own ledger unable to settle questions the far side can."}
