2026-07-26 19:01:39,602 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B28 CONFIRMED — the pre-wake send (S2 wake-timed-send) has NEVER fired for any unit. Two independent gates, and every unit fails one of them.\n\n  mesh/index.js:145  wakeTrusted: (unit) => n.beatSource === \'device\' && n.beat\n  mesh/index.js:117  dispatch(): mode===\'dev\' bypasses the butler entirely\n\n  GARG !8cee336b  mode=live  -> reaches the butler, but beatSource=measured -> wakeTrusted FALSE -> never pre-fires\n  BNCH !987ab80f  mode=dev   -> direct send, never queued, never sees the butler or the wake tick\n\nEvidence: `pm2 logs pac-host --lines 4000 | grep -c "pre-wake"` = 0. Log string verified present at\nbutler.js:115,265,269 before grepping, so the zero is a real absence and not a wrong needle.\n\nTHE GATE IS INVERTED. It admits the unit that does not need it (BNCH: 0 hops, mains, awake, answers\nin ~3 s) and excludes the only one it was written for (GARG: 1 hop, asleep 99%, 8 s window).\n\nThe stated justification at index.js:139-142 — "a measured beat has been wrong in production\n(BNCH 335061, GARG 1830694)" — is stale. Both figures predate B2 (beat-lock), which is what fixed\nthe seeding. Post-B2 GARG measures 899136 against a true 900000, stdev 3.0 s over 48 gaps. I never\nre-tested the premise after fixing the thing that caused it.\n\nREPORTING FAILURE, recorded as part of the bug: S2 was reported as shipped on the strength of the\ncode being present. Its acceptance table never asked "does this path execute for a real unit", so\nthe answer "no, for either of them" went unnoticed until Peter asked. Same class as F1 — presence\nmistaken for reachability. Any future acceptance criterion for a conditional path must name the\nunit it fires for and show a log line proving it did.\n\nPeter\'s instruction 2026-07-26: "at this stage nothing should be gated to any device."\n\nFix: remove the wakeTrusted condition. SEPARATE DECISION, not folded in: BNCH\'s mode=dev bypass\n(host.config.json units) is the second gate and changing it alters bench behaviour — raise, do not\nsilently fix.'}
2026-07-26 19:02:39,238 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'wake-gate-removal', 'title': 'Remove the wakeTrusted gate so the pre-wake send actually fires — B28: it has never fired for any unit', 'steps': ['Investigate: trace every gate on the pre-wake path and prove which one blocks GARG', 'Document: write and attach the spec', 'Implement: remove the wakeTrusted condition', 'Observe: prove a real pre-wake fire at GARG, and prove the check fails with the fix reverted', 'Track: hash, validate, commit']}
2026-07-26 19:06:50,193 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_file_attach args={'file_path': 'projects/mt-alarm/services/docs/history/wake-gate-removal.md', 'kind': 'plan', 'label': 'wake-gate-removal spec — both gates, one rule'}
2026-07-26 19:15:46,908 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B30 CONFIRMED — unitMode() resolves UNKNOWN to \'dev\', i.e. to AWAKE.\n\n  mesh/index.js:544-559  final fall-through is `return \'dev\'`\n\nReached whenever there is no model entry or no lastHeardMs — every unit, after every pac-host\nrestart. Measured 19:13:23 today, immediately after a restart:\n\n  !8cee336b  mode=dev  lastHeardMs=null  slp=null  beat=null  nextWake=null\n  !987ab80f  mode=dev  lastHeardMs=null  slp=null  beat=null  nextWake=null\n\nGARG classified AWAKE while asleep. A command issued in that state is sent DIRECTLY at a\nsleeping radio instead of being queued — the message is simply lost, and no ledger row records\nthat it was ever attempted.\n\nThis is the exact error index.js:514-519 already warns about for _awake ("at all => null, meaning\nUNKNOWN — not false"), committed in the opposite direction one function further down. _awake is\ncareful to say "I do not know"; unitMode turns "I do not know" into "it is listening".\n\nMASKED UNTIL NOW by the config pin "!8cee336b": {"mode":"live"}, which forced GARG live\nunconditionally. Removing the pins (wake-gate-removal) exposed it. The pin was load-bearing and I\ndid not anticipate that.\n\nFIX: unknown must resolve to \'live\', not \'dev\'. There is no cost — butler already treats an\nunknown unit correctly ("UNKNOWN IS NOT ASLEEP: a unit we have never heard still gets its\nimmediate try", butler.js:255), so a wrongly-live unit still transmits at once, and additionally\ngains the ledger, the retry and the ack. A wrongly-dev unit loses the message outright.\n\n---\n\nB31 CONFIRMED — the measured wake model is not persisted; every restart discards it.\n\n  mesh/payloads/mesh.db tables: cache, meta, requests   <- no node/model table\n  mesh/lib/model.js is a no-I/O module by design (header, line 1-5)\n\nThe butler QUEUE survives a restart. The MEASUREMENTS that tell it when to deliver do not:\nbeat, beatSource, lastHeardMs, slp are all in-memory only.\n\nCost per restart, from model.js seeding (two agreeing gaps to seed, RESEED_AFTER=3 to overturn):\n  - lastHeard/mode: blind until the unit\'s next transmission        ~15 min for GARG\n  - beat/nextWake:  null until THREE transmissions give two gaps    ~30 min\nSo the wake scheduler is unusable for roughly half an hour after every restart, and pre-wake\ndelivery cannot fire at all in that window even once B30 is fixed.\n\nPeter, 2026-07-26: "so there\'s a bug, that should be persistent, i.e in the sqlite db"\n\nFIX DIRECTION: persist the per-node model to mesh.db and reload on boot. Needs a migration (6),\na load on construct, a write on change, and a decision on staleness — a beat measured days ago\nagainst a device that has since been reflashed is not evidence, so persisted rows need an age\nand a rule for discarding them. NOT a quick change; own task.'}
2026-07-26 19:56:02,849 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B32 CONFIRMED — "always listening" is not representable. A unit that never sleeps is treated as\nasleep ~99% of the time, and its commands are held for a wake window that does not exist.\n\nPeter, 2026-07-26, on being the one to spot it: "if the device never sleeps, it will never receive\nany commands. next wake should be -1 shouldnt it?" — correct, and the display is the smaller half.\n\nROOT: mesh/index.js:520-527  _awake() never consults slp.\n\n  _awake(id) {\n    const n = this.model.node(id);\n    if (!n || !n.lastHeardMs) return null;\n    return Date.now() - n.lastHeardMs <= this.cfg.mode.windowMs;   // <- lastHeard ONLY\n  }\n\nBNCH reports slp:0 ("always listening", six status replies today). It beacons every ~899 s and\nwindowMs is 10 s, so _awake returns FALSE for ~890 of every 899 s on a unit that is listening the\nwhole time. Nothing downstream reads slp — unitMode is the only consumer of it in the codebase.\n\nCONSEQUENCE: butler.js:264-280 branches on `awake === false` and takes the HELD path, setting\nnextTryAt = nextWake(unit) — a wake time for a device that has no wake. The command is then freed\nonly when the unit happens to TRANSMIT. Measured: Peter\'s ping queued 19:41:29, held with\nnextTryAt null, released 19:53:09 by the beacon. 12 minutes, on a mains unit that was listening\ncontinuously the entire time. THE ALWAYS-AVAILABLE DEVICE GETS THE WORST SERVICE.\n\nFOUR SITES, one class — UNKNOWN and KNOWN-NEGATIVE sharing a value:\n  index.js:520  _awake      null = never heard;  false = asleep AND = always-listening-but-quiet\n  index.js:534  _nextWake   null = beat unknown  AND = no wake needed\n  butler.js:264 enqueue     only `awake === false` branches; no always-listening case\n  index.js:548  unitMode    reads slp — the ONLY place that does\n\nSame class as B30 (unknown resolved to awake). After fixing B30 I did not sweep for the class,\nwhich is why Peter found this one rather than me.\n\nFIX DIRECTION: _awake returns TRUE unconditionally when slp === 0 — the device\'s own statement\noutranks inference from silence. butler gains an always-listening path that delivers immediately\nand never sets nextTryAt. nextWake stops meaning two things: keep it null and add an explicit\nwakeSource: "always-listening" | "measured" | "device" | "unknown". NOT -1 in a ms-epoch field —\nnextWake - now would silently compute a plausible number instead of failing loudly; raised with\nPeter, his call.\n\nCONTRACT IMPACT: nextWake/awake/wakeSource are in docs/api/http.md and node-dash renders them.\nNot binding until node-dash acks.\n\n---\n\nCORRECTION to my own earlier evidence, recorded so it is not reused: I reported that BNCH\nanswered a command "900 s from the nearest beacon, tries=1", offered as proof it never sleeps.\nThat gap was computed against TELEMETRY_APP beacons only, but lastHeardMs updates on ANY packet\nfrom the node including its own command replies. The number does not show what I said it did.\nB32 rests on index.js:520 reading no slp, which is code, not that measurement.'}
2026-07-26 20:09:34,869 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': "B33 CONFIRMED — nextWake anchors on the LAST frame of a wake burst, so every prediction is late\nby the burst span. This is why the pre-wake send still does not fire after the gate was removed.\n\n  mesh/index.js:534  _nextWake()  ->  t = n.lastHeardMs + n.beat\n\nA wake emits a BURST, not one frame: 2-4 telemetry frames, plus any command reply. lastHeardMs is\nthe last of them; the beat runs from the FIRST.\n\nMEASURED 2026-07-26, GARG, from the recorder CSV:\n  burst 19:52:57 -> 19:53:11   span 14 s   3 frames (TELE, TEXT)\n  predicted nextWake = lastHeard 19:53:11 + beat 899711 = 20:08:11   (API reported 20:08:10)\n  actual wake        = burst start 19:52:57 + 899711    = 20:07:57   (observed: butler logged\n                       'window open - delivering status (try 2)' at 20:07:57)\n  => prediction 13 s LATE, and the reactive onHeard path won the race.\n\nBurst span across 57 bursts: mean 5.0 s, max 59 s. wakeLeadMs is 10 s, so the pre-wake band\n(nextWake -10 s) routinely opens AFTER the unit has already woken. The tick never gets to fire.\n\nPERVERSE: the 14 s burst was long BECAUSE it contained a TEXT frame — GARG's own reply to our\ncommand. Sending a command lengthens the burst and makes the NEXT prediction worse.\n\nNot jitter, not tuning. A2/A3 of wake-gate-removal cannot pass while this stands.\n\nFIX: anchor on burst START. In model.heard(), a frame arriving after a long silence opens a new\nwake and sets wakeAnchor; frames within a few seconds are continuations and must NOT move it.\n_nextWake becomes wakeAnchor + beat. lastHeardMs keeps its meaning for _awake(), which correctly\nwants the most recent frame. Threshold should derive from an existing declared value (mode.windowMs\nor minBeatMs), not a new inline constant.\n\nSUPERSEDES my own earlier advice, recorded so it is not acted on: I told Peter wakeLeadMs was\nundersized against p95 prediction error of 11 s and suggested ~15 s. That analysis used\nbeacon-to-beacon GAPS, which are anchor-independent and so blind to this. Raising the lead would\nhave masked a systematic error with a wider margin and made it harder to find. Do NOT tune\nwakeLeadMs until B33 is fixed and the anchor is correct.\n\nBeat measurement itself is NOT affected: consecutive lastHeard values differ by beat plus the\nchange in span, which averages out. GARG measures 899711 against a configured 900000, consistent\nwith BNCH. Only the ANCHOR is wrong."}
