2026-07-26 20:29:23,665 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': "B36 CONFIRMED — node ids are built without zero-padding, so any node whose num < 0x10000000 gets a\nNON-CANONICAL id, one hex digit short.\n\nFIVE sites, none padded:\n  mesh/lib/gw.js:109    '!' + (e.from_num >>> 0).toString(16)\n  mesh/lib/gw.js:168    '!' + (pkt.from   >>> 0).toString(16)     <- the 'heard' event\n  mesh/index.js:432     '!' + (num >>> 0).toString(16)\n  mesh/index.js:549     '!' + (num >>> 0).toString(16)\n  mesh/index.js:765     '!' + (num >>> 0).toString(16)\n\nCONTRAST, and it is the proof this is an oversight rather than a convention:\n  mesh/lib/align.js:32  (Number(num) >>> 0).toString(16).padStart(8, '0').slice(-4)   <- correct\n\nMEASURED 2026-07-26 20:28, from node_state after the persistence work landed:\n  our model     !4c588a4     (7 hex digits)\n  recorder CSV   04c588a4    (8, canonical)\nSame physical node, two spellings.\n\nIMPACT: our two units are unaffected — 8cee336b and 987ab80f both start with a high nibble — so\nthis is not deployment-blocking. But any node with a leading-zero num is stored and matched under\na form no other system uses. If that node ever reaches us via a path that DOES pad, we hold two\nmodel entries and two node_state rows for one device, and a lookup by the canonical id misses.\nRoughly 1 node in 16 has a leading zero nibble.\n\nFOUND BY: the node_state table, which is the first thing in this service to write node ids out\nwhere they can be read side by side with another system's. The bug predates it.\n\nFIX: one helper, used by all five, padStart(8,'0'). Trivial. NOT done in wake-state-persistence —\ngw.js is outside that spec's scope and an id-format change touches matching everywhere, so it\nwants its own task and its own regression pass over resolve/roster/queue lookups."}
