2026-07-27 09:36:05,834 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "B44 CONFIRMED/OPEN — the wake predictor uses the single previous gap as the beat, so err_ms is the FIRST DIFFERENCE of the gap and our prediction error is ~2x the device's actual jitter", 'description': '**Where:** `mesh/lib/model.js:133` — `beat = gap;`\n\n**Evidence (measured, unit !987ab80f, 5-minute beat, fw 2-260727-90):**\n\n```\ngap:  300014  300017  299977  300059  299971\nerr:      —      +3     -40     +82     -88\n```\n\n`err_ms` equals `gap_n - gap_(n-1)` on every row, exactly, three times over (-40, +82,\n-88). It is not a prediction error against a stable beat; it is the first difference of a\nnoisy series.\n\n```\nsd(gap deviation from 300000)   31.8 ms\nsd(first difference)            62.5 ms     ratio 1.97\nmean |err| as shipped           53 ms\nmean |err| vs a stable beat     28 ms\n```\n\n**Cause, and it is mine.** Fixing B38/B41 I replaced the damped EMA\n(`beat * 0.75 + gap * 0.25`) with `beat = gap`. The EMA was genuinely wrong — it lagged a\nreal beat change by ~1.5 s (measured: gap 901160, beat in use 899634). But a single sample\npredicting another single sample adds the two noises in quadrature. I fixed lag by\nimporting variance and did not notice, because both estimators stay inside the 100 ms\ntarget and nothing was measuring the difference.\n\n**Why the fix is not a revert.** `agrees()` and the `beatRejects` machinery already detect\na genuine beat change and relearn through a separate path. So a gap that AGREES can safely\nbe averaged over a window without reintroducing the lag B41 removed — the two concerns were\nconflated into one assignment.\n\n**NOT yet known:** the magnitude of err has grown on every sample so far (3, 40, 82, 88),\nn=4. Peter predicted continued growth; the sign flipped at -88, which rules out simple\naccumulation, but the growth in magnitude is recorded as unresolved rather than dismissed.\nNeeds >=10 consecutive gaps.\n\n**Separately, not this bug:** `nextWake` predicts the ANCHOR (first frame arriving at the\ngateway), not the wake instant. The ~2.1 s offset between them rests on ONE observation and\nis currently unmeasurable — the CH34x tap (1a86:55d4) is not passed through to the VM.\n\n**Tracked by:** task `send-receive-timing` (764), steps 2, 3 and 6. Acceptance is\nmean |err| over >=10 comparable wakes from `wake_log`, against the 100 ms target.\n\n**Reported late.** This was first written into a step title of task 764 rather than\nappended here. Peter caught it. A finding recorded only in a task step is not reported.'}
