2026-07-27 10:10:59,315 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'Observe and TUNE the awake time. Measure how long the device is actually awake per wake, and what it spends that time on, then tune the window — awake time is the battery cost of every wake and it is currently a fixed constant.', 'description': '**Peter asked for this first and it was missing from the original task. Added late.**\n\n**Observe.** Awake duration per wake, measured, not assumed. Known so far from earlier\ncaptures: **8.8-9.9 s idle, 9.9-12.2 s when handling commands** — but those came from a\nhandful of observations, not a distribution. Wanted: a per-wake series long enough to state\na mean and a spread, and a breakdown of where the time goes (beacon TX, RX window, drain,\nsleep entry).\n\nDevice-side constants for reference: `SLEEP_WINDOW_MS` 8000, `TX_DRAIN_MS` 6000. The device\nsleeps via `sleepNow(wokeAt)`, which sleeps to `wokeAt + heartbeatMs` rather than a constant,\nso a longer wake eats into the following sleep rather than shifting the schedule.\n\n**Tune.** Awake time is the battery cost of every single wake, and a multi-day deployment\npays it ~288 times a day at a 300 s beat. The RX window is a fixed constant chosen without\nmeasurement. Questions to answer with numbers:\n- how much of the 8 s window is ever actually used?\n- what is the latest point in a window that a command has ever arrived (feeds from step 5)?\n- how much drain time is genuinely needed vs the 6 s allowed?\n- what would shortening the window cost in delivery probability?\n\n**Constraint: any change to the window itself is a FLASH and is device-session territory.**\nThis step produces the measurement and the recommendation. The service side owns none of the\nconstants — so the deliverable here is evidence handed to the firmware session, not an edit.\n\n**Acceptance:** a mean and spread of awake duration over >=10 wakes, plus a stated\nrecommendation for the window with the number that justifies it. Not an adjective.\n\n**Depends on** device-side serial visibility, same blocker as steps 4 and 5.'}
2026-07-27 10:11:12,428 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'CORRECTION to steps 4, 5 and 7 — the blocker names the wrong device.\n\nSteps 4 and 5 say the blocker is "the CH34x tap (1a86:55d4) passed through to the VM".\nThat is wrong and must not be relied on.\n\nThe debug adapter in use is Peter\'s **M5 serial debug and flasher device — a USB -> Serial\ndebugger and programmer**, enumerating as `303a:1001 Espressif USB JTAG/serial debug unit`,\nby-id `usb-Espressif_USB_JTAG_serial_debug_unit_64:E8:33:86:1F:74-if00`. He has used it for\nabout a month. It IS passed through and IS present. The Espressif VID/PID and product string\ncome from the ESP32 inside the adapter and say nothing about what it can debug.\n\nSo steps 4, 5 and 7 are NOT blocked on a missing device. They are blocked on the adapter\nbeing silent, cause unknown.\n\nWhat is measured, and nothing beyond it:\n- 0 bytes at 9600 across three wake windows (09:23:54, 09:28:54, 09:33:53)\n- 0 bytes at 115200 across a confirmed wake (10:08:39)\n- the baud had NEVER been set: uartlog.py opens the port with open(PORT,\'rb\') and\n  deliberately never touches termios, a rule written for the RAK\'s own CDC and wrongly\n  carried over to this adapter. An unset CDC-ACM port sits at the 9600 default, and line\n  settings do not survive re-enumeration.\n- so baud is now RULED OUT as the cause; the silence is unexplained.\n\nSeparately confirmed and relevant to what the tap would show: `g_sleepDbg` is\n`static bool = false` (main.cpp:129) with no persistence found, so the power cycle during the\nUSB rearrangement turned sleep debugging OFF. `sleepdbg on` re-queued as ms30a5lq.0.\n\nDo not attach a cause to the silence without testing it. An earlier claim here that the\nadapter "has no UART receive pins to tap with" was invented to explain a measurement and was\nfalse.'}
2026-07-27 10:18:17,636 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'serial-aliases', 'title': 'Name the three UARTs and ban raw Serial/Serial1/Serial2 from the firmware — the serial mix-ups cost time several times a day, every day', 'steps': ["Investigate: enumerate all 47 raw call sites in device/src/main.cpp (Serial 11, Serial1 29, Serial2 7) and confirm each one's intended port. Confirm transport/src/generated/*.pb.h matches are protobuf SerialConfig fields, NOT Arduino ports, and are out of scope.", 'Document: spec in device/docs/history/serial-aliases.md. Three defines - SERIAL_USB=Serial (board CDC 239a:8029), SERIAL_CAM=Serial1 (camera, RX15/TX16), SERIAL_DBG=Serial2 (debug tap, RX8/TX17). State the before/after for every call site.', 'Implement: replace all 47 sites. Pure rename, no behaviour change. dbgOut writes to SERIAL_USB and SERIAL_DBG exactly as it writes to Serial and Serial2 today.', 'Observe: grep must show ZERO raw \\bSerial[12]?\\b in src/main.cpp outside the three define lines. Build must succeed. Diff must be rename-only - no logic lines changed.', 'Track: hash the spec, commit. NOTE THIS IS A FLASH CHANGE and takes effect only on the next upload; a deployment is imminent and the flash freezes once the unit is out.', "ALSO FIX THE COMMENTS I GOT WRONG TODAY: platformio.ini and main.cpp both say the debug tap is 'the CH34x tap (/dev/rak-debug)'. The adapter actually in use is Peter's M5 USB->Serial debugger/programmer (303a:1001, by-id usb-Espressif_USB_JTAG_serial_debug_unit_64:E8:33:86:1F:74-if00). The CH34x 1a86:55d4 is not present. platformio.ini names the M5 by-id path directly under a comment calling it the CH34x."]}
