2026-07-27 10:33:49,286 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': "B45 CONFIRMED/OPEN — agrees() accepts a reboot's partial gap as a new beat: a 251340 ms gap overwrote a 300000 ms beat, corrupting every subsequent wake prediction", 'description': "**Where:** `mesh/lib/model.js` — the `agrees(gap, beat)` tolerance path, same region as\n[B44].\n\n**Measured, 2026-07-27, unit !987ab80f:**\n\nA reflash rebooted the device mid-cycle. The first post-reboot beacon arrived 251340 ms after\nthe previous one — a PARTIAL cycle, because the reboot restarted the device's wake clock\npart-way through.\n\n```\nbefore   beat = 300068   (correct, device configured for 300 s)\nevent    gap  = 251340   err = -48728 ms\nafter    beat = 251340   <-- ADOPTED\n```\n\nThe model now believes the device wakes every 251 s. It does not. Every prediction is wrong\nuntil enough clean gaps relearn it.\n\n**Why it slipped through.** The same machinery correctly REJECTED a 1484948 ms hole earlier the\nsame morning (the unpowered stretch during the USB rearrangement) and logged\n`long-gap:1484948ms vs beat 300021 — missed wakes or reboot?`. So rejection works for a gap\nthat is a large MULTIPLE of the beat. It fails for a gap that is a FRACTION of it: 251340/300068\n= 0.84, close enough to pass tolerance while being badly wrong.\n\nA missed beat produces a near-multiple. **A reboot produces a near-fraction, and nothing looks\nfor that.**\n\n**Interaction with B44:** because `beat = gap` (model.js:133) adopts a single sample outright,\none bad gap is enough. With a windowed estimator a lone 251 s sample would be outvoted. The two\ndefects compound — B45 is how the bad sample gets in, B44 is why one sample is sufficient to do\ndamage.\n\n**Related and already known:** B34 — no reboot detection at all, because `gw.js` drops\n`device_metrics.uptime_seconds`. A reboot is exactly the discontinuity that should invalidate\nthe anchor and the beat, and we cannot currently see one. Fixing B34 would give B45 a real\nsignal instead of a tolerance heuristic.\n\n**NOT yet known:** whether the earlier post-reboot outliers this morning (+542, -1134, +1449,\n-1768, +958 ms) share this cause. They began after the power cycle, and the growth pattern was\nattributed to B44's first-differencing. That attribution is now less certain and should be\nre-derived once B44 is fixed, not assumed.\n\n**Service-side. No flash needed.**"}
