2026-07-27 10:34:02,852 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "B45 CONFIRMED/OPEN — agrees() accepts a reboot's partial gap as a new beat: a 251340 ms gap overwrote a 300068 ms beat, corrupting every subsequent wake prediction", 'description': "**Where:** `mesh/lib/model.js` — the `agrees(gap, beat)` tolerance path, same region as B44.\n\n**Measured, 2026-07-27, unit !987ab80f:**\n\nA reflash rebooted the device mid-cycle. The first post-reboot beacon arrived 251340 ms after\nthe previous one — a PARTIAL cycle, because the reboot restarted the device's wake clock\npart-way through.\n\n```\nbefore   beat = 300068   (correct, device configured for 300 s)\nevent    gap  = 251340   err = -48728 ms\nafter    beat = 251340   <-- ADOPTED\n```\n\nThe model now believes the device wakes every 251 s. It does not. Every prediction is wrong\nuntil enough clean gaps relearn it.\n\n**Why it slipped through.** The same machinery correctly REJECTED a 1484948 ms hole earlier the\nsame morning (the unpowered stretch during the USB rearrangement) and logged\n`long-gap:1484948ms vs beat 300021 — missed wakes or reboot?`. So rejection works for a gap that\nis a large MULTIPLE of the beat. It fails for a gap that is a FRACTION of it:\n251340/300068 = 0.84, close enough to pass tolerance while being badly wrong.\n\nA missed beat produces a near-multiple. **A reboot produces a near-fraction, and nothing looks\nfor that.**\n\n**Interaction with B44:** because `beat = gap` (model.js:133) adopts a single sample outright,\none bad gap is enough. With a windowed estimator a lone 251 s sample would be outvoted. The two\ndefects compound — B45 is how the bad sample gets in, B44 is why one sample suffices to do\ndamage.\n\n**Related and already known:** B34 — no reboot detection at all, because `gw.js` drops\n`device_metrics.uptime_seconds`. A reboot is exactly the discontinuity that should invalidate\nthe anchor and the beat, and we currently cannot see one. Fixing B34 would give B45 a real\nsignal instead of a tolerance heuristic.\n\n**NOT yet known:** whether the earlier post-reboot outliers this morning (+542, -1134, +1449,\n-1768, +958 ms) share this cause. They began after the power cycle, and the growth pattern was\nattributed to B44's first-differencing. That attribution is now less certain and must be\nre-derived once B44 is fixed, not assumed.\n\n**Service-side. No flash needed.**"}
2026-07-27 10:39:10,772 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "B46 CONFIRMED/OPEN — the sleepmode reply reports `on`, we only read `slp`, so a confirmed 'sleep off' never reaches the model and the unit is treated as sleeping forever", 'description': '**Where:** `mesh/index.js:310`\n\n```js\nif (reply.slp != null) { this.model.sleep(id, reply.slp); learned = true; }\n```\n\n**The reply, measured verbatim (request ms317rwh.2, settled 10:36:57 on 2026-07-27):**\n\n```json\n{"type": "sleep", "on": 0, "ok": true, "note": "always listening"}\n```\n\nThere is no `slp` key. `reply.slp` is `undefined`, the branch never fires, and\n`model.sleep()` is never called. The device told us it is always listening and we discarded\nthe statement.\n\n**Observed effect.** After TWO confirmed `sleepmode off` commands (ms30wjeg.1 settled 10:28:43,\nms317rwh.2 settled 10:36:57), `/v1/mesh/mode/!987ab80f` still reported `mode=live, slp=1,\nawake=false`.\n\n**Why it matters more than a wrong field:**\n\n1. `dispatch` queues for a wake window instead of sending immediately (index.js:599 reads\n   `slp === 0`), so every command to a unit we have explicitly woken is delayed a whole cycle.\n2. `_nextWake` returns a schedule for a device that is not sleeping (index.js:635).\n3. `wakeSource` reports `measured` instead of `always-listening` (index.js:653).\n4. **Worst:** `wake_log` records a "wake" whenever a frame ARRIVES. An always-listening device\n   still sends telemetry every beat, so we cannot distinguish "woke and beaconed" from "was\n   already awake and sent scheduled telemetry". The entire beat series — and B44\'s statistics\n   built on it — may be describing telemetry period, not a sleep cycle.\n\n**This is the same defect as B43** (the `interval` reply states the new beat as `secs` and we\nignored it). Both are a reply whose field name we did not read. Third instance of the project\'s\nsignature failure: a value that arrives, is parsed, and is thrown away.\n\n**Fix direction:** read `on` as well as `slp` — and then AUDIT EVERY REPLY TYPE against what\n`_learnFromReply` actually consumes, rather than fixing this one field and waiting for the\nfourth instance. That audit is the real deliverable.\n\n**Consequence for open work:** B44 and B45 are both computed from `wake_log`. If point 4 holds,\ntheir numbers need re-deriving once this is fixed. Do not close either on the current data.\n\n**Service-side. No flash needed.**\n\nFound because Peter said "so we have conflicting args" and made me reconcile the ack against\nthe behaviour instead of trusting the ack.'}
2026-07-27 10:42:23,287 64308 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'reply-field-audit', 'title': 'B46 + B43 class: the device emits 29 reply types and we learn from 4 — fix the sleep reply, then audit every type against what _learnFromReply consumes', 'steps': ['Investigate: for each of the 29 reply types the firmware emits, list the fields it carries and which of them _learnFromReply reads. DONE IN PART: reads pong (align), interval.secs, reply.slp, fw. The sleep reply carries `on`, not `slp`, so it is dropped (B46).', "Fix B46: learn sleep state from the sleep reply's `on` field. Acceptance: after a confirmed `sleepmode off`, /v1/mesh/mode reports slp=0, mode=dev, wakeSource=always-listening — measured, not asserted.", 'Establish whether wake_log has been recording TELEMETRY PERIOD as wake gaps for an always-listening unit. If so, B44 and B45 statistics are invalid and must be re-derived, not carried forward.', 'Record every OTHER droppable field the audit finds as its own ledger entry. Do not fix them in this task - one change per measurement.']}
