2026-07-27 12:51:29,071 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'wake-preempt-performance', 'title': "Wake pre-empt: validate the whole loop against the device's <50ms period, and MEASURE how well it actually works", 'steps': ["Investigate and VALIDATE the current behaviour end to end, and state its actual performance as numbers: (a) prediction error — our predicted nextWake minus the observed first frame of the burst, distribution not a single sample; (b) how often the pre-wake send actually fires vs the reactive path winning; (c) whether the packet was still alive when the window opened, from ACK outcomes. Sources: wake_log, the request ledger's per-attempt rows, the debug module. No fix in this step.", 'Establish the device side of the contract rather than assuming it: the firmware sleeps beat measured FROM THE WAKE INSTANT (sleepNow targets wokeAt+heartbeatMs), claimed <50ms. Confirm what the current build actually does and what the residual per-cycle error is. Device is 2-260727-112 as of 2026-07-27.', 'wakeLeadMs is 10000, not the 15000 the design intends. Establish which is right from the measurement in step 1 — the reply lifetime is ~30 s, so the lead must be long enough to be airborne at window open and short enough that the message has not expired. Do not change it before the number exists.', "windowMs is ASSUMED, not measured (windowSource:'assumed'). Measure the real RX window from ACK evidence — the lead is currently an estimate timed against an estimate.", 'Only after the above: change ONE thing, re-measure, and attribute the difference. One change per measurement.']}
2026-07-27 12:51:37,542 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'THE DESIGN, as Peter stated it 2026-07-27.\n\nDEVICE (live mode): sleeps for `beat`. On waking it stamps the wake instant and subtracts the time it spent working, so the next sleep targets wokeAt+beat. The period is therefore relative to the LAST WAKE, not to the end of its tasks — a long wake does not push the next one out. Measured accuracy <50 ms.\n\nSERVICE: must know the same instant to within 50 ms, and pre-send any queued message wakeLeadMs BEFORE the predicted wake (design intent ~15 s, currently 10 s). The reason is the message lifetime: a Meshtastic message lives ~30 s and then ceases to exist, so transmitting early means it is still in the air, and still alive, at the moment the RX window opens. Sending after we have watched the window open wastes most of the window.\n\nTHE POINT OF THIS TASK: none of that is a measurement yet. State how well it actually works — prediction error distribution, how often the pre-wake path beats the reactive one, and whether the packet was alive at window open — before changing a single value.'}
2026-07-27 12:52:41,977 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 3, 'text': 'CORRECTION (Peter, 2026-07-27): 10 s is NOT a defect and not a shortfall against a 15 s intent. wakeLeadMs is a deliberately tunable variable and 10000 is a fine starting value — finding its right value from evidence is the POINT of this task, not a bug to fix. Read this step as "tune it against the measurement", not "restore it to 15000".'}
2026-07-27 12:59:12,603 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_file_attach args={'file_path': 'docs/history/wake-preempt-performance.md', 'kind': 'plan', 'label': 'Measured behaviour + 8 OPEN QUESTIONS that must not be glossed over'}
2026-07-27 13:09:14,119 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'FIRST: make BNCH emulate a live deployment — sleepmode on (persists across boot, VERIFIED) and interval 60 so a test cycle is ONE MINUTE, not fifteen. Nothing else in this task is testable until the bench unit sleeps.', 'description': 'Peter, 2026-07-27: a dev unit that never sleeps cannot exercise the pre-empt, and waiting 15 min per cycle wastes the day.\n\nVERIFIED POSSIBLE, both halves, no firmware change:\n- `sleepmode on` calls saveSettings() (device/src/main.cpp:2854) and slp is in PersistedSettings, reloaded at :1016 — so the unit BOOTS INTO THE SLEEP CYCLE rather than always-on. That is the emulation.\n- `interval` accepts 30-86400 s (:2222, clamp :1008). 60 s is legal. A 1-minute beat gives ~15 test cycles in the time one GARG cycle takes.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS HERE: 11 of the 12 recorded pre-wake firings were at BNCH, which never sleeps and so has no RX window to beat. The feature has been exercised almost entirely against the one unit that cannot benefit. A sleeping BNCH is the first test subject that is BOTH representative AND observable — it is on USB, so its own SLEEPNOW/HB lines are readable on the CDC while the service predicts the same instant (Q2).\n\nWATCH FOR: the wake window is ~8 s and wakeLeadMs is 10 s, so at a 60 s beat the lead is a sixth of the whole cycle. If that distorts the result, use 300 s instead and say which was used with every number.'}
2026-07-27 13:10:47,600 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B47/B48/B49/B50 — found 2026-07-27 while investigating the wake pre-empt and the CDC debug path. Recorded here so they are not lost; full context in docs/history/wake-preempt-performance.md (L1-L4) and serial-monitor-guard.md.\n\nB47 UNVERIFIED — wake_log records 82 DISTINCT UNITS, of which 2 are ours. Every foreign Meshtastic node on the mesh gets wake rows, with "errors" running to minutes. Any aggregate over the table is meaningless without a declared-devices join, and no consumer currently has one. Either filter at the write site or fix every read. (= Q4)\n\nB48 UNVERIFIED — wake_log records an ALWAYS-LISTENING unit as waking. BNCH has slp=0 and never sleeps, yet contributes 24 "clean" rows with median err 5.6 s; those are telemetry arrivals wearing the word "wake". This is the B44/B45 contamination that reply-field-audit predicted, now confirmed present in the table. (= Q5)\n\nB49 CONFIRMED — TWO CONFIGS, and the obvious one is inert. mesh/config.yaml:85-89 pins BOTH units to mode:dev. The running service never reads that file: pac-host loads host/host.config.json, whose modules.mesh.units is {}. The API proves the runtime ignores the pin — GARG reports mode:live. Editing config.yaml to change service behaviour does nothing, silently. Related: host.config.json\'s butler comment still says wakeLeadMs "ONLY applies when the beat is DEVICE-REPORTED", describing the wakeTrusted gate that mesh/index.js:145 says was removed. (= Q9)\n\nB50 CONFIRMED, COSMETIC BUT A TRAP — mesh/payloads/requests.db is a 0-byte file with no tables, dated 07-26 19:15. Every request lives in mesh.db.requests. A file named requests.db holding no requests will mislead the next person looking for the ledger. (= L3)\n\nALSO, not defects but constraints on every claim made from evidence today:\n- ACK COVERAGE IS ALMOST NIL for the only sleeping unit: 109 of GARG\'s 112 ledger rows have ack_state NULL. Q6 proposes measuring the RX window from ack evidence and that evidence does not yet exist. (L1)\n- ONE row already shows failed + ack_state=delivered — arrived, answer lost. The exact class S1 was built to expose and the signature of the undiagnosed 07-26 GARG silence. Watch for more. (L2)\n- pac-host-out.log begins 07-25 07:29 with 35 restarts and no rotation. I asserted "the pre-wake send has never fired" from a grep of it and was WRONG — it had fired 12 times under a different log phrase. Log-derived negatives mean "not in this file", never "never". (L4)'}
2026-07-27 13:23:12,567 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B51/B52/B53 — found 2026-07-27 on the FIRST bench run with a genuinely sleeping unit (BNCH, sleepmode on, interval 60). Both sides visible for the first time: the device\'s own SLEEPNOW/HB lines on the CDC, and our wake_log/debug feed. Full detail in docs/history/wake-preempt-performance.md R1-R9.\n\nB51 CONFIRMED, HIGH — A MEASURED BEAT OVERWROTE A DEVICE-STATED ONE, WITH A VALUE EXACTLY 2x WRONG.\nThe device stated beat=60000 in its interval reply; learn-beat-from-interval-reply captured it and set beatSource=\'device\'. mode.burstGapMs is 120000, so at a 60 s beat every second wake falls inside the previous wake\'s burst window and never re-anchors. Our measured gaps therefore came out ~120 s, and the model learned that as the schedule:\n  13:17:06 beat=60000  long-gap:120647ms   13:19:06 beat=60000 long-gap:120021ms   13:21:06 beat=120241 beat-changed:60000->120241\nService now reports beat=120241 beatSource=measured. Device serial for the same minutes: beat=60000ms, wakes 2210696 -> 2270696 -> 2330696, EXACTLY 60000 apart. nextWake is therefore correct only every other wake and the pre-wake band opens a full beat early half the time.\nAliasing at an exact FACTOR (2x) is the signature to catch. Open question recorded as Q13: may a measured beat ever overwrite a device-stated one, and should a factor-disagreement be treated as evidence our observation is aliased rather than that the device changed?\n\nB52 CONFIRMED — THE WAKE DETECTOR IS SILENTLY BLIND BELOW mode.burstGapMs (120 s).\nSix wakes occurred; wake_log recorded two. Not the device and not the link: the serial log shows two telemetry frames sent every wake, and the recorder CSV holds every one of them arriving via both radios at 12:13:05 / 12:14:05 / 12:15:05 / 12:16:05 / 12:17:06 UTC. The frames reached us and our own threshold discarded the observation. 120000 is not arbitrary (its comment reasons it sits above the longest burst seen, 59 s, and far below a 900 s beat) — but that reasoning inverts once the beat is shorter than the threshold, and the failure is SILENT. Measured burst span at this beat was ~1 s, nothing like 59 s. Q11 proposes making it a fraction of the beat, e.g. min(120000, beat/3), but the 59 s burst that motivated 120 s carried a command reply and whether it scales with the beat is unknown.\nCONSEQUENCE FOR TESTING: a 60 s test beat is below the floor of our own instrument. Test beats must exceed burstGapMs with margin (300 s works) until this is settled.\n\nB53 CONFIRMED, COSMETIC BUT MISLEADING — the long-gap note blames the device for our own threshold. It reads "missed wakes or reboot?" for wakes whose frames we demonstrably received and recorded elsewhere. A note that accuses the wrong component is worse than no note: it is what sent this investigation looking at the device first. (= Q12)\n\nMEASUREMENTS WORTH KEEPING, not defects:\n- THE DEVICE PERIOD IS EXACT. Wake instants on its own clock: 1790696, 1850696, 1910696, 1970696 - differences 60000, 60000, 60000 ms, zero error. Peter\'s <50 ms claim is confirmed and beaten. Every end-to-end error is ours.\n- THE CDC SURVIVES SLEEP. Four sleep cycles, dmesg USB event count 97 before and 97 after, no re-enumeration. The device only PRINTS while awake, but the port stays up. This contradicts CLAUDE.md\'s "a sleeping device services its USB CDC only inside its ~8 s window".\n- AWAKE TIME IS 11202 ms, stable. mode.windowMs is 10000 and its comment claims it is the firmware\'s SLEEP_WINDOW_MS (8000) plus margin. The real wake is longer than both. First hard number for Q6, and it came from the device stating it rather than from ACK evidence.\n- THE PRE-EMPT WORKS. ping queued 12:19:14Z, "window imminent in 8560 ms, sending EARLY", PRE-WAKE (early) delivering, ack_state=delivered, result {"type":"pong","upt":2280}. Transmitted 8.56 s before the predicted wake and mesh-gw confirmed delivery - the mechanism doing exactly what it was built for, on the kind of unit it was built for.\n- BUT THE REPLY IS THE SLOW LEG: same request took 3 tries and settled on a LATE reply 109522 ms (~2 beats) after it was asked. Tuning wakeLeadMs cannot touch the return path. Establish where the reply time goes before tuning the outbound lead.'}
2026-07-27 13:30:21,702 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B54 CONFIRMED — THE ACCURACY METRIC REPORTS HEALTH WHILE THE SCHEDULE IS 2x WRONG. Found 2026-07-27, same bench run as B51/B52. Detail: docs/history/wake-preempt-performance.md R10, question Q14.\n\nAfter the model adopted the aliased 120 s beat (B51), it began predicting every OTHER wake to within +-0.4 s:\n  13:26:05 gap=120031 err=-210ms     13:28:06 gap=120457 err=+426ms\n  /v1/debug/accuracy -> {"n":24,"medianMs":2666,...}\n\nerr_ms is prediction MINUS observation, and both come from us. A schedule that is exactly double the truth is perfectly self-consistent, so it scores BETTER than the correct schedule did. The instrument improves as the model becomes more wrong, and /v1/debug/accuracy would have shown a healthy unit while it was being polled at half its real rate.\n\nNothing in the instrument compares the schedule to any EXTERNAL truth. We now have one for the first time: the device states its beat in the `interval` reply (which learn-beat-from-interval-reply already captures, setting beatSource=\'device\') and prints beat= in every SLEEPNOW line on the CDC. A check as cheap as "is the measured beat a small-integer multiple or divisor of the stated beat" would have caught this within two wakes.\n\nSEVERITY, stated honestly rather than overdrawn: aliasing at exactly 2x still lands on a REAL wake whenever it fires, so commands are still delivered. What is lost is half the delivery opportunities, and all confidence that a good accuracy number means a good schedule. The second loss is the serious one — it is the number a future session would trust.'}
2026-07-27 13:49:52,921 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'Track: three DEVICE-side requirements raised with the device session (REQ1 window remotely configurable, REQ2 close window after N messages, REQ3 log the wake life cycle). Flash-gated — may not land before deployment. Do not block on them.', 'description': 'Peter, 2026-07-27, stated while we measured the wake pre-empt with a sleeping bench unit visible on the CDC. Raised as [wake-cycle-observability] on device--services, with a correction reply (#6) restoring a word my shell ate.\n\nREQ1 — the listen window must be remotely configurable. SLEEP_WINDOW_MS is compile-time (device/src/main.cpp:138, used :3565); tuning it costs a reflash, impossible during a deployment. It is one of the TWO quantities that decide whether a pre-sent command lands (the other is our wakeLeadMs) and tuning one while the other is frozen is guesswork. Model to copy: the interval command — we send it, the reply states the new value, we learn from the reply and mark beatSource=device.\n\nREQ2 — consider closing the RX window early once N messages have arrived. Battery: the window runs its full 8 s whether or not anything comes. Configurable count, not fixed at 1, or it closes on the first of two frames. Interacts with REQ1.\n\nREQ3 — log the life cycle: each phase START, so each phase END is derivable. Today a wake prints its actions and one summary (SLEEPNOW ... awake=) but never "RX window opened" or "drain started". The instant that matters most to us is invisible.\n\nWHY THIS IS ON OUR TASK BOARD AT ALL: REQ1 and REQ3 bound what this task can measure. windowMs is currently ASSUMED 10000 and measurement showed the real awake time ranges 9140-11209 ms, so the assumption is wrong in BOTH directions. Until the device states its window, Q6 cannot be answered properly and wakeLeadMs is being tuned against a guess.\n\nNOT BLOCKED ON THEM. Three defects found today are ours and come first: B51 (a measured beat overwrote a device-stated one with a value exactly 2x wrong), B52 (the wake detector is silently blind below its own 120 s burstGapMs), B54 (the accuracy metric reports health while the schedule is doubled).'}
2026-07-27 13:54:15,055 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'beat-authority', 'title': 'B51: a device-STATED beat must outrank a measured one, and a factor-of-N disagreement is aliasing, not a schedule change', 'steps': ["Investigate: every writer of beat/beatSource in mesh/lib/model.js (seed :113-126, agree :127-135, reseed :136-147) and how B43's interval-reply learning sets beatSource='device'. Establish exactly where a measured value can overwrite a stated one.", 'Decide the authority rule (Q13/Q15): device-stated is authoritative; a measurement may CORRECT it only within beatTolerance; a disagreement by a small-integer FACTOR (2x, 3x, 1/2, 1/3) is evidence our observation is aliased and must be rejected and logged loudly, never adopted. Record the counter-argument too: a stated beat can go stale (reflash, out-of-band command) and GARG states nothing at all, so measurement stays the fallback.', 'Implement. Scope is mesh/lib/model.js and whatever settings the rule needs declared.', "Acceptance, a number: replay today's BNCH sequence (device stated 60000; observed gaps ~120000 because burstGapMs hid every second wake) and prove beat stays 60000, beatSource stays 'device', and the aliasing is logged. ZERO overwrites in the replay, against 1 today.", 'Track: close B51 with the replay as evidence. Note in the ledger that this ALSO removes the mechanism that made B54 invisible.']}
2026-07-27 13:54:25,784 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'wake-detector-resolution', 'title': 'B52/B53: burstGapMs is fixed at 120 s, so the wake detector goes silently blind below it — and then blames the device', 'steps': ['Investigate: mode.burstGapMs (settings.js:115-123) and its only consumer (model.js:69-70). Measured burst spans: ~1 s on BNCH at a 60 s beat (frames at :05 and :06), against the up-to-59 s GARG bursts at a 900 s beat that motivated 120000 — and those long ones carried a command reply. Establish whether burst span scales with the beat or with what the wake carried.', 'Decide (Q11): fixed 120 s, or a fraction of the beat such as min(120000, beat/3), with a declared floor and ceiling. The failure to avoid is SILENCE — today the detector stops resolving wakes with no error and no warning, just fewer rows.', "Fix the note wording (Q12/B53): a long-gap note must not say 'missed wakes or reboot?' when the frames were received. It accused the device of the fault and sent the whole investigation to the wrong component first.", 'Acceptance, a number: with BNCH at a 60 s beat, wake_log records at least 90% of the wakes the recorder CSV shows arriving. Today it recorded 2 of 6 while the recorder held all six (12:13:05, 12:14:05, 12:15:05, 12:16:05, 12:17:06 UTC).', 'Track: close B52 and B53 with that ratio as evidence.']}
2026-07-27 13:54:38,637 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'wake-phase-precision', 'title': "Peter's target: predict the wake instant to under 10 ms. The period is EXACT, so only the phase needs estimating", 'steps': ["BLOCKED until beat-authority and wake-detector-resolution land. A min-filter over an aliased schedule converges beautifully on the WRONG answer, and today's schedule is aliased 2x. Do not start before both are closed.", 'Investigate the anchor: a wake sends TWO TELEMETRY_APP frames ~1 s apart (DEVICE then ENV), each duplicated by two relays, and mode.anchorPorts cannot tell them apart — so which frame anchors varies wake to wake, injecting ~1 s of jitter. That is the DOMINANT error term, and it is ours. Decide how to anchor on ONE identified frame.', "Calibrate the fixed offset, now possible for the first time: the bench unit prints its own wake instant on the CDC (SLEEPNOW woke=... -> next=...) while we independently record our anchor. Collect N paired samples and measure device-wake -> our-anchor directly, with the monitor's --filter time so both sides carry timestamps.", 'Design and implement the phase estimator: the delay is fixed_offset plus one-sided non-negative jitter (CSMA, relay, BLE queueing all ADD; none subtract), so the MINIMUM residual over many wakes converges on true_phase + fixed_offset, far tighter than a mean. Standard clock recovery, the NTP argument. No averaging of the kind that cost 2.5 hours to converge.', "Acceptance, Peter's number: |predicted - the device's own stated wake instant| < 10 ms over at least 20 consecutive wakes on the bench unit. Measured against the DEVICE's statement, never against our own prediction — R10 showed a self-referential error metric scores +-0.4 ms on a schedule that is 2x wrong. Today: +-0.9 s on GARG."]}
2026-07-27 13:54:50,227 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'debug-feed-integrity', 'title': 'B47/B48/B54: the instrument records the wrong units, calls telemetry a wake, and reports health while the schedule is 2x wrong', 'steps': ['B47 (Q4): wake_log holds 82 DISTINCT UNITS, of which 2 are ours. Every foreign Meshtastic node on the mesh gets wake rows and their errors run to minutes, so any aggregate over the table is meaningless. Decide: filter at the write site, or a declared-devices join at every read — no consumer has one today.', "B48 (Q5): an ALWAYS-LISTENING unit is recorded as waking. BNCH with slp=0 contributed 24 'clean' rows at median 5.6 s; those are telemetry arrivals wearing the word 'wake'. Either skip units with slp=0 at the writer, or rename the column and tell every consumer what it now means. This is the B44/B45 contamination reply-field-audit predicted, now confirmed present.", "B54 (Q14/R10): /v1/debug/accuracy is SELF-REFERENTIAL — err_ms is prediction minus observation, both ours, so an aliased schedule scored +-0.4 ms and the endpoint reported health while the unit was polled at half its real rate. Compare against the DEVICE'S stated beat, which we now hold (interval reply, and beat= in every SLEEPNOW line).", "Acceptance, a number: replay today's aliased BNCH sequence through the accuracy endpoint and have it report ALIASED rather than healthy. Today that sequence returns {n:24, medianMs:2666} with no indication anything is wrong. Plus: aggregate queries return rows for OUR units only, 2 not 82.", 'Track: close B47, B48, B54.']}
2026-07-27 13:55:01,902 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'wake-preempt-value', 'title': 'Does the pre-empt actually help? Measure first-attempt success and tries, NOT latency', 'steps': ['BLOCKED on beat-authority and wake-detector-resolution: while the schedule is aliased 2x and the detector resolves 2 wakes in 6, any rate measured here describes the instrument, not the feature.', "Establish why latency is the WRONG metric, from today's evidence: two pings, same unit, same firmware, same wakeLeadMs, 20 minutes apart — 109.5 s / 3 tries when queued mid-cycle, 12.8 s / 1 try when queued with the band already open. Latency measured when the operator pressed the button. GARG's historic median of 357.7 s is ~0.4 of a 900 s beat: dominated by WAITING for the next wake, which no lead value can shorten.", "Instrument the two paths so they are countable: 'PRE-WAKE (early)' vs 'window open —' both already log distinctly. Count deliveries by path and outcome.", 'Acceptance, two numbers over at least 20 attempts each on a SLEEPING unit: (a) first-attempt delivered rate, pre-wake vs reactive; (b) mean tries. Baselines to beat: GARG tries mean 2.0 (n=59) against BNCH 1.3 (n=119), whole history.', 'Then and only then, Q7: what lead value do the measured window (Q6) and phase accuracy imply? State it as a number derived from measurement, not a preference. wakeLeadMs is a deliberately tunable variable, not a defect — 10 s is a fine starting value (Peter, 2026-07-27).']}
2026-07-27 13:55:16,412 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'device-wake-observability', 'title': "TRACKING ONLY, flash-gated: the three firmware requests, the invisible RX, and GARG's old firmware", 'steps': ['No implementation here — device/ belongs to another session and all of this is flash-gated with a deployment imminent. This task exists so none of it is forgotten if it does not land.', 'Chase [wake-cycle-observability] on device--services: REQ1 make the listen window remotely configurable (SLEEP_WINDOW_MS is compile-time at main.cpp:138, used :3565); REQ2 consider closing the RX window after N messages, count configurable not fixed at 1; REQ3 log each life-cycle phase START so each phase END is derivable. A correction reply (#6) restores a word my shell ate — the model to copy is the INTERVAL command.', "Chase [sleep-rx-invisible-confirmed]: the device answers commands it never logs receiving. Across a 15-minute capture the heartbeat's rx= age climbed 360->960 s and never reset, no RX: line was printed, yet at up=2271s (12:19:57Z, the exact instant of our pre-wake send) it emitted ACK id=0x26dda692 and REPLY pki id=0x806f6a9f, and our ledger recorded ack_state=delivered with a pong. Reception leaves NO trace. rx= is the field whose own comment says it proves the receiver is alive.", "Q10: GARG runs OLD firmware and is the only unit that sleeps, so every wake measurement we hold for it came from a build that may not implement the guarantees measured on the bench (exact period, sleepNow(wokeAt)). Establish with the device session which claims hold for GARG's build. Flash-gated, so it may not be answerable before deployment.", 'Record what actually lands before the flash freezes, and what does not — so the next session knows which device-side facts are current and which are aspirations.']}
2026-07-27 14:01:30,363 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B55 CONFIRMED, SWAP-CRITICAL, ours — A SITE SWAP INVALIDATES EVERY PER-UNIT MEASUREMENT WE HOLD, AND NOTHING DETECTS IT. Found 2026-07-27 when Peter said he is swapping the bench and deployed units later today. Detail in docs/history/wake-preempt-performance.md R17.\n\nFIRST, THE DEVICE SIDE IS FINE — I checked before assuming, and my initial alarm was WRONG. loadSettings (device/src/main.cpp:1067-1071) re-baselines on a role change:\n  const bool moved = (s->roleIdx != g_roleIdx);\n  applyNumericSettings(moved ? g_role->heartbeatMsDefault : s->heartbeatMs, ..., moved ? (g_role->sleepDefault ? 1u : 0u) : s->sleepOn);\nRole comes from VBUS at boot (USB=bench index 0, battery=garage index 1). SITE_ROLES (include/secrets.h): bench sleepDefault=false heartbeatMsDefault=300000; garage sleepDefault=true heartbeatMsDefault=900000. So the interval 60 and sleepmode on I set on BNCH today will NOT follow the board to the garage — the comment there names that exact hazard ("a bench unit\'s sleepmode off following the board to the garage would leave it awake on battery until it died"). Retracted my warning rather than leave it standing.\n\nTHE DEFECT IS OURS. Node ids stay with the BOARDS, so after the swap !987ab80f is the garage unit and !8cee336b is the bench unit. Our persisted node_state holds beat, beatSource, wake_anchor and fw per unit id, and every one of them becomes wrong at the instant of the swap:\n  !987ab80f  stored 60000 (and today an aliased 120241)  ->  actually 900000\n  !8cee336b  stored ~901200                              ->  actually 300000\ninvalidate-beat-on-fw-change (B42) voids a measured beat when the FIRMWARE changes. A role change is not a firmware change, so the stale beat survives the swap. Correcting it then costs the full seeding path — two agreeing gaps, ~45 min at a 900 s beat — during which nextWake is confidently wrong and the pre-wake band fires against a schedule from the other site.\n\nALSO STALE: mesh/config.yaml devices pins "!8cee336b": label "Garage alarm", which inverts on the swap. config.yaml\'s own comment records that this bit us on 2026-07-25 ("labels ENCODE LOCATION, so they must be toggled on every physical swap ... they were inverted on 2026-07-25"). label-from-device (B40) derives the label from the device\'s own shortName and the firmware re-seeds names from the role on a move, so this MAY self-correct — unverified, and worth verifying before the swap rather than after.\n\nTHE DETECTION SIGNAL ALREADY EXISTS, unused: on a role change the firmware sets g_roleChanged and announces position + nodeinfo once the mesh is up (main.cpp:1084-1085). So a unit that has moved TELLS us, via a changed shortName and a changed position — two independent signals. Nothing on our side consumes either as a reason to void the wake model.\n\nFIX DIRECTION, not done: treat a role/shortName/position change like a fw change — void beat, beatSource and wake_anchor for that unit, exactly as invalidate-beat-on-fw-change already does. Own task; it is the same class as B42 and should probably extend it rather than duplicate it.\n\nPRIORITY: before the swap if possible, because the failure lands the moment the boards move and lasts ~45 min per unit while we send commands against the wrong schedule.'}
2026-07-27 14:04:42,361 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'wake-lifecycle-logging', 'title': "REQ3 + sleep-rx-invisible: log every wake phase boundary, and make a sleeping unit's RX visible. FLASH BEFORE THE SWAP.", 'steps': ["Investigate: DONE. sleepCycle (device/src/main.cpp:3527-3625) has no phase markers, so boundaries are inferred from print side-effects — R16 had to derive the RX window opening from the last 'TXT enq' line. The true wake instant is g_loopTop, stamped in doSleep:3496. Separately, the sleep RX window (3563-3600) receives via mesh.receive() and calls mtdb::heard() but NEVER sets g_lastRxMs and NEVER logs, while the RX: line at 4097 belongs to the continuous-listen path used only when awake. That is the whole of [sleep-rx-invisible].", 'Document: write device/docs/history/wake-lifecycle-logging.md (real spec, device scope) plus a crossrepo pointer in services/docs/history/, and attach the pointer. Same arrangement as serial-dbg-to-cdc-crossrepo.', 'Implement A (REQ3): one compact line per phase boundary, all offsets relative to wokeAt so each phase START also gives the previous phase END. CDC only, so no airtime and no battery cost on a deployed unit with no host attached.', "Implement B (sleep-rx-invisible): in the sleep RX window, set g_lastRxMs and log the packet, so rx= stops reading 'receiver dead' on a unit that is receiving and answering.", 'Observe: capture with pio device monitor --filter time and show the phase timings for a clean wake and an AGC-timeout wake. Acceptance A: every phase boundary readable WITHOUT arithmetic on other lines. Acceptance B: rx= resets within one beat of a command being delivered — today it climbed 360->960 s while the device answered two commands.', 'Track: close the device-side half of [sleep-rx-invisible-confirmed] and [wake-cycle-observability] REQ3. Append anything new found on the way to the standing bug ledger.']}
