2026-07-27 14:26:03,228 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'beat-authority', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'SCOPE ADDITION 2026-07-27, after Peter announced the unit swap: B55 belongs in THIS task, not a separate one, because it is the same design decision seen from the other side.\n\nB55: a site swap invalidates every per-unit measurement we hold and nothing detects it. Node ids stay with the BOARDS, so after the swap !987ab80f is the garage unit and !8cee336b is the bench unit, while our persisted node_state still holds the OTHER site\'s beat, anchor and fw for each id.\n\nTHE INTERACTION THAT MAKES IT ONE TASK: this task\'s core rule is "a device-STATED beat outranks a measured one". Right now our model holds beat=60000 for !987ab80f with beatSource=\'device\' — genuinely stated by the device, and about to become 15x wrong the moment that board boots on battery as the garage unit at 900000. Under the naive form of the rule, a stale STATED value could never be corrected by measurement, which is worse than today. So the rule needs a staleness trigger, and the swap is exactly the staleness case.\n\nTHE SIGNAL ALREADY EXISTS AND WE IGNORE IT: on a role change the firmware sets g_roleChanged and announces position + nodeinfo once the mesh is up (device/src/main.cpp:1084-1085). It also re-seeds names from the role. So a moved unit TELLS us, twice over — changed shortName and changed position. Nothing on our side treats either as a reason to void the wake model.\n\nALSO NOW AVAILABLE, from today\'s device work: the unit STATES its RX window (win in status and config, 2-260727-116) and re-states its beat whenever interval is used. So after a swap a single status round-trip re-establishes both authoritative values — the model does not have to re-measure for 45 minutes, it has to ASK.\n\nFIX DIRECTION: extend invalidate-beat-on-fw-change (B42) rather than duplicate it — same class, same void action, additional triggers: fw change (existing), role/shortName change, position change. Then a status round-trip repopulates from stated values.\n\nPRIORITY: this half is swap-critical. The failure lands the instant the boards move and costs ~45 min per unit of confidently-wrong schedule while we send commands against the other site\'s beat.'}
2026-07-27 14:58:50,342 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'fw-version-only-on-change', 'title': 'The build counter burns a number per BUILD, not per CHANGE — so the flashed version is never the tested one', 'steps': ["Investigate: DONE. tools/bump_fw.py already resets per day correctly (today's archive runs 1..116, yesterday 7 files, counter reads '260727 116'). Peter's request 'each new day should start with 1' is ALREADY the behaviour. The real defect is different: every invocation bumps, so `pio run` then `pio run -t upload` burns two numbers for one change and the version flashed is not the version just built and inspected. Measured on my own work today: built 113, flashed 114; built 115, flashed 116.", 'Document: device/docs/history/fw-version-only-on-change.md. Keep the daily reset exactly as it is.', "Implement: bump only when a SOURCE file is newer than the counter. Compare the newest mtime under src/, include/, lib/ and transport/ against the counter file's mtime; if nothing is newer, reuse the current version and rewrite nothing. Preserve the existing behaviour on a missing or corrupt counter (start the day at 1).", 'Acceptance, a number: run `pio run` twice with no edit in between and the version must NOT move; touch a source file and it must increment by exactly 1. Today the same sequence burns 2 numbers.', 'Track: tell the device session, since they share the counter and will see the cadence change.']}
2026-07-27 15:09:59,711 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'agc-reset-necessity', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'UNPARKED AND MEASURED 2026-07-27 on fw 2-260727-118, at Peter\'s direction ("the AGC is a HIGH priority, and I dont want assumptions to be made, this needs to be tested"). Probes added: agcProbe() reads SX1262 0x08AC (REG_RX_GAIN) and 0x08B5 (sensitivity bit) RAW at the START of a wake (before anything re-applies them) and at the END (just before the radio sleeps). Five consecutive sleep cycles, 60 s beat.\n\nRESULT, verbatim:\n  15:04:09 AGCPROBE sleep gain=0x96(boost) sens=0x05 resets=0\n  15:04:59 AGCPROBE wake  gain=0x96(boost) sens=0x04 resets=0\n  15:05:02 AGC reset: calibration timeout\n  15:05:02 LC radio  +2458ms agc=1\n  ... identical on all five wakes, resets counter climbing 0->4\n\nFINDING 1 — THE PREMISE IS FALSE FOR OUR DUTY CYCLE. gain reads 0x96 (BOOSTED) at every sleep AND every wake. The resetAGC() comment claims "The SX1262 loses RX boost ~60s after boot"; across a sleep/wake cycle the gain register demonstrably retains 0x96. Peter\'s hypothesis was right: whatever the datasheet\'s retention note means, it is not "our 11 s wake loses the boost".\n\nFINDING 2 — THE RESET FAILS EVERY SINGLE TIME. "AGC reset: calibration timeout" on 5 of 5 wakes. That branch returns EARLY (main.cpp resetAGC), so it never reaches calibrateImage(), setDio2AsRfSwitch(), setRxBoostedGainMode() or applyRxSensitivityPatch(). It accomplishes nothing at all.\n\nFINDING 3 — AND IT BLOCKS THE FIX FOR THE THING THAT IS ACTUALLY LOST. The sensitivity bit does NOT survive sleep: 0x08B5 reads 0x05 (bit0 set) going into the first sleep and 0x04 (bit0 clear) on waking, and stays 0x04 forever after. The only code that re-applies it on wake is the ELSE branch of `if (g_agcOn && !mesh.busy()) resetAGC(); else applyRxSensitivityPatch("wake")` — which is unreachable while AGC is enabled. So enabling AGC costs 2458 ms per wake AND leaves the sensitivity bit clear, while DISABLING it would restore the patch.\n\nCOST, measured: 2458 ms of the 11202 ms awake time, every wake, ~22%. On a battery unit that is the largest single controllable cost in the cycle, and it buys nothing.\n\nANSWERS TO PETER\'S FOUR QUESTIONS:\nQ-A (do we check before calling it?) NO. doSleep calls resetAGC() whenever g_agcOn && !mesh.busy(), with no test of whether the state it forces is already correct. It should read 0x08AC first: the answer is already 0x96.\nQ-B (is it needed in sleep mode?) On this evidence NO for the gain, YES for the sensitivity bit — which the current code fails to re-apply precisely because the reset is enabled.\nQ-C (should the function test necessity?) Yes, and the test is one register read.\nQ-D (what does Meshtastic do?) resetAGC() is a documented VERBATIM PORT of Meshtastic SX126xInterface<T>::resetAGC(). What was NOT ported is their calling context: they run it periodically while awake, we run it on every sleep resume. Still to check against their source.\n\nNOT FIXED YET — deliberately. The measurement is the deliverable; the fix is a separate change with its own acceptance number, and the obvious candidates (skip when 0x08AC already reads 0x96; always apply the sensitivity patch regardless of the AGC branch; investigate why calibration times out) are three changes, not one. Flash-gated with a swap imminent.'}
2026-07-27 15:11:12,402 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'flash-sleeping-unit-myth', 'title': 'CLAUDE.md says wake a sleeping RAK over the mesh before flashing. Tested 2026-07-27: false.', 'steps': ["Investigate: DONE by experiment. CLAUDE.md states 'To reflash a sleeping unit: sleepmode off over the MESH first, confirm it landed, then flash.' Peter, 2026-07-27: 'I think that the message in CLAUDE.md about needing to contact the unit before a flash is a myth and is wrong. the rak will wake up automatically when flashed.'", "TESTED: unit confirmed asleep (slp=1, awake=False) on a 60 s beat, then `pio run -t upload` with NO prior wake. Result: 'Forcing reset using 1200bps open/close' -> 'Device programmed' -> SUCCESS in 30.17 s. USB event count 118 -> 119, i.e. one normal DFU enumeration. Identical binary flashed so nothing else changed.", 'MECHANISM, established independently the same morning (wake-preempt-performance R1): the nRF52840 keeps the USB peripheral alive in System-ON sleep. The CDC stayed enumerated across four sleep cycles with the USB event count unchanged. So the port the 1200 bps touch needs is present regardless of whether the application is sleeping.', "The 7-hour DFU incident was 60 UPLOADS IN A LOOP, not one upload at a sleeping unit. The surviving rule is 'never loop an upload' — each retry is another reset. The wake-first step was ceremony attached to the wrong cause.", 'Implement: correct services/CLAUDE.md. State what was tested, keep the never-loop rule, and keep the recovery path for a board already in DFU.', 'Track: tell the device session, since the same claim may be in their CLAUDE.md, and note the cost — two flashes today each waited ~2 minutes for a sleepmode off round-trip that was never needed.']}
