2026-07-27 15:19:19,370 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'fw-version-format', 'title': 'Version becomes YYMMDD-<daily>-<lifetime>, and the services parser must read BOTH it and the old format', 'steps': ["Investigate: DONE. Peter wants the daily build number to be the headline ('270727-1 is the first build of today') plus an overall count. The current shape is 2-YYMMDD-n. CRITICAL and nearly missed: services/mesh/index.js:424 extracts fw from the device long name with /(?:^|\\s)(\\d+-\\d{6}-\\d+)\\s*$/, which requires the CURRENT shape. A new-format string would not match, fw would silently become null, and invalidate-beat-on-fw-change (B42) keys off fw changing — so stale beats would stop being voided, exactly at the swap.", 'ALSO CRITICAL: GARG runs old firmware (2-260725-20) and KEEPS it after the swap, so the parser must accept BOTH formats indefinitely. This is not a switch-over, it is a widening.', 'Implement device side: tools/bump_fw.py emits YYMMDD-<daily>-<lifetime>. Daily resets on a new day (already correct); lifetime is monotonic across all days. Increment on EVERY build, per Peter 2026-07-27 — this reverses the fw-version-only-on-change decision made earlier the same day, recorded there rather than silently dropped.', 'Implement services side: widen the regex to accept both \\d{6}-\\d+-\\d+ (new) and \\d+-\\d{6}-\\d+ (old). One expression, both shapes, with the old one still winning for GARG.', "Acceptance, numbers not adjectives: (a) two consecutive builds produce daily counts n and n+1 with the lifetime count also +1; (b) the parser test extracts '260727-3-119' from a new-format name AND '2-260725-20' from an old-format name; (c) after flashing, GET /v1/mesh/devices reports a non-null fw for the bench unit in the new format — today it would report null and nobody would notice until a beat went stale.", 'Track: this is an interface change between device and services. Announce on device--services, and note that node-dash may render fw too.']}
2026-07-27 15:26:44,663 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'agc-reset-necessity', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'FIXED AND VERIFIED 2026-07-27 on 2-260727-119. Full detail in device/docs/history/agc-reset-necessity.md.\n\nTHE CHANGE (two lines at the wake site in doSleep):\n  was: if (g_agcOn && !mesh.busy()) resetAGC(); else applyRxSensitivityPatch("wake");\n  now: if (g_agcOn && !mesh.busy() && g_radioMod.SPIgetRegValue(0x08AC) != 0x96) resetAGC();\n       applyRxSensitivityPatch("wake");   // ALWAYS\nA one-register necessity check before an expensive reset, and an unconditional re-assert of the bit that measurably never survives a sleep.\n\nACCEPTANCE, all four met:\n  sensitivity bit at the SLEEP probe   0x04 -> 0x05, all 5 cycles\n  AGC calibration timeouts per 5 wakes    5 -> 0\n  LC radio phase                    2458 ms -> 396 ms\n  SLEEPNOW awake=                  11202 ms -> 9141 ms\n~2.06 s saved on EVERY wake, and the repair that was previously unreachable now happens.\n\nCORRECTION TO MY OWN CRITERION, recorded not restated: I first wrote "AGCPROBE wake sens= must read 0x05". Wrong — the wake probe runs BEFORE the patch by design, because its job is to measure what SURVIVED sleep, so it reads 0x04 forever and that IS the finding. The criterion that proves the repair is the SLEEP probe.\n\nTHE UNPREDICTED FINDING, and it is worth more than the saving: THE CALIBRATION TIMEOUT WAS POSITIONAL, NOT RANDOM.\n  called from the WAKE site, immediately after mesh.wake():  timed out 5 of 5\n  called from the PERIODIC site in loop(), later in the same wake: SUCCEEDED 5 of 5, ~390 ms\nSame function, same board, same wake, different instant. The radio is simply not ready that early after waking. That fully explains the old behaviour — a reset that could never succeed because of WHERE it was called — and retires the theory that calibration is unreliable on this hardware.\n\nSTILL OPEN, now with better evidence:\n1. The PERIODIC caller still runs a full reset every ~60 s, which on a 60 s beat is once per wake, ~390 ms of awake time buying nothing (F1: the boost never drops). The same necessity check belongs there. One line, its own measurement, deliberately NOT folded in.\n2. Why is the radio not ready immediately after mesh.wake()? Now that the timeout has a cause, the question is whether a short settle or a BUSY-line wait would make a wake-site reset viable for the case where the boost genuinely IS lost.\n3. RXPATCH is now applied twice per wake (once unconditionally, once inside the periodic reset). Harmless — one SPI write — but duplicated, and it looks odd in a capture.'}
2026-07-27 16:05:08,908 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B56 CONFIRMED, device-side, low severity today but exactly the class this project keeps paying for — PIN_CAM_RX / PIN_CAM_TX ARE DECLARED IN io.h AND CONSUMED BY NOTHING.\n\n  include/io.h:42  static const uint32_t PIN_CAM_RX = 15;  // camera link RX (variant default)\n  include/io.h:43  static const uint32_t PIN_CAM_TX = 16;  // camera link TX (variant default)\n\ngrep over src/ include/ lib/ transport/ returns those two declarations and NO other reference. setup() calls SERIAL_CAM.begin(115200) with no setPins(), so the pins the camera link actually uses come from the VARIANT, not from io.h.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS. io.h opens with "EVERY pin and I/O assignment on this board, in ONE place. Nothing outside this file may name a bare Arduino port object or a bare pin number." Two of its constants describe the hardware without controlling it: editing them changes nothing, and the file that is supposed to be the single source of truth is silently not.\n\nIT IS CURRENTLY HARMLESS AND THAT IS THE TRAP. The variant in force, variants/WisCore_RAK4631_Board/variant.h:100-101, sets PIN_SERIAL1_RX (15) and PIN_SERIAL1_TX (16), which happens to agree with io.h. So everything works and the disagreement is invisible. Another variant in the wider tree — pio/projects/mtfw/variants/rak4631_epaper_onrxtx/variant.h:80-81 — sets BOTH to (-1). Building against that variant would leave Serial1 with no pins and the camera link permanently dead, while io.h still claimed 15/16 and the boot probe reported a plain "CAMPROBE: NO RESPONSE" with no hint that the pins were the cause.\n\nFOUND WHILE diagnosing a camera that is genuinely absent (2026-07-27, Peter re-attached it; CAMPROBE: NO RESPONSE on two consecutive boots, camu ping returns FF, no 0x62 on the I2C scan). The pin question was eliminated by reading the variant, which is precisely the lookup io.h exists to make unnecessary.\n\nFIX DIRECTION, not done: either SERIAL_CAM.begin() passes the io.h constants explicitly (Adafruit\'s Uart has setPins(rx, tx) — the debug UART used it until it was deleted on 2026-07-27), or io.h stops claiming ownership and says the variant owns these two, naming the variant file. The first is better: it makes io.h true rather than merely descriptive. Either way it is a FLASH change and a swap is imminent, so it waits.'}
2026-07-27 16:22:53,489 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B57 CONFIRMED, device-side, and it is the "values that lie" class this project has already paid for twice — THE UNIT REPORTS A HEALTHY BATTERY WHEN NO BATTERY IS CONNECTED.\n\nMEASURED 2026-07-27. Peter confirmed the bench unit has NO battery fitted (that is also why the camera was dead: it was powered from the VBAT header pin). At that moment the firmware was reporting, over the air, in status:\n  "vbat":4.26,"batt":100\n\nRAK19007 datasheet, on the exact pin Peter measured (J11 pin 4, silkscreen BAT): the path is battery JST P2 -> VBAT net -> J11 pin 4, with NO switch and no gating, and "when powered by USB without a battery connected: the VBAT net remains unpowered, J11 pin 4 would read 0 V". The RAK4631 measures battery voltage from that same net. So the true value was 0 V and we reported 4.26 V at 100%.\n\nTHE CODE, device/src/main.cpp:1465-1479:\n  static float readVbatVolts() {\n      analogReference(AR_INTERNAL_3_0); analogReadResolution(12);\n      (void)analogRead(BATTERY_PIN); delay(2);\n      float raw = analogRead(BATTERY_PIN);\n      return raw * (3.0f / 4096.0f) * 1.73f;\n  }\n  static uint8_t lipoPercent(float v) { if (v >= 4.2f) return 100; ... }\nThere is NO validity check anywhere: no "is a battery present", no plausibility band, no distinction between "measured 0 V" and "measured nothing". A floating ADC input on an unpowered net reads whatever it reads, the arithmetic converts it, and lipoPercent() clamps anything >= 4.2 V to a confident 100%.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS:\n1. The deployed unit is battery-powered and 2.5 km away. Battery telemetry is the ONLY warning we would get before it dies, and this shows the reading can be fabricated rather than absent. A disconnected or failed cell could report 100% indefinitely.\n2. It breaks the project\'s own rule, stated in services/CLAUDE.md: "If a value was not measured, the API returns null and says why. Never infer a physical fact." The device is the other end of that rule and does the opposite.\n3. It silently corroborates a wrong conclusion: while diagnosing the camera today, vbat=4.26 was the single piece of evidence pointing AWAY from "no battery fitted", and it was false. It cost time and it would have cost more if Peter had not known his own hardware.\n\nFIX DIRECTION, not done (flash-gated, swap imminent): the clamp at >= 4.2 V is what turns an implausible reading into a confident one. A reading above the LiPo maximum, or below a floor, is evidence of NO BATTERY rather than of a full one, and should be reported as such — null/absent over the air, not 100%. Also worth deciding whether USB presence (already latched at boot for the site role, NRF_POWER->USBREGSTATUS) should qualify the reading.\n\nNOT the same as B16/config-values-that-lie (a config knob nothing read). This is a MEASUREMENT that reports a plausible number for a quantity that does not exist.'}
2026-07-27 16:28:59,929 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B57 WITHDRAWN, 2026-07-27, within minutes of being recorded. NOT A DEFECT.\n\nThe entry claimed the unit reports a healthy battery when none is connected, on the strength of Peter saying "the battery is not connected" while status reported vbat:4.26 batt:100. He then found his multimeter was set to AC range, not DC: there IS power on that pin and there IS a battery. The firmware\'s 4.26 V was CORRECT, and the reading it was accused of fabricating was the true one.\n\nSo the premise is withdrawn and the finding goes with it. Read B57 as void, not as open.\n\nWHAT SURVIVES, downgraded to UNVERIFIED and stated as code-reading only, NOT as an observed fault:\nreadVbatVolts() (device/src/main.cpp:1465) converts the ADC with no plausibility check, and lipoPercent() clamps anything >= 4.2 V to 100. So a reading above the LiPo maximum is reported as a full battery rather than as an implausible measurement. That MIGHT matter on a unit whose battery becomes disconnected in the field, but nothing has been observed doing it, and today\'s evidence now says the measurement path works. It stays here as a question to test deliberately (disconnect the cell on the bench and read the ADC), not as a bug.\n\nTHE LESSON, recorded because it is mine and it is the more useful half: I took one statement about the hardware, found code that COULD produce the claimed symptom, and wrote it up as CONFIRMED with a severity argument attached. The reading I called fabricated was the accurate one, and the instrument that was wrong was outside the system entirely. "Confirmed" should mean I measured both sides, not that I measured one and reasoned to the other — and the whole point of today\'s AGC work was that untested premises are expensive.\n\nCamera status after this: it HAS power, so power is eliminated. It still does not answer — CAMPROBE: NO RESPONSE on two boots, camu ping returns FF, no 0x62 on the I2C scan. Remaining candidates are the camera\'s own firmware/sleep state, cable continuity on G4/G13, or baud.'}
2026-07-27 16:31:26,611 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'DEPLOYMENT RISK (not a defect, no fix implied) — THE CAMERA CANNOT BE RESTARTED REMOTELY IF IT EVER FULLY POWERS DOWN. Recorded 2026-07-27 while diagnosing a camera that had power but would not answer.\n\nTOPOLOGY, confirmed by Peter: the RAK feeds the M5 Timer Camera X through the CAMERA\'S OWN BATTERY CONNECTOR, at 4.12 V — electrically what a healthy cell would present. There is NO external/USB power at the camera.\n\nWHY IT WAS SILENT, and it is not a fault in either firmware:\n- M5 docs for Timer Camera X: power on is "press and hold for 2s" on the PWR button, and "when using external power, the device will remain powered on". USB keeps it up; a battery alone does not start it.\n- Our camera firmware says the same from the inside (pio/projects/timercam-chunk/src/main.cpp:362-366): "the regulator cuts power on battery (USB masks it) - the ESP powers off entirely and can\'t ext0-wake. With the hold, deep sleep actually reaches ~10 uA and ext0 wake works."\n- The latch is G33 (PIN_BAT_HOLD), asserted in setup() at :493-494 and held across deep sleep with gpio_hold_en at :365. The ESP32 holds its OWN power up, so it can only do so once it is already running.\n\nSo applying battery voltage supplies the regulator but does not boot the board, and with no power downstream of the latch nothing on the UART can wake it - ext0 needs the chip powered. That fully explains CAMPROBE: NO RESPONSE on two boots, camu ping returning FF, and no 0x62 on the I2C scan, with 4.12 V measured at the input.\n\nTHE RISK FOR THE DEPLOYED UNIT: our design deliberately chose ext0 deep sleep (~10 uA, on-demand capture) over an RTC-scheduled wake that cuts power via G33 - the trade is recorded in that file\'s header. That choice is right for responsiveness, but it means the camera stays alive ONLY as long as it never loses the latch. If it ever powers down completely - deep discharge, a supply interruption, a failed boot, or any path that releases G33 - it cannot be restarted over the mesh, over the UART, or by the RAK. It needs a physical 2 s button press at the garage, 2.5 km away.\n\nNO BM8563 RTC ALARM IS CONFIGURED as a fallback: the firmware\'s own comment names "RTC-scheduled wake" as the alternative it did NOT take. So there is currently no automatic path back from a full power-down.\n\nWORTH DECIDING BEFORE THE SWAP, not after: whether an RTC alarm should be armed purely as a recovery path (wake every N hours, latch G33, go back to ext0 sleep), so a camera that loses power restores itself instead of waiting for a site visit. That is a camera-firmware change and it is flash-gated on a board that is about to be deployed.'}
2026-07-27 16:43:12,175 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'camera-rtc-recovery', 'title': 'The camera needs a 2 s button press to start, and nothing remote can do it — arm the BM8563 RTC so it self-starts', 'steps': ["Investigate: DONE, from M5 docs and our own camera source. A fully powered-off Timer Camera X has exactly TWO documented power-on sources: the PWR button (press and hold 2 s) and the BM8563 RTC alarm. M5 docs also state 'when using external power, the device will remain powered on' — USB masks the whole problem, battery-only does not. Our firmware (pio/projects/timercam-chunk/src/main.cpp) uses NEITHER: it latches its own power with G33 in setup() and relies on ext0 deep sleep, which only works while the chip still HAS power. Its header says the RTC 'is on its OWN bus (G14/G12) and is not touched here'.", "THE GAP, stated plainly: the camera is powered from the RAK's VBAT pin with no USB. On a cold start, or after any full power-down (deep discharge, supply interruption, failed boot, any path that releases G33), it stays off until somebody physically presses the button. On the deployed unit that is a 2.5 km site visit, and the alarm's image capture is silently dead until then.", "Decide the mechanism: arm a BM8563 timer/alarm IRQ before entering deep sleep, re-armed on every boot, so a camera that has lost power restarts itself within N minutes. The RTC INT drives the power circuit — that is what makes this a 'Timer' Camera and it is the documented path. Confirm against the BM8563 datasheet and M5's own driver (bmm8563_setTimerIRQ) rather than assuming register layout.", 'Decide N. It is a straight trade: shorter N recovers faster, longer N costs less battery. The recovery wake can be very short — power on, latch G33, re-arm, drop back to ext0 sleep — so the cost is dominated by boot current, not by N itself.', 'Check the interaction with ext0: while the ESP32 IS alive in deep sleep, the RTC IRQ must not fight the on-demand wake path or cause a reboot loop. Establish what the IRQ does to a board that is already powered.', "Acceptance, measured on the bench with NO USB to the camera: pull the camera's power, restore it, and WITHOUT touching the button confirm it comes back within N minutes and answers camu ping. Today that test fails permanently by design.", 'FLASH-GATED and it is the CAMERA that must be flashed, over USB, which needs physical access. Do it before the swap or the deployed camera keeps this failure mode.']}
2026-07-27 16:51:00,245 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B58 CONFIRMED, device-side — `camu ping` CANNOT SUCCEED AGAINST A SLEEPING CAMERA, AND A FAILED PING OVERWRITES THE BOOT PROBE\'S CORRECT ANSWER WITH cam:0.\n\nFound 2026-07-27. Peter powered the camera on (2 s button press), rebooted the RAK, and status reported cam:1 — the boot probe had found it. I then ran `camu ping` from the USB console, it returned {"ping":"FF","ok":0}, and status has read cam:0 ever since. Peter noticed the disagreement; the flag was right before I touched it.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, from the firmware:\n- camBootProbe() is PATIENT: 4 attempts, 800 ms each, ~3.2 s total. That is enough for an ESP32 to wake from ext0 deep sleep and finish booting, which is why it found the camera.\n- `camu ping` writes 0x55 and waits ~400 ms for 0xAA. The camera sleeps in ext0 deep sleep between interactions; the 0x55 start bit WAKES it, but an ESP32 needs roughly 1-2 s to boot before it can answer. 400 ms cannot span that, so the ping fails against a healthy sleeping camera every time.\n- g_camAlive is documented as "probed at boot, tracked LIVE by every camera interaction thereafter (grab outcome, camu ping)". So the failed ping does not merely report wrongly, it WRITES the wrong answer into the flag that status/telemetry then reports.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND TODAY: cam is the field a remote operator uses to decide whether the camera is present. On the deployed unit, one impatient probe turns "camera present and asleep" into "camera absent" permanently, until the next boot probe or a successful grab. That is a diagnostic that manufactures a fault and then persuades you of it — the same class as the AGC reset that failed 5/5 and was believed for weeks, and it cost real time here today: it is why I went looking at wiring, power, pins and the variant while the camera was in fact fine.\n\nFIX DIRECTION, not done (flash-gated, swap imminent):\n1. Give `camu ping` a wake-aware timeout: send the 0x55, then wait on the order of the boot time (the grab path already allows 9 s via g_gsDeadline and gets this right), or send a wake pulse first and probe after.\n2. A FAILED probe should not clear g_camAlive when the failure is indistinguishable from "asleep". Absence of an answer within 400 ms is not evidence of absence. Either widen the timeout or make only a POSITIVE result write the flag.\n3. Consider reporting cam as tri-state — present / absent / unknown — rather than a bool that has to pick one when it does not know. Same rule as the services side already follows: say only what was measured.\n\nNOT the camera\'s fault and not the wiring: power, G4/G13 orientation, RAK pins 15/16, the variant and the CAM_UART build were all verified correct today.'}
2026-07-27 16:58:51,742 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'camera-rtc-recovery', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'DEFERRED BY PETER, 2026-07-27, deliberately and with the cost understood: "probably both can wait, it means that for the remote unit I have to power the cam on after deploying it. no big deal for the time being". He is going onsite to swap the units and does not want either camera fix holding that up.\n\nSO THE ACCEPTED POSITION IS:\n- The deployed camera will be powered on BY HAND (2 s button press) after it is wired at the garage.\n- If it ever loses power completely thereafter, it stays off until the next site visit. There is no remote path back — not over the mesh, not over the UART, not from the RAK. That is understood and accepted, not overlooked.\n- B58 (camu ping cannot succeed against a sleeping camera, and a failed ping overwrites cam:1 with cam:0) also waits. It is a diagnostic defect, not a capability one, but it means cam= will keep lying after any impatient probe.\n\nWHAT THAT MAKES IMPORTANT AT THE SITE VISIT: pressing the button is a step that must not be forgotten, because forgetting it is indistinguishable at home from the camera being broken — especially while B58 stands. Worth doing a `cam grab` from the house before leaving the site, since a successful grab is the only probe that proves the camera end to end AND sets cam:1 correctly.\n\nBoth fixes remain fully specified above and unblocked whenever the flash freeze lifts.'}
