2026-07-27 17:14:12,256 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_file_attach args={'file_path': 'docs/history/beat-authority.md', 'kind': 'plan', 'label': 'B51+B55: device-stated beat wins; factor-of-N is aliasing; identity change voids the schedule'}
2026-07-27 18:02:34,620 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B59 CONFIRMED — /v1/mesh/devices REPORTS ONLY THE GATEWAY\'S rssi/snr, SO IT UNDERSTATES THE LINK WHENEVER THE OTHER RADIO HEARS BETTER.\n\nFound 2026-07-27 while Peter was onsite. I reported the garage unit as "-116 dBm" from GET /v1/mesh/devices; he replied "well last time it contacted it was 106". He was right and the API was misleading. The recorder holds both receptions of the SAME packet:\n\n  16:25:13Z  E9:B0:3F:17:27:91 (OMNI)  rssi=-116  snr=-13.25\n  16:25:13Z  F4:12:FA:39:F7:B6 (YAGI)  rssi=-106  snr=+1.50\n\nTen dB and, far more importantly, the SIGN OF THE SNR: OMNI hears it at -13.25 dB (deep below the noise floor, near the SF11 demod limit around -17.5) while YAGI hears the same transmission at +1.5 dB, comfortably above it. Those two numbers support opposite conclusions about link health, and we publish only the pessimistic one.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING A DETAIL:\n1. It is the number used to judge whether the deployed unit is in trouble. Reporting the worse receiver makes a healthy link look marginal, which invites exactly the wrong action - moving antennas, raising TX power, or a site visit that was never needed.\n2. It cost credibility in the field today: the operator standing next to the hardware had better information than the service, and had to correct it.\n3. align.js already knows there are two radios - cfg.align.radios lists omni !2687afb1 and yagi !fa39f7b4 - so the second receiver is not news to the codebase, only to this route.\n\nFIX DIRECTION, not done: /devices should report the BEST reception (max snr, or the pair), not merely whichever came via the configured gateway. Candidates: rssi/snr from the strongest receiver plus a `via` naming which radio heard it, or both readings side by side. The honest minimum is to stop presenting one radio\'s view as "the" signal.\n\nRELATED, worth checking at the same time: one row at 16:24:40Z shows OMNI hearing 8cee336b at rssi=-33 snr=6.5, which is implausible for a 2.5 km link and sits 83 dB away from the reading 30 s later. Either a relayed copy is carrying the relay\'s own rssi, or a duplicate is being attributed to the wrong hop. Unexplained; do not build on that figure until it is understood.'}
2026-07-27 18:17:43,430 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'radio-selection', 'title': 'Use BOTH radios and transmit through whichever measurably reaches the target best — supersedes static routing, absorbs B59', 'steps': ["Investigate the plumbing that already exists. mesh-gw has BOTH radios connected and READY (!2687afb1 'Peter Omni RAK' / E9:B0:3F:17:27:91, and !fa39f7b4 'Peter YAGI Rotator' / F4:12:FA:39:F7:B6), and its send endpoint is POST /{node_id}/messages — the radio is a PATH PARAMETER. So per-radio transmit needs no new infrastructure; our service simply always posts to the configured gatewayId. Establish exactly where that id is bound and what else assumes a single gateway.", 'Define the metric. Use SNR, not RSSI: measured 2026-07-27 on one packet, omni -116/-13.25 and yagi -106/+1.5. The RSSI gap is 10 dB but the decisive fact is SNR crossing zero, which is what decides demodulation. Decide the window (last N receptions? decayed mean?) and the hysteresis margin (~3 dB) needed to stop two near-equal radios swapping on every packet and making delivery data unattributable.', "Keep per-(unit, radio) link stats. Today we keep rssi/snr per UNIT, from whichever radio the gateway event arrived through — that is B59, and it is why /devices reported -116 while Peter measured -106. The data already exists (the recorder logs BOTH receptions of every packet); it is simply not kept in the model. This is the plumbing the whole task rests on, and it also fixes B59's reporting.", "THE BEAM GUARD, and note it INVERTS under a measured scheme. The yagi is on target now (az 122.0 vs a derived bearing of 121.2, error +0.8 deg) but it ROTATES. A measurement taken at one heading is void the moment the rotator moves — so the guard stops being 'is it pointed right' and becomes 'are these samples still valid'. Live facts are on the rotator ws (ws://192.168.10.186:81): az, busy, stall, plus ppd/blDeg/northOffset. Do NOT copy those into config; read them.", 'VERIFY RECIPROCITY IS NOT BEING ASSUMED TOO FAR. Antenna gain is reciprocal, so hearing better does imply transmitting better — but configured TX POWER is not. Check txp on both radios before trusting a received-strength comparison as a transmit-strength prediction.', "OPEN QUESTIONS WE HAVE NOT THOUGHT THROUGH — each could invalidate the design, listed so none is discovered late: (a) CHANNEL PARITY: is the yagi configured with the same channels and PSKs as the omni, at the same indices? The ta2m skill records that OMNI's Private channel is index 2; the yagi's numbering is unverified and a wrong index would transmit on the PUBLIC channel. (b) REPLY ADDRESSING: a command sent via the yagi leaves with the YAGI as sender, so the device replies to it — does our correlation, the butler's ack path and S1's message_status keying still hold when the sending radio changes? (c) DUPLICATE RECEPTION: with two radios every packet already arrives twice; check whether the wake model, wake_log and lastHeard are double-counting today. (d) PKC/DM: a directed message encrypts to the sender's key, so switching radios changes keys mid-conversation. (e) AIRTIME: per-node duty cycle accounting moves with the sending radio. (f) LEDGER: which radio a command went out on MUST be recorded, or wake-preempt-value's first-attempt-rate numbers are unattributable. (g) FAILOVER: a radio whose BLE state is not READY must be routed around. (h) CONTENTION: align.js already drives scans/bursts and the rotator may be pointed elsewhere for other work — who owns the antenna's heading. (i) SAFETY: moving the rotator is a physical action; decide who may command it and whether this task ever does.", "Config shape, in host/host.config.json NOT mesh/config.yaml — align.radios exists in both and only the former is read by the running service (B49, proven this morning when GARG's mode:dev pin turned out to be inert). Radios gain antenna type, rotates, and rotator host/ports; `gateway` names a RADIO rather than repeating a node id. Do NOT declare bearings — they derive from positions we already hold, and a declared physical fact is the trap this project keeps hitting.", "Acceptance, measured not asserted: for a sleeping unit, first-attempt delivered rate and mean tries with selection ON vs OFF, at least 20 attempts each. Baseline to beat is GARG's historic tries mean 2.0 (n=59). Plus: /devices reports the BEST reception with a `via` naming the radio, where today it reports the gateway's view only.", "Worth stating up front: if the ~10 dB is reciprocal this is probably worth more to command delivery than the entire wake pre-empt effort. GARG sits at -114 to -128 dBm, and 10 dB is the difference between 'usually lands' and 'usually does not'."]}
2026-07-27 18:23:57,595 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'radio-selection', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'CRITICAL CORRECTION to the metric, 2026-07-27, from Peter onsite: "my guess is that was relayed from yagi to omni a known bug". He was right, and it invalidates the naive form of this whole task.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE. The same packet id, heard twice, 8 seconds apart, with DIFFERENT relay values:\n  17:17:19Z  YAGI (F4:12:FA:39:F7:B6)  pkt=48a6a4e7  rssi=-112  snr=-2.75  hop=1/3  relay=2\n  17:17:27Z  OMNI (E9:B0:3F:17:27:91)  pkt=48a6a4e7  rssi= -46  snr=+6.00  hop=/3   relay=40\nThe -46 dBm is the signal of whatever RELAYED it, not of 987ab80f. Two readings of one transmission, 66 dB apart, describing two different physical links.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS TO THIS TASK: the plan was "transmit through whichever radio hears the unit best". Computed over raw receptions that is unsound - it would pick an antenna based on how loudly a NEIGHBOUR shouted, not on our path to the target. Antenna reciprocity says nothing about a third node\'s relay.\n\nSO THE METRIC MUST USE DIRECT RECEPTIONS ONLY: hop_limit == hop_start (no hops consumed), and ideally relay unset/self. Anything else is a different link and must not enter the comparison.\n\nAND THE DIRECT PICTURE IS MUCH WORSE THAN THE HEADLINE NUMBERS SUGGESTED:\n  987ab80f  last DIRECT 16:46:58Z  rssi=-128 snr=-19.25\n  8cee336b  last DIRECT 16:24:39Z  rssi=-128 snr=-19.75\nSF11 demodulates to roughly -17.5 dB, so BOTH units are at or below the floor on a direct path to BOTH of our antennas. Every reception in the last hour arrived via other nodes relaying. The mesh has been carrying us and the raw figures hid it.\n\nCONSEQUENCES TO CARRY INTO THE DESIGN:\n1. Filter to direct receptions before any comparison, or the selection is confidently wrong.\n2. If direct samples are scarce (they now are), the selector must handle "no recent direct evidence" as UNKNOWN rather than defaulting to a stale winner - the same unknown-is-not-a-known-negative rule as B30/B32.\n3. This also revises B59: /devices should report the best DIRECT reception with a `via`, and say so when it only has relayed data. Reporting a relay\'s rssi as the unit\'s link is the defect underneath both.\n\nALSO CORRECTED, mine: I earlier flagged a -33 dBm reading as "implausible, do not build on it". It is not a sensor fault - it is this same relay effect. The reading is real, it just describes a different link than the one labelled. Recorded so the earlier caution is not carried forward as a phantom bug.'}
