2026-07-27 18:43:12,014 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'radio-selection', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'LINK TOPOLOGY, from Peter 2026-07-27 — it is YAGI-TO-YAGI, not yagi-to-omni.\n\n"there\'s a yagi in the garage and a yagi here, both point at each other", and "garage yagi is fixed at bearing 310 degrees".\n\nGEOMETRY, derived from the two site positions in device/include/secrets.h:\n  house -> garage   121.2 deg   2.501 km\n  garage -> house   301.2 deg\n  house yagi (rotator, live)  az 122.0  -> 0.8 deg off-axis\n  garage yagi (FIXED)         az 310.0  -> 8.8 deg off-axis\nBoth are inside the main lobe of any typical LoRa yagi, so both ends are essentially aimed at each other. The garage end\'s 8.8 deg costs something small; no dB figure without the antenna\'s pattern, and we do not have it.\n\nWHAT THIS EXPLAINS: the 10 dB and the SIGN of the SNR. YAGI hears the garage unit at -106/+1.5 while OMNI hears the same packet at -116/-13.25. That is not one directional antenna beating an omni — it is a DIRECTIONAL-TO-DIRECTIONAL path, with gain at both ends, against an omni that has gain at neither. It also means the advantage is larger and more reliable than a single-ended yagi would give.\n\nWHAT IT DOES TO THIS TASK: it strengthens the case rather than complicating it. We RECEIVE on the good path and TRANSMIT on the bad one. The reciprocity argument now applies at both ends, so transmitting via the house yagi should land at the garage with both antennas\' gain in the budget.\n\nA CORRECTION TO MY OWN EARLIER ADVICE, recorded so the config design does not inherit it: I wrote "do NOT declare bearings - they derive from positions we already hold, and a declared physical fact is the trap this project keeps hitting". That is right for the BEARING TO A TARGET, which is pure geometry and should always be derived. It is WRONG for an ANTENNA\'S MOUNTING BEARING, which is an installation fact that cannot be derived from anything and therefore MUST be declared. So:\n  derive:  bearing(house -> garage) = 121.2 deg\n  declare: garage yagi fixed at 310 deg; house yagi on a rotator, heading read live from ws:81\nThe distinction is derivable-vs-observed, not bearings-in-general.\n\nSTILL UNKNOWN, and it bounds the whole design: WHICH ANTENNA IS THE DEPLOYED RAK ACTUALLY CONNECTED TO - the garage yagi, or a local whip? Everything measured so far is the receive path INTO the house. What the deployed unit HEARS depends on its own antenna, and if it is not on that yagi then the return path has none of this gain. Ask before assuming symmetry.'}
