2026-07-27 19:41:21,868 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "B60 OPEN — 'message_status matches no request' logs a WARN roughly once a minute for packets we never sent, burying real delivery failures", 'description': "Observed 2026-07-27 18:12–18:40 UTC in pac-host logs, ~25 occurrences in 28 minutes:\n\n```\nWARN [mesh] [butler] butler: message_status delivered for packet 3160416710 matches no request\n```\n\nThe packet ids in these warnings do NOT appear in any ledger entry's sentIds — checked against\nrequest ms3kfnse.0, whose sentIds are [3540927512, 2577761749, 3033344706, 280145752, 513237257].\nSo these are delivery reports for traffic that is not ours: mesh-gw emits message_status for\npackets beyond the ones we POST, and the butler treats every unmatched one as anomalous.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS, and it is not the log volume. Interleaved in that same run is exactly one line\nthat matters:\n\n```\nWARN butler: delivery failed for !8cee336b: timeout: reply not received (try 1/5)\n             — next window 2026-07-27T18:34:49.595Z\n```\n\nA real, actionable delivery failure at the same severity as ~25 pieces of noise. This is the\nfailure mode the debug-module rewrite exists to stop — an instrument that reports so much that\nthe signal cannot be seen.\n\nRelevant to S1 ack-consumption, the highest-value open item: that task consumes message_status\ndeliberately, so it must first establish WHICH message_status events describe our sends. Two\nreadings, not yet distinguished:\n  (a) mesh-gw reports status for all gateway traffic and unmatched is normal — then this must not\n      be a WARN, and correlation is strictly by our own id set;\n  (b) our sentIds are incomplete and some sends are not recorded — then requests are under-tracked\n      and any ack-based evidence would be wrong.\n\nDo not guess between them: the ids exist on both sides. NOT fixed — found while measuring\nwake-phase-precision and outside its scope."}
