2026-07-27 19:51:22,949 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'B61 CONFIRMED/OPEN — a SECOND sender (node-dash) is running a traceroute sweep through both our radios, ~48 transmissions/hour, including at our own units', 'description': 'Found 2026-07-27 ~19:50 while chasing B60. Peter\'s prompt: "search ps aux and find out if there is a zombee process". No zombie of ours — exactly one pac-host (pid 231241). The second sender is a live, legitimate process from ANOTHER project.\n\nEVIDENCE, both sides:\n\n1. `ss -tnp` shows two clients established to mesh-gw :8001 —\n   - pid 231241  our pac-host\n   - pid 77423   /usr/share/pac/dev/projects/mt-radar/node-dash/src/index.js\n\n2. mesh-gw\'s own journal, last 60 minutes: **48** `POST /{radio}/traceroute` requests, issued through\n   BOTH radios (E9:B0:3F:17:27:91 omni and F4:12:FA:39:F7:B6 yagi), to ~20 distinct nodes:\n\n   ```\n   5 send_traceroute to=!aee62111\n   5 send_traceroute to=!8cee336b     <- OUR BENCH UNIT\n   4 send_traceroute to=!30327710\n   3 send_traceroute to=!932e30cd\n   ...\n   ```\n\n3. `grep -rn traceroute` over mesh/ host/ debug/ node/ trial-logger/ returns NOTHING. We never\n   send one. Peter confirms he sent "one ping earlier" — one packet, not 48.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS. This is not log noise, it is airtime.\n\n- SF11 gives roughly 1.6 s airtime per frame on a shared channel.\n- Our device is deaf ~99% of the time: 8 s of listening per 60 s on the bench, per 900 s in the field.\n- A Meshtastic message lives ~31 s and there is no store-and-forward anywhere.\n\nSo a second sender injecting ~1 transmission/minute through both radios competes directly with the\nwindow the butler exists to hit, and it does so with no knowledge of our wake schedule. Directly\nobserved the same evening: a device RX window opened for its full 8 s and logged `rx=0` while five\nof our transmissions were outstanding (serial: `LC rxopen +1112ms win=8000` -> `LC rxdone +9112ms rx=0`).\nNOT yet proven to be the cause of that miss — contention is a candidate, not a conclusion.\n\nAlso worth testing against the UNEXPLAINED 2026-07-26 GARG event (15 transmissions over 45 minutes,\nzero replies, telemetry still arriving). Contention from a second sender is a hypothesis that was\nnot available when that was investigated. Do not assert it — check whether the sweep was running then.\n\nEXPLAINS B60: the unmatched `message_status` warnings are these traceroutes and any other\nnode-dash traffic. Reading (a) is correct, (b) is not — our sentIds are not incomplete. B60\'s fix is\ntherefore about severity and correlation, not about under-tracking.\n\nNOT OURS TO FIX. node-dash is a separate project and is never edited from here. Raise it with that\nsession: does the sweep need to run continuously, can it be paused during a deployment, and can it\nbe kept off the units we own. Decision for Peter — a deployment is out and this changes the airtime\nbudget it is running in.'}
2026-07-27 19:51:50,535 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'B61a — the traceroute sweep is CONSTANT (35-50/hr for 2+ days), so it does NOT explain the 2026-07-26 GARG silence', 'description': 'Checked immediately after raising B61, against the temptation to adopt a convenient explanation.\n\nmesh-gw journal, traceroute POSTs per hour from 2026-07-26 14:00 to 2026-07-27 19:00 — every single\nhour, without a gap:\n\n```\nJul 26 14:00  40      Jul 27 00:00  46      Jul 27 10:00  45\nJul 26 15:00  38      Jul 27 01:00  47      Jul 27 11:00  44\nJul 26 16:00  40      Jul 27 02:00  50      Jul 27 12:00  48\nJul 26 17:00  44      ...                   ...\nJul 26 22:00  48      Jul 27 06:00  48      Jul 27 18:00  46\nJul 26 23:00  50      Jul 27 08:00  44      Jul 27 19:00  40\n```\n\nThe rate is flat: no spike, no onset, no gap. It was the same before, during and after the GARG\nevent on 2026-07-26 (15 transmissions over 45 minutes, zero replies, telemetry still arriving).\n\nCONCLUSION, stated so it is not later misremembered: a CONSTANT background load cannot explain a\nSUDDEN onset of silence. B61 is a standing airtime cost that degrades every window we try to hit;\nit is NOT the cause of the 07-26 event, and that event remains unexplained. The hypothesis raised\nin B61 is hereby tested and rejected for that specific incident.\n\nWhat the flat rate does establish: every delivery measurement this project has ever taken — the\nfirst-attempt rates, the retry counts, the wake-preempt value numbers — was taken WITH this load\npresent. So it is part of the baseline, not a new variable, and removing it would improve results\nrather than invalidate them.'}
