2026-07-27 20:18:00,082 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'B62 CONFIRMED/OPEN — the DIRECT house<->garage path is at the demodulation floor since deployment; we reach GARG almost entirely via a third-party relay we do not control', 'description': 'Measured 2026-07-27 20:17 from the recorder CSV, GARG (!987ab80f) frames received AFTER deployment (>= 17:00 BST). Raised because Peter said "the last data shows the GARG as direct and with a reasonably strong signal" — the data says the opposite, and the two properties belong to DIFFERENT frames.\n\n```\nDIRECT   n=16   rssi median -128   best -127   snr median -19.25   best -17.00\nrelayed  n=61   rssi median -116   best  -31   snr median  -6.75   best  +6.75\n```\n\nSF11\'s demodulation floor is about -17.5 dB SNR. **15 of the 16 direct frames are below it.**\nThe best direct SNR all evening is -17.00, i.e. exactly at the floor.\n\nThe single most recent wake (19:02 UTC) shows the conflation plainly — four frames, one wake:\n\n```\n19:02:17  omni  DIRECT   rssi -128  snr -20.25  hop 3/3\n19:02:23  omni  relayed  rssi -120  snr -9.75   hop 2/3\n19:02:23  yagi  relayed  rssi -116  snr -6.75   hop 2/3\n19:02:25  yagi  relayed  rssi -110  snr -0.75   hop 1/3\n```\n\nThe DIRECT copy is the WEAKEST of the four. The strong one is relayed with two hops consumed. Any\nview that takes best-RSSI across copies and hop-count from another copy will report "direct, good\nsignal", which is true of no single frame. The YAGI did not hear the direct frame at all.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS, beyond signal reporting:\n\n1. Delivery to the deployed unit currently depends on a mesh node we neither own nor control. If it\n   goes away, or moves, or its owner reconfigures it, we lose the garage with no warning and no\n   diagnostic that would distinguish that from a device fault.\n2. Every relayed arrival carries the relay\'s airtime and processing, so the wake-phase estimate for\n   the field unit is biased by a path we cannot characterise. This is why the relay exclusion was\n   replaced by a trimmed-minimum discriminator rather than a hard gate — with 79% relayed, the gate\n   left the unit at phaseSamples:0 (wake-phase-precision §4).\n3. It is consistent with the ~19 dB asymmetry measured earlier: the unit hears us at -128/-17.5 on\n   its own pong while our yagi hears it at -106/+1.5, and we transmit on the OMNI.\n\nCANDIDATE for the UNEXPLAINED 2026-07-26 GARG event (15 transmissions over 45 min, zero replies,\ntelemetry still arriving). Unlike the traceroute sweep — which B61a rejected because it is a flat\nconstant — a marginal link fails INTERMITTENTLY by nature, and telemetry continuing to arrive via\nrelay while our directed sends fail is exactly the signature. NOT asserted: it needs the same test,\nwhich is whether the direct-path SNR on 07-26 collapsed during that window.\n\nACTIONS, none taken from here:\n- Feeds `radio-selection` directly: transmitting via the YAGI rather than the OMNI is the obvious\n  lever, and the yagi already hears relayed copies 6 dB better than the omni.\n- Physical: garage yagi bearing (fixed 310 deg, SWR 1.2), house yagi rotator alignment, and whether\n  the deployed unit\'s antenna survived the swap intact. FOR PETER — this may be a site visit, and it\n  is a hardware question, not a services one.'}
