2026-07-27 20:48:26,432 70497 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'V1 legacy traceroute dispatch paths still live behind FF.SSOT_TRACEROUTE in two files — duplicate logic that can diverge', 'description': '**Status:** CONFIRMED · **Found during:** traceroute-manual-enable Phase 1 (2026-07-27)\n\nTwo files still carry a complete V1 dispatch path alongside the V2 one, selected at runtime by\nthe `FF.SSOT_TRACEROUTE` feature flag:\n\n- `src/passive-tracer.js:114-129` — `if (!FF.SSOT_TRACEROUTE) { bridge.post(...) ... }` with its\n  own timeout handling and its own `traced` emit\n- `src/lifecycle.js:65-72` — `if (!FF.SSOT_TRACEROUTE) { ... bridge.post(\\`/${sender}/traceroute\\`) }`\n  with its own hand-rolled cooldown (`rotator._lastTracedNum` / `_lastTracedAt`)\n\nBoth are marked in-code `[V1] LEGACY — remove when SSOT_TRACEROUTE verified`.\n\n**Why it is a defect and not just tidy-up:** they bypass `traceroute.dispatch()` entirely,\nposting to the bridge directly. Any invariant added at the dispatch chokepoint — cooldown,\nattribution, and now the `traceroute.enabled` gate — silently does not apply to them. That is\nthe same class of failure as `mesh-send.js` claiming to be the only send path while\n`imap-receiver.js:117` and `op-manager.js:180` posted around it.\n\n**Currently dormant:** `FF.SSOT_TRACEROUTE` is `true` (`src/feature-flags.js:23`), so the V1\nbranches do not execute today. The risk is that flipping the flag, or copying one of these\nblocks, reintroduces an ungated dispatcher.\n\n**Not fixed here** — out of scope for traceroute-manual-enable, which only adds the gate.\nRemoving them needs its own task and a decision on whether the flag is still wanted at all.'}
