2026-07-27 20:50:25,535 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "ROTATOR LOCK — Peter's design, agreed 2026-07-27: arbitration goes in the rotator, not in node-dash or us", 'description': 'Peter, 2026-07-27, unprompted and in parallel with the B62 finding: "node-dash has lots of modes, several make exclusive use of the yagi/pointer pretty much continuously... my solution is at the rotator. it already has a busy response... but if I add a hold time (ms) then it could simply emit busy to node dash without any node dash changes at all and would allow us to own a lock period."\n\nTHE DESIGN (his, and it is the right shape): arbitration lives at the CONTENDED RESOURCE. node-dash needs no change and cannot opt out; it simply sees the existing `busy` response and waits. A hold time means the lock expires on its own, so a crashed client cannot park the antenna.\n\nNOT OURS TO BUILD — the rotator is ESP32 firmware (192.168.10.186, ws on :81). What follows is the\nSERVICES side, which is ours, and the constraints we raised and he agreed to.\n\nAGREED, all four:\n\n1. POINT AND HOLD AS ONE OPERATION, not merely "don\'t move". A lock that only blocks others still\n   leaves the yagi wherever node-dash last aimed it, so we would own an antenna pointing at\n   somebody else\'s node. Request is goto-bearing-122-and-hold.\n\n2. SLEW IS FREE — settled. Peter: "antennae slew is VERY fast 60 RPM". That is 360 deg/s, so a\n   worst-case 180 deg slew is ~0.5 s. It therefore does NOT enter the timing budget, and the lock\n   can be acquired 1-2 s before the window rather than tens of seconds. Comfortably inside our\n   measured 18 ms wake-prediction accuracy. Recorded because the original concern was that slew\n   time would have to be added to wakeLeadMs — it does not.\n\n3. THE ROTATOR ENFORCES EXPIRY, we do not promise to release. pac-host restarted five times during\n   this evening\'s work alone; a hold that depends on our cleanup would eventually strand the yagi.\n\n4. THE ACQUIRE MUST TELL US WHETHER WE GOT IT. Without that we cannot distinguish "yagi is ours,\n   pointed at the garage" from "yagi is busy, pointing elsewhere", and would transmit into a\n   mispointed antenna believing it was aimed. On failure we fall back to the OMNI rather than not\n   sending.\n\nSIZING, agreed: hold 15-30 s (variable), a few times per 900 s beat. Covers pre-send, the device\'s\n8 s RX window, and its reply. Peter: "not a problem at all and it wont affect node-dash it will\njust wait until it is free."\n\nWHY IT MATTERS NOW — B62. Since deployment, 15 of 16 DIRECT frames from GARG are below SF11\'s\ndemodulation floor (median -128 dBm / -19.25 dB SNR) and we reach it almost entirely via a relay we\ndo not own. The yagi already hears its relayed copies ~6 dB better than the omni, and we currently\ntransmit on the OMNI. So this lock is what makes "transmit to the garage on the yagi" possible at\nall, given node-dash otherwise owns that antenna continuously.\n\nBLOCKED ON: the rotator firmware gaining hold-time. Nothing to build here until it exists, but the\nservices side is then small — acquire, verify, send inside the window, let it expire.'}
