2026-07-27 22:07:37,265 70497 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PARKED, opened 2026-07-27 at Peter\'s instruction: "neither the services nor the docs expose the full command dictionary."\n\nThere is no authoritative list of what a PAC device actually accepts. Three surfaces, all incomplete:\n\n1. node-dash — `public/app-control.js` hardcodes CONTROL_SHORTCUTS = [\'ping\',\'status\',\'config\',\'reboot\']. Four verbs, chosen by hand.\n2. services (pac-host) — `GET /v1/mesh/verbs` was agreed with us and is OWED, but NOT BUILT (xsession [mesh-verbs], carried over from mt-transport at its retirement). Their stated blocker: the device\'s own `cmds` help string omits whole families (cam/chunk/push/camu).\n3. docs — `docs/mt-transport/API.md` and `config-schema.json` describe the CONFIG schema, which is a different surface from commands and does not enumerate verbs.\n\nPROVEN CONCRETELY, 2026-07-27: `hop` exists in firmware (`mt-alarm/device/src/main.cpp:2972`, `hop <0-7>`, RAM-only, 0 = override off) and appears in NONE of the three. I told Peter the capability did not exist; he corrected me and was right. That is the cost of having no dictionary — the dashboard can only offer what someone remembered to hardcode, and an operator has no way to discover the rest.\n\nNot urgent: `queueCommand` is an unvalidated passthrough (`src/pac-host.js`), so anything the device understands can already be sent by hand. This is about discoverability and about the Control page telling the truth, not about capability.'}
