2026-07-28 07:22:04,507 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'nextwake-current-cycle', 'title': '_nextWake can return the wake already in progress, so one prediction in 33 is a full beat late', 'steps': ['Investigate: _nextWake (mesh/index.js:694-729) roll-forward `while (t < now) t += beat`, how the debug module captures nextWake as the prediction for the FOLLOWING wake, and every caller of nextWake (butler cue, devices route, mode route). Establish whether the one-beat error is a boundary in the roll or something else.', 'Document: spec with the exact before/after, and state which adjacent files are deliberately NOT touched.', 'Implement: roll past the wake currently in progress, not merely past now.', 'Observe: static (the line), functional (a prediction taken AT a wake instant is the next wake, not the current one), regression (butler still gets a usable cue; bench prediction unchanged).', 'Track: hash, validate, commit, and record the overnight baseline this is measured against (33 wakes, median 68 ms, one 901129 ms outlier).']}
2026-07-28 07:22:13,432 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Stop _nextWake returning the wake that is already in progress.\n\nOvernight 2026-07-27 23:02 -> 2026-07-28 07:02, GARG: 33 wakes received of 33 expected, median |err| 68 ms, 26 of 33 within 1 s — and ONE error of 901129 ms, almost exactly one beat (900000). We heard every wake, so it is not a missed observation: the prediction stored at the previous wake pointed at that same wake rather than the next one.\n\nSuccess is a NUMBER: zero one-beat errors across a night of >=30 wakes, with the median unchanged. A one-beat error is not a near miss — it aims a command at a window that has already closed, costing a full 15-minute cycle on the deployed unit.'}
2026-07-28 07:22:19,047 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'The roll-forward guards against the past but not against the present:\n\n    let t = anchor + n.beat;          // anchor = the HELD phase\n    const now = Date.now();\n    while (t < now) t += n.beat;      // mesh/index.js:723-727\n\nAt a wake instant, `anchor + beat` lands on the wake happening right now. If that value is even 1 ms in the future — which happens whenever this wake\'s frame arrives slightly EARLIER than the held phase predicted — the loop does not roll, and the wake in progress is returned as the next one.\n\nReachable only since the phase became HELD (wake-phase-precision 3c): a drifting phase moved the anchor each cycle and masked it.\n\nThe fix rolls past the wake currently in progress rather than merely past `now`, using the declared window (mode.windowMs) as the width of "in progress". No new declared value.\n\nPhase 1 must also confirm this is the only consumer path that matters: the butler takes nextWake as its send cue, and the debug module stores it as the prediction it will score the FOLLOWING wake against. A change here moves both.'}
2026-07-28 07:23:46,458 175346 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B63 CONFIRMED — the pre-empt path fires DURING an open window, so a "pre-wake" send can actually be an in-window send', 'description': 'Found 2026-07-28 during nextwake-current-cycle Phase 1. Not fixed — outside that task\'s scope.\n\n`butler._windowImminent` (mesh/lib/butler.js:129-135) returns true when\n\n    at - now <= wakeLeadMs && at - now > -wakeLeadMs\n\nand `at` comes from `_nextWake`, which under B-nextwake-current-cycle can return the wake\nALREADY IN PROGRESS (a few ms in the future). So during an open window `at - now` is ~0, the\ntest passes, and `_onWakeTick` logs\n\n    butler: <unit> — window imminent in <n> ms, sending EARLY rather than waiting to hear it\n\nfor a window that is not imminent but already open. The unit\'s own `heard` event also fires\n`onHeard -> _deliverNext(unit,\'window\')` for the same window.\n\nWHY IT IS PROBABLY BENIGN, and why that is not the same as harmless:\n`_onWakeTick` skips a unit already in `this.inflight` (butler.js:111), so the two paths cannot\ntransmit concurrently for the same unit — one wins and the other is skipped. No duplicate\ntransmission has been observed.\n\nWHAT IS WRONG ANYWAY: the log line asserts a cause it has not established, exactly the class of\ndefect B53 was raised for. "sending EARLY" describes a pre-emption that did not happen, which is\nmisleading in precisely the logs used to judge whether pre-emption works at all — i.e. it\ncorrupts the evidence for wake-preempt-value.\n\nNOT VERIFIED: whether any observed "pre-wake" fire in the historical logs was in fact an\nin-window send. That needs the log line correlated against the wake anchor, and would change how\nthe existing pre-empt counts are read.'}
