2026-07-28 08:48:57,901 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'B65 CONFIRMED — our OWN two radios relay each other, so every per-radio signal statistic I produced was contaminated by desk-range frames', 'description': 'Found by Peter 2026-07-28: "we accept relays from OMNI -> YAGI and YAGI -> OMNI and that corrupts signal and hop counts". Correct, and it invalidates figures I wrote into a spec, a config comment and two commit messages.\n\nMECHANISM. Both our gateway radios are ordinary mesh nodes, so each rebroadcasts what it hears.\nA frame GARG sends can reach us three ways: directly, via a third-party relay, or via OUR OTHER\nRADIO. The third case records the signal of a path across the room, not to the garage.\n\n`relay_node` is the low byte of the relaying node\'s number, which identifies them:\n    omni  !2687afb1 -> 0xB1 = 177\n    yagi  !fa39f7b4 -> 0xB4 = 180\n\nMEASURED, GARG frames since deployment (n=234):\n\n    DIRECT                          82  (35%)   rssi -115   snr  -6.00\n    relayed, third party (4)       110  (47%)   rssi -120   snr -11.50\n    relayed, third party (2)        14  ( 6%)   rssi -113   snr  -4.00\n    relayed, third party (40)        9  ( 4%)   rssi  -36   snr  +6.25\n    relayed by OUR OWN YAGI (180)   17  ( 7%)   rssi  -29   snr  +5.75\n    relayed by OUR OWN OMNI (177)    1  ( 0%)   rssi  -39   snr  +6.25\n\nOur own radios are 8% of frames and the STRONGEST by ~80 dB, so they drag any median upward.\n\nWHAT IS NOW KNOWN TO BE WRONG:\n  - "the yagi is 11 dB better than the omni on the relayed path (-111/-2.5 vs -119/-13.5)" —\n    written into docs/history/radio-selection.md, host/host.config.json and commit d870054.\n    Contaminated. WITHDRAWN pending a recomputation that excludes relay in {177,180}.\n  - "we reach GARG almost entirely via a relay we do not own" (B62) — it is 35% DIRECT, not 21%.\n    The 21% came from a small early sample.\n  - "best relayed -31 dBm" quoted as evidence the yagi hears well — that was our own yagi\'s\n    rebroadcast measured across the room.\n\nWHAT SURVIVES: the DIRECT figures, which carry no relay by definition. Direct is now\n-115 dBm / -6.0 dB SNR, i.e. 11 dB ABOVE the SF11 demodulation floor — a usable link a third of\nthe time, and materially better than the "-128 / -19.25, permanently below the floor" I reported.\nThe free-space deficit is ~52 dB rather than 65, so a physical fault or obstruction is still\nindicated, but less catastrophically than stated.\n\nFIX: every per-radio statistic must exclude frames whose relay_node is one of ours, and should\nstate the direct/relayed split separately rather than reporting a blended median. Feeds\nradio-selection, which currently chooses a transmit antenna using exactly these corrupted numbers.\n\nNOT VERIFIED: whether our two radios also relay OUR OWN outbound commands to each other, which\nwould inflate ack/delivery evidence the same way. Same check, on the outbound side.'}
2026-07-28 08:54:08,414 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'self-relay-exclusion', 'title': 'B65: our own radios relay each other, and nothing marks it — tag self-relayed frames at the gateway boundary', 'steps': ['Investigate: how relay_node is carried (low byte of the relaying node num), where our own radio identities are already known (mesh-gw /devices -> _radioAddrs), and every consumer that could be misled by a self-relayed frame — model.heard phase samples, the recorder CSV, any per-radio statistic.', 'Document: spec with the exact derivation, what is excluded where, and what is deliberately left alone.', 'Implement: derive selfRelay at the gateway boundary so no downstream consumer has to remember, and keep self-relayed frames out of the phase estimate.', 'Observe: static, functional (a synthetic self-relayed frame is flagged and excluded; the same frame from a third party is not), regression (direct and third-party relayed frames unaffected).', 'Track: correct the withdrawn figures in radio-selection.md and host.config.json that were computed from contaminated medians.']}
