2026-07-28 09:07:52,761 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'fw-surface-parity', 'title': 'Everything in the firmware is in the service: all 30 verbs and all 21 config fields, or an explicit recorded reason why not', 'steps': ['Investigate: build the INVENTORY that has never existed. For each of the 30 firmware verbs and 21 config fields: its arguments and sub-verbs, its reply shape, whether services can send it, whether services PARSES the reply, and whether any consumer can discover it. The firmware command table in src/main.cpp is the source of truth, not memory and not this list.', "Decide the shape of the exposure. A verb is not the same as a config field: a field has read/write/confirm, a verb has arguments and a reply to parse. Both must be discoverable by a consumer, which today only the config schema is. Contract change -> needs node-dash's ack.", 'Implement: every verb reachable and its reply parsed; every writable field mapped with a confirm predicate. Anything deliberately NOT exposed is recorded with the reason, not silently absent.', 'Observe: a round-trip against the bench for every verb and field, showing the reply we parsed. DEFERRED entries state why (e.g. reboot, wedge) rather than being skipped quietly.', 'Track: docs/api/http.md tells the truth about the whole surface; announce to node-dash; close config-map-completeness as superseded by this.']}
2026-07-28 09:08:06,120 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-07-28: "makes no diff if they are configs or not, they should be in the service, everything that is in the fw should be in the service."\n\nThat is the rule. One surface, no distinction between a config field and a verb.\n\nTHE FIRMWARE ACCEPTS 30 VERBS (from the command table in device/src/main.cpp, not from memory):\n\n  agc alarm cam camu chunk config debug detect echo env help hop interval lname\n  name nodes ping push reboot sch schema simenv sleepdbg sleepfor sleepmode\n  status telemetry txp wedge window\n\nAND 21 CONFIG FIELDS, of which services can write 6:\n\n  writable now : agc.on agc.sec chunk.gap chunk.hop name lname\n  ENOMAP       : beat slp mute push.auto det.n det.win alm.on alm.ovr alm.und\n                 alm.hum alm.ren tele.chg tele.ka rx.win\n  read-only    : txp (writable:false in firmware)\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND TIDINESS. Verbs that exist and were unknown to me have cost real time this\nweek: `hop` was found by grepping firmware mid-investigation after I had told Peter three times we\ncould not control hop count; `txp` was found the same way while trying to answer whether the remote\nwas attenuated. Both were sitting in the command table. A surface nobody has enumerated is a\nsurface that gets rediscovered one emergency at a time.\n\nSUCCESS IS A COUNT, stated before the work: every one of the 30 verbs and 21 fields is either\nreachable from the service AND its reply parsed, or carries a recorded reason why not. Today:\n6 of 21 fields writable, and no verb inventory exists at all.'}
2026-07-28 09:08:13,687 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': '1. INVENTORY FIRST, because it has never existed. For each verb and field: arguments and sub-verbs, reply shape, whether we can send it, whether we PARSE the reply, whether a consumer can discover it. Source of truth is the firmware command table; the 30-verb list in the goal is a starting point to be verified, not trusted.\n\n2. Three distinct gaps will fall out, and they need different fixes:\n   (a) a verb/field with a working firmware command that services simply never mapped — pure wiring, ours alone;\n   (b) one we can send but whose reply we discard — the "decoded and never handled" class this project keeps paying for (a gateway event decoded and never handled, an ACK never requested, a field promised and populated by nothing);\n   (c) one the firmware does not implement — a device-session request, and the ONLY category that is not ours.\n\n3. Discoverability is part of the deliverable, not a nicety. Today only the config schema is discoverable; the 30 verbs are not, so every consumer (and every future session) rediscovers them by reading firmware. Whatever shape it takes, it is a docs/api/http.md contract change and needs node-dash\'s ack before it binds.\n\n4. Some verbs must NOT be casually exercised and that is recorded rather than skipped: `reboot` and `wedge` on a deployed unit, `txp` (refused on a deployed role by firmware), `sleepmode` (can make a unit uncommandable). Deferred with a reason is acceptable; silently absent is not.\n\n5. Supersedes config-map-completeness, which covered only the 21 config fields. Close that one referencing this.'}
2026-07-28 09:12:53,134 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'THE DEFECT IS THE MISSING SSOT, NOT THE MISSING WIRING.\n\nPeter, 2026-07-28: "this is a problem I have stated NUMEROUS times in previous sessions. there is no SSOT for commands/verbs, etc etc, basically the API, if there was this issue would have been picked up and fixed ages ago. it now relies on MY memory to repeatedly keep bringing it up."\n\nHe is right, and the repetition IS the evidence. A gap that only surfaces when a human remembers it will keep costing what it has already cost: `hop` was found by grepping firmware after I had told him three times we could not control hop count; `txp` the same way while answering whether the remote was attenuated. Both were in the command table the whole time.\n\nWHAT EXISTS ALREADY, and proves the pattern works:\n  device/tools/gen_schema.py generates docs/config-schema.json from the CONFIG_FIELDS table,\n  wired as a pre-build step (platformio.ini:116). Its own docstring: "A file nothing forces to\n  keep up will rot. Generating it on every build makes drift impossible... one source of truth."\n\nTWO GAPS:\n  1. The generator covers the 21 CONFIG FIELDS and was never extended to the 30 VERBS.\n  2. NOTHING ON THE SERVICES SIDE CHECKS IT. `grep -l config-schema.json mesh/test/*.js` returns\n     nothing, so even for the fields it does cover, divergence is invisible. That is the whole\n     reason this falls to Peter\'s memory: there is no failing test to notice.\n\nSUCCESS IS A TEST THAT FAILS, not a count of wired verbs. When firmware gains a verb or field, a\nservices suite must go red until it is either handled or exempted WITH A RECORDED REASON. The\ncurrent 51-item backlog (30 verbs + 21 fields, 6 wired) is then just the first run of that test.\n\nMeasured today: 15 of 21 fields unwritable (71%), 4 of 29 reply types parsed (86% discarded),\n0 of 30 verbs discoverable by any consumer.'}
