2026-07-28 09:24:28,001 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "B66 CONFIRMED — three config fields are advertised by the firmware's own schema but NO verb can set them: det.n, mute, push.auto", 'description': 'Found 2026-07-28 during fw-surface-parity Phase 1, by reading each handler in device/src/main.cpp rather than inferring from verb names. NOT OURS TO FIX — this is a device-session request.\n\nThese three appear in CONFIG_FIELDS, so they are published in the generated config-schema.json, rendered by node-dash\'s config form, and reported as `writable: true`. Nothing can write them:\n\n  det.n      "Detect count", 1..10, writable:true\n             The detect handler reads ONLY a hold time. Its own comment says why:\n                 uint32_t t = strtoul(cmd + 6, nullptr, 10); // hold seconds (edge-count N dropped)\n             The edge-count argument was removed; the FIELD was left advertised.\n\n  mute       "Powerup mute", 0..3600, writable:true\n             g_muteS (main.cpp:119) is written only by loadSettings() (1150, 1187, 1214, 1233,\n             1250) — i.e. restored from persisted settings. There is no command path at all.\n\n  push.auto  "Auto upload", boolean, writable:true\n             `push` has sub-verbs (pub and others) but none writes an auto flag.\n\nWHY THIS IS WORSE THAN ENOMAP, and why it is recorded separately: an ENOMAP field is one WE have\nnot wired — our defect, fixable here. These three cannot be set by any means available to anyone:\nnot by services, not by the CLI, not by a person with a serial console. The device advertises a\ncontrol that does not exist. That is the "field promised in the API and populated by nothing" class\nthe project charter names as one of its expensive defects, and here the promise is made by the\nFIRMWARE\'s own generated schema.\n\nTHREE POSSIBLE DISPOSITIONS, for the device session to choose:\n  1. add the missing verb (restore the detect count argument; add mute and push auto setters);\n  2. mark the fields writable:false in CONFIG_FIELDS, as txp already is — honest, and the\n     generated schema then tells the truth with no service change;\n  3. remove the fields if the settings they name are genuinely dead.\n\nOption 2 is the cheapest honest fix and needs no new command surface.\n\nVERIFIED: by reading the handlers and by grepping every writer of g_muteS. NOT VERIFIED: whether\nthe underlying settings are still USED by the firmware (g_muteS clearly is — main.cpp:2286 gates\nthe PIR startup lockout on it — so `mute` is a live setting with no way to change it, which is the\nworst of the three).'}
