2026-07-28 09:37:06,088 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'B67 CONFIRMED — POST /v1/mesh/config/:target sends DIRECT with a 30 s timeout, so a config write to a sleeping unit fails by design', 'description': 'Found 2026-07-28 during config-field-wiring Phase 4, while trying to prove a newly-wired field end to end.\n\nMEASURED, twice, against the bench (!8cee336b, beat 60 s, sleep on):\n\n  POST /v1/mesh/config/!8cee336b {"rx.win":8}\n  -> {"error":"unit did not answer","detail":"the unit is asleep or out of range; it answers\n      only in its wake window"}     after 30.2 s\n\nRepeated with the request fired deliberately 1 s INSIDE the predicted wake window: same result.\n\nMECHANISM. The Config domain is constructed with a DIRECT command function\n(mesh/index.js: `command: (node, verb, args) => this.command(node, verb, args, this._idem())`),\nnot the butler. So `POST /v1/mesh/config/:target`:\n  - transmits once, immediately, whenever the caller happens to ask;\n  - waits `timing.replyTimeoutMs` (30 s) for an answer;\n  - has NO cross-window retry, which is the entire delivery guarantee for a sleeping unit.\n\nThe device is deaf ~99% of the time. So a config write to a sleeping unit succeeds only if the\ncaller happens to transmit inside an 8 s window — and even then a single attempt must not be lost.\nEvery other command path (POST /v1/mesh/command on a `live` unit) queues through the butler and\nretries across windows; config alone does not.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE NOW: 11 fields were just wired (6 -> 17 of 21). Wiring them makes them\nreachable in principle while this makes them unreliable in practice on exactly the unit that\nmatters — the deployed one at a 900 s beat, where a missed single attempt costs 15 minutes and the\nroute has already returned an error to the caller.\n\nCONFOUND on this measurement, stated so it is not read as stronger than it is: the gateway is\ncurrently the YAGI, aimed at the garage, while the bench is on the desk. A bench command over the\nyagi took 2 tries / 5 sends earlier today, so a single-shot direct send failing is expected\nindependent of this defect. The DESIGN issue is confirmed by reading the wiring; the two failed\nround trips are consistent with it but do not isolate it.\n\nFIX (not done here, outside config-field-wiring\'s scope): route config writes through the butler\nlike every other command, so they queue for the window and retry across windows. That changes the\nroute from synchronous to queued, which is an http.md contract change and needs node-dash\'s ack.'}
2026-07-28 09:37:32,605 242420 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': "B2 CONFIRMED — amqtt.h stores dead Commands& reference\nfile: include/amqtt.h:20 and :150\nAMQTTClient constructor takes `Commands& console` and stores it as a member, but the member is never read or called anywhere in amqtt.h. It is dead storage — a leftover from an older design before command dispatch moved to main_mqtt.cpp's onCmd(). The Commands& reference keeps the Commands object alive unnecessarily via a constructor arg.\nEvidence: grep of amqtt.h shows two occurrences (constructor param and member declaration) and zero call sites.\nFound during: typed-dispatch investigation, 2026-07-28."}
