2026-07-28 09:44:27,248 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'B63 CORRECTED — I recorded it CONFIRMED on reasoning; the measurement does not support the in-window claim', 'description': 'Raised 2026-07-28 09:43 against my own entry from this morning, under the rule that a finding must not be stated more strongly than its evidence.\n\nWHAT I CLAIMED (step 45): `_windowImminent` returns true during an OPEN window because `at - now`\nis ~0, so `_onWakeTick` logs "sending EARLY" for a window that is already open.\n\nWHAT THE LOG ACTUALLY SHOWS — every "window imminent" line in the last 400:\n\n  22:17:08  !987ab80f — window imminent in 8730 ms, sending EARLY\n  22:32:08  !987ab80f — window imminent in 9531 ms, sending EARLY\n  22:47:09  !987ab80f — window imminent in 8480 ms, sending EARLY\n\n8.5-9.5 s of lead against wakeLeadMs=10000. That is pre-emption working exactly as designed —\ntransmitting just before the window opens — NOT firing inside one. `at - now ~ 0` does not appear\nin any sample.\n\nSTATUS: the mechanism I described is REACHABLE in principle (the imminent test does admit\n`at - now` near zero, and _nextWake could return the in-progress wake), but the defect it was\nsupposed to cause is NOT OBSERVED. And the cause has since been removed anyway:\nnextwake-current-cycle (commit 6e47034, 2026-07-28) makes _nextWake roll past the window in\nprogress, so `at` can no longer be ~0 at a wake instant.\n\nDOWNGRADED to UNVERIFIED. Not deleted — the imminent test\'s lower bound (`at - now > -wakeLeadMs`)\nstill admits a window that opened up to 10 s ago, so the log line could still describe an\nalready-open window in some ordering. What is missing is any sample of it happening.\n\nTO SETTLE IT: after the next successful pre-empt to a unit whose queue is not failing, compare the\n"window imminent in N ms" value against the wake anchor recorded for that same cycle. If N is\nconsistently near wakeLeadMs, close this. All current samples predate 6e47034, so the post-fix\nbehaviour is genuinely unmeasured rather than fine.'}
