2026-07-28 09:45:53,317 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'instrument-truthfulness', 'title': 'B64 + B60: the instrument hides a number it has, and shouts about traffic that is not ours', 'steps': ["Investigate: confirm wakesDirect is maintained but unpublished, and that the unmatched message_status warn fires for other gateway clients' packets rather than ours. DONE in the ledger sweep.", 'Document: spec both changes with exact before/after.', 'Implement: publish wakesDirect alongside phaseSamples; downgrade the unmatched-status log to debug when the packet id was never one of ours, keeping WARN for an id we DID send whose row is gone.', 'Observe: static, functional (a synthetic status for an id we never sent does not warn; one for an id we did sent still does), regression.', 'Track: hash, validate, close B64 and B60 in the ledger with the evidence.']}
2026-07-28 09:46:07,967 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Two small, certain, service-side defects where the instrument misreports itself. Both were found today, both are ours alone, neither needs another party.\n\nB64 — `wakesDirect` is maintained in the model and published by nothing. It counts how many of the current phase window\'s samples were DIRECT, which is exactly what tells you whether a phase estimate is trustworthy or biased by relay delay. Verified live: GET /v1/mesh/devices returns no such key. The field was added in the very change whose comment says "a prediction whose input is invisible cannot be trusted or debugged".\n\nB60 — `butler: message_status <s> for packet <id> matches no request` logs at WARN roughly once a minute. B61 established why: node-dash is a second client on the same gateway running 35-50 traceroutes an hour, and mesh-gw reports status for ALL gateway traffic. So the overwhelming majority of these are other clients\' packets and entirely expected. Meanwhile a REAL failure — "delivery failed for !8cee336b: timeout (try 1/5)" — sits at the same severity, one line among ~25 pieces of noise.\n\nSuccess: wakesDirect readable from the API, and the unmatched-status log distinguishing "an id that was never ours" (expected, debug) from "an id we DID send whose row is gone" (real, warn).'}
2026-07-28 09:46:15,749 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': '1. mesh/index.js — publish `wakesDirect` beside `phaseSamples` in the devices route. One line plus the reason.\n\n2. mesh/lib/butler.js — the unmatched-status branch currently warns unconditionally. Split it:\n   - the packet id appears in NO entry\'s sentIds, current or settled -> another client\'s traffic on\n     a shared gateway. log.debug. This is normal and says nothing about us.\n   - the id was ours and the row has gone (pruned, or a bug) -> keep log.warn. That is the case the\n     original comment was written for ("either the row was pruned, or something is answering a\n     packet we have no record of sending") and it stays loud.\n\n   The discriminator has to be honest about what it can know: the butler only holds recent entries,\n   so "never ours" is really "not in anything we still hold". Stated in the comment rather than\n   implied, because a pruned row would then read as someone else\'s packet.\n\n3. Tests must show the split BITES: a status for an id we never sent must not warn; one for an id\n   we did send must. Assert on the logger, not on the absence of a crash.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE: B67 (config sends direct, needs node-dash ack), B66 (device-side), B63 (downgraded to\nUNVERIFIED — no sample of it happening, and its cause was removed by 6e47034).'}
