2026-07-28 10:09:26,898 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B67 RAISED TO PRIMARY — it is not "config lacks retry", it is that config and images BYPASS the single delivery path entirely', 'description': 'Raised 2026-07-28 10:05 after Peter: "well that is a PRIMARY bug isnt it?" and "why does every send NOT use the same method, that\'s the way it should be isnt it?"\n\nBoth correct, and my original entry under-rated it twice over.\n\nUNDER-RATING 1 — SEVERITY. I filed it as a reliability wrinkle on newly-wired fields. It is worse:\nthe project exists so that nobody has to drive to the garage to change a setting ("answers commands\nso nobody drives over to change a setting", project description). The deployed unit sleeps ~99% of\nthe time. A config write to it fails BY DESIGN. So the entire config surface — including the 11\nfields wired this morning — is unusable on the only unit that matters.\n\nUNDER-RATING 2 — FRAMING. I described the fix as "add retry to config". Peter identified the real\ndefect: there should be ONE send path and there are three.\n\n    dispatch()      POLICY    queue for the wake window, retry across windows, ledger\n      butler\n        command()   PRIMITIVE one transmission, wait for one reply\n          gw.sendText\n\nThe layering is CORRECT and is not the bug: index.js:141 is the butler calling command() to\ntransmit, exactly as intended.\n\nThe bug is the CALLERS that skip the policy layer:\n    index.js:120   Config  -> this.command(...)   one attempt, 30 s, no window awareness\n    index.js:94    Images  -> this.command(...)   same\n    index.js:956-7 ping/status helpers -> this.command(...)\n\nThese were wired before the butler became the delivery guarantee and never migrated. The wake-window\nlogic, the cross-window retry and the ledger all exist; those callers simply do not get them.\n\nONE DELIBERATE EXCEPTION, which stays: align sends via gw.sendText directly, documented — 4 pings\nper press is instrument traffic and would drown the outbox that exists to show a person what THEY\nsent. That is a reason; the other two are an accident.\n\nMEASURED, twice, against the bench (60 s beat, sleeping):\n    POST /v1/mesh/config/!8cee336b {"rx.win":8}\n    -> {"error":"unit did not answer"}   after 30.2 s\n  including one fired deliberately 1 s inside the predicted wake window.\n\nCONTRACT: I claimed this needs node-dash\'s ack because it turns the route from synchronous to\nqueued. That may be avoidable — dispatch already awaits settlement via _awaitSettled and returns\nthe reply when it lands in time, so the route can stay synchronous and simply become reliable.\nTo be established in the fix, not assumed.'}
2026-07-28 10:09:40,487 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'one-send-path', 'title': 'B67 PRIMARY: every send goes through the butler — config and images currently bypass it, so they fail on a sleeping unit by design', 'steps': ["Investigate: every caller of the direct command() primitive, what each needs back, and whether routing through dispatch() can keep each route's existing response shape. Establish whether this is a contract change at all — dispatch already awaits settlement, so the route may stay synchronous and simply become reliable.", 'Document: spec naming each caller, its new path, and the one documented exception (align).', 'Implement: config and images route through dispatch; only the butler calls command(). Align unchanged, with its reason restated.', "Observe: static (no caller left on the primitive except the butler and align), functional (a config write to a SLEEPING unit now succeeds across a wake window where it previously returned 'unit did not answer'), regression.", 'Track: hash, validate, close B67 with the measured before/after.']}
2026-07-28 10:11:05,816 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'STANDING BUG LEDGER. Append, never delete. An entry leaves only when it is fixed, or disproved WITH EVIDENCE.\n\nEVERY ENTRY CARRIES A SEVERITY IN ITS TITLE. Added 2026-07-28 after Peter: "this kind of bug should never be an \'oh by the way\' comment and glossed over, maybe we need to severitise bugs."\n\nHe is right, and the evidence is two mis-ratings in one day by me:\n  B49 "two configs"  filed as "small housekeeping"  -> silently invalidated a whole night\'s\n                      radio experiment; I reported a yagi switch that never happened.\n  B67 "config sends direct"  filed as an aside      -> the deployed unit cannot be configured at\n                      all, which is the thing the project exists to do.\nWithout a severity field, a project-stopping defect and a log-noise nit look identical in a list of\n50, and which is which depends on whoever wrote it last being in a good mood.\n\nTHE SCALE — judged by CONSEQUENCE, never by how hard it is to fix or how embarrassing it is:\n\n  P1 STOPS THE PRODUCT. The alarm cannot report, or the deployed unit cannot be commanded or\n     diagnosed remotely. Driving to the site becomes the only option. Fix before anything else.\n     Examples: B67 (config unusable on a sleeping unit).\n\n  P2 SILENTLY WRONG. It produces numbers, states or logs that are believable and false. Nobody is\n     told. Decisions get made on them.  THIS IS THE CLASS THAT HAS COST THIS PROJECT MOST — every\n     expensive defect here has been silent, not loud.\n     Examples: B49 (editing a dead config file that reports success), B65 (our own radios\' relays\n     in a 2.5 km link\'s statistics), B52 (a beat aliased 2x while err_ms scored well).\n\n  P3 DEGRADES. Real and visible: worse delivery, wasted airtime or battery, an avoidable retry.\n     It costs something measurable but nothing is lied about.\n\n  P4 FRICTION. Noise, missing observability, an unmapped field. Costs time, not correctness.\n     Examples: B60 (log noise burying a real failure), B64 (a field maintained and unpublished).\n\nRULES THAT GO WITH IT:\n  - A severity may be RAISED at any time with evidence, and raising one is announced, never quiet.\n    Under-rating a live defect once seen is the failure this exists to prevent.\n  - "Probably fine", "pre-existing" and "not related to this change" are not disposals.\n  - An UNVERIFIED finding is recorded as unverified, never as a hunch worth ignoring — and never\n    stated more strongly than its evidence (see B63, downgraded 2026-07-28 when the logs\n    contradicted the claim I had recorded as CONFIRMED).\n  - If it was found in passing and is out of scope: it still gets an entry. Reported in\n    conversation only is NOT reported — conversation is discarded and the next session starts blind.'}
2026-07-28 10:11:38,072 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'SEVERITY RETROFIT 2026-07-28 — assigning P1..P4 to the open entries, so the list stops needing my judgement to read', 'description': 'The scale is defined in this task\'s goal. Existing titles are not rewritten (append-only), so this is the index. New entries carry their severity in the title from here on.\n\nP1 — STOPS THE PRODUCT\n  B67  config writes send direct with no retry, so the DEPLOYED unit cannot be configured at all.\n       The project exists so nobody drives to the garage to change a setting. In progress:\n       task one-send-path.\n\nP2 — SILENTLY WRONG (the class that has cost this project most)\n  B49  two configs, one dead under pac-host — editing it reports success and does nothing. FIXED.\n  B65  our own radios relay each other; desk-range frames entered a 2.5 km link\'s statistics. FIXED.\n  B62  the direct path to the garage sits at the demodulation floor and we reach the unit mostly\n       via a relay we do not own — so "it works" is true and fragile at once. OPEN, physical.\n  B66  three config fields the FIRMWARE advertises as writable that no verb can set. `mute` is a\n       LIVE setting (gates the PIR startup lockout) that nothing can change. OPEN, device-side.\n  B54  err_ms is prediction-minus-observation, both ours, so a doubled schedule scores well.\n       OPEN — the metric cannot see its own aliasing.\n\nP3 — DEGRADES\n  B61  node-dash\'s traceroute sweep, 35-50/hour through both our radios, competing for airtime\n       with an 8 s window. OPEN, another project\'s to decide.\n  B15  image push has no give-up path. OPEN.\n\nP4 — FRICTION\n  B60  unmatched message_status warned once a minute, burying a real delivery failure. FIXED.\n  B64  wakesDirect maintained and published nowhere. FIXED.\n  B50  orphan requests.db. OPEN.\n\nUNVERIFIED — recorded, not rated, because rating implies a consequence we have not shown\n  B63  the pre-empt firing inside an open window. Downgraded today: the logs show 8.5-9.5 s leads,\n       which is correct pre-emption. Its cause was removed by 6e47034 anyway.\n\nNOT OURS\n  B11  dev/CLAUDE.md wrongly lists trial-logger as a separate service.\n  B19  config-schema default path pointed at the abandoned tree (superseded by the generated file).'}
