d — the rest\naccrue on their own. Criterion 4 (enabled=false / socket down) is covered by the code path\nreturning false but NOT by an assertion; worth adding.\n\nNOT MEASURED, deliberately: whether this improves command success. GARG\'s direct path has been\ngone since 07:17 (B68) and 0 of 28 sends landed today (B69), so any rate measured now describes\nthe link, not this change. One change per measurement.'}
2026-07-28 12:17:51,478 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B70 (NOT OURS, node-dash) — POST /rotator/move answers {"moving":true,"az":N} even when the rotator REFUSES the move', 'notes': 'Observed 2026-07-28 12:17:16 while verifying the wake hold (rotator-wake-hold, commit 3187a27).\n\nWith the rotator in a service-armed hold at az 121, a competing move was fired through\nnode-dash:\n\n  POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/rotator/move {"az":166}\n  ->  {"moving": true, "az": 166}\n\nThe rotator refused it. az stayed 121 for every one of the next 8 seconds, held=true throughout,\nand holdMs counted down normally. The reply is optimistic: node-dash returns `moving:true` on\nhaving SENT the command, not on the rotator having accepted it.\n\nsrc/rotator-api.js:69-80 does `rotator.move(az); res.json({moving:true, az})` with no wait for\nthe device\'s {evt:busy,held:true} or {evt:done}. Its two refusal checks are node-dash\'s OWN\n(dashMode===1, scanner.active) and both return 409 — so the API already has the right shape for\na refusal, it just does not report the ROTATOR\'s.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: any client that trusts the response believes it has repointed the antenna when\nit has not. Our own arming path is unaffected — mesh/lib/rotator.js talks to the device WS\ndirectly and resolves aim() on the device\'s own {evt:done}, never on an HTTP acknowledgement.\nThat was written that way for a different reason and happens to be immune.\n\nNOT OURS. node-dash is a separate project and is never edited from here. Raise it there.'}
