2026-07-28 12:18:01,643 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B70 (NOT OURS, node-dash) — POST /rotator/move answers {"moving":true,"az":N} even when the rotator REFUSES the move', 'notes': 'Observed 2026-07-28 12:17:16 while verifying the wake hold (rotator-wake-hold, commit 3187a27).\n\nWith the rotator in a service-armed hold at az 121, a competing move was fired through node-dash:\n\n  POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/rotator/move {"az":166}\n  ->  {"moving": true, "az": 166}\n\nThe rotator refused it. az stayed 121 for every one of the next 8 seconds, held=true throughout,\nholdMs counting down normally. The reply is optimistic: node-dash returns moving:true on having\nSENT the command, not on the rotator having accepted it.\n\nsrc/rotator-api.js:69-80 does `rotator.move(az); res.json({moving:true, az})` with no wait for the\ndevice\'s {evt:busy,held:true} or {evt:done}. Its two refusal checks are node-dash\'s OWN\n(dashMode===1, scanner.active) and both return 409 — so the API already has the right shape for a\nrefusal, it just does not report the ROTATOR\'s.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: any client trusting that response believes it repointed the antenna when it did\nnot. Our own arming path is unaffected — mesh/lib/rotator.js talks to the device WS directly and\nresolves aim() on the device\'s own {evt:done}, never on an HTTP acknowledgement. Written that way\nfor a different reason; immune by accident, not by design.\n\nNOT OURS. node-dash is a separate project and is never edited from here. Raise it there.'}
2026-07-28 12:27:22,623 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B71 CONFIRMED, HIGHEST-VALUE OF THE DAY (NOT OURS — node-dash/omni) — mast-tilt telemetry broadcasts every 3 s at hop 3 from a radio 5 m from the yagi, and it is very likely what has been killing the 2.5 km link', 'notes': 'Found 2026-07-28 while Peter asked why the omni\'s reception looked odd. Identified by Peter:\n"yes those are mast tilt data", and "the OMNI is now outside and probably 5 meters from the YAGI".\n\n## What the omni is actually doing\n\nFrom the recorder, today, from_id 2687afb1 (OMNI):\n\n  port          PRIVATE_APP\n  n             4559 packets today\n  to_id         ffffffff  — BROADCAST, every single one\n  channel       0\n  hop_limit     3         — so every neighbour rebroadcasts each one\n  median gap    3.0 s     (mean 7.0, min 1.0)\n  rate          ~500/hour, flat from 00:00 to 08:40\n\nThe YAGI sends NONE of these. Its own traffic is 65-70 packets/hour, almost all TELEMETRY_APP.\nSo this is one radio\'s behaviour, not a site-wide setting.\n\n## Why it matters at 5 m\n\nCALCULATED, not measured — flagged as such. SF11/BW250, ~30 byte payload, CR 4/5, no LDRO:\n58.25 symbols x 8.192 ms = ~0.48 s airtime per broadcast.\n\n  one every 3.0 s x 0.48 s  =  ~16% duty cycle from the omni ALONE\n  EU868 is normally 1%.\n\nAt 5 m and 868 MHz free-space loss is ~45 dB, so a +20 dBm omni puts roughly -25 dBm into the\nyagi before the yagi\'s own gain — against GARG at -115 dBm. That is ~90 dB above the signal we\nare trying to receive. While the omni transmits, the yagi is deaf.\n\nGARG\'s telemetry is ~1.6 s on air. P(overlap with an omni TX) ~ (1.6 + 0.48)/3.0 ~ 70%.\nThat is exactly the shape of what we observe: direct frames present but marginal, and\nintermittently absent altogether.\n\n## THE NATURAL EXPERIMENT — this is the evidence, not the arithmetic\n\nThe omni was off air 08:40-11:16 UTC for Peter\'s maintenance. Nobody was testing this.\n\n  hour   omni PRIVATE_APP   GARG DIRECT frames on yagi   GARG med SNR\n  06          521                    5                      -6.00\n  07          494                    2                      -6.50\n  08          383                    0                      -9.25\n  09            0                    0                      -9.50\n  10            0                    1   <- first direct since 07:17, at 10:47   -7.50\n  11           94                    2   <- omni back at 11:16; 11:17 rows relayed again  -6.75\n\n## What this supersedes\n\nB68 recorded "the deepest 2-3 dB of the yagi\'s reception went away at 06:00, cause unknown", and\nstep 59 withdrew my wrong bearing attribution. THIS is the candidate cause B68 lacked, and it fits\nevery feature of it: a co-sited transmitter censors the WEAKEST signals only, which is why the\nyagi\'s mean RSSI and total volume stayed flat while its <=-113 dBm bucket fell ~30%.\n\nB69 (0 of 28 commands delivered today) is the same story in the transmit direction.\n\nIt may also be the long-missing explanation for B62 — the link running ~52 dB below a two-yagi\nfree-space budget over 2.501 km.\n\nSTILL NOT PROVEN. The correlation is strong and the mechanism is sound, but the omni\'s downtime\nwas maintenance, not a controlled test, and the decline began ~06:00 while the omni went off at\n08:40. A clean test is free: the omni is back on air, so if this is the cause the direct fraction\nshould fall away again with nobody touching anything.\n\n## Disposition — NOT OURS\n\nnode-dash and the omni\'s own config own this; services is never edited from there and sends\nnothing on channel 0 and nothing at that rate. Raise it there. Points worth making:\n\n1. hop_limit 3 on LOCAL data is the worst part — mast tilt is a fact about a mast 5 m away and\n   does not need relaying by the whole region. hop 0 would confine it.\n2. node-dash already reaches the omni over BLE through mesh-gw. It is worth asking why the tilt\n   reading goes over LoRa at all rather than over the link it already has.\n3. Every 3 s is a very high rate for a mast tilt reading.\n4. Meshtastic channels are LOGICAL. Channel 0 and channel 2 share one physical frequency, so\n   channel separation gives no protection here.'}
2026-07-28 12:45:35,488 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'CORRECTION to B71 — Peter was right, the tilt broadcasts are NOT on air. The mechanism is withdrawn entirely, and B68 goes back to unexplained.', 'notes': 'Peter, 2026-07-28: "it should not be on the mesh and I dont think it is, because it\'s on the\nprivate port !?"\n\nHe is right, and the test he prompted goes further than his point. This is the SECOND cause I\nhave wrongly attached to B68 today — the first was the yagi bearing (step 59). Both were\nwithdrawn on measurement. Recording that pattern explicitly: I have twice taken a real\ncorrelation and asserted a mechanism I had not tested.\n\n## The decisive test\n\nPRIVATE_APP is a PORTNUM, not a transport, so the port name settles nothing either way. What\nsettles it is whether the YAGI ever demodulates one off the air — the two radios share only air,\nnot a bus.\n\n  omni PRIVATE_APP rows today            4737\n    reported by                          the OMNI\'s own gateway ONLY\n    with rssi (demodulated off air)      0\n    relayed by any other node            0\n\nCONTROL, which proves the test is capable of a positive: the YAGI\'s own telemetry IS seen by the\nOMNI over RF — 5 rows, all carrying rssi, at -29, -29, -42, -43 dBm. So cross-reception happens\nand the recorder records it. The absence for PRIVATE_APP is real, not a blind spot.\n\n## And it is worse for my theory than that\n\nThe omni\'s TELEMETRY_APP never appears off-air either:\n\n  OMNI own packets by port      n       ever seen off-air\n    PRIVATE_APP (tilt)        4738            0\n    TELEMETRY_APP             1083            0\n    ROUTING_APP/ADMIN_APP       20            0\n    (no port)                   43           43\n\nFrom this data I cannot demonstrate the omni puts much on air at all. So the withdrawn item is\nnot just "the tilt packets" — it is the whole co-site-interference mechanism I built on them,\nincluding the ~0.48 s airtime, the ~16% duty cycle, the EU868 remark and the ~70% collision\nprobability. ALL of that was arithmetic on a premise I had not checked. None of it stands.\n\n## What actually survives\n\n1. The omni\'s off-air window (08:40-11:16 UTC) still coincides with GARG\'s direct frames\n   returning at 10:47. The CORRELATION is unchanged; the MECHANISM is now absent.\n2. It is also CONFOUNDED, and I should have said so at the time: Peter did not merely power the\n   omni down in that window, he MOVED IT OUTSIDE. Before/after differs in physical position as\n   well as in air time, and nothing in the recorder separates those.\n3. The two radios rarely capture each other — 5 and 43 events in a full day — at -29 to -43 dBm.\n   Co-siting at 5 m is real, but there is no evidence here that it costs us reception.\n\n## Disposition\n\nB71 is WITHDRAWN as a defect. Nothing for node-dash to fix; the tilt feature is doing exactly\nwhat Peter designed it to do and is not on the mesh.\n\nB68 reverts to its original standing: the yagi\'s <=-113 dBm bucket fell ~30% from 06:00 while\nits total volume and mean RSSI stayed flat. CAUSE UNKNOWN. It should stay unknown until\nsomething is actually measured, rather than acquiring a third theory.'}
2026-07-28 13:01:13,402 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'log-levels-per-scope', 'title': 'Per-scope log levels, changeable without a restart — one global level means debugging anything floods everything', 'steps': ['Investigate: lib/log.js state.level is a single shared number closed over by the root and every child (log.js:28, and the header comment says so). Enumerate every scope in use and the current volume each produces.', 'Document: spec with the resolution rule (most specific scope prefix wins), the config shape, and the runtime route. Name what is NOT changing.', 'Implement: per-scope levels, config wiring, and a route to change them at runtime without losing the wake model to a restart.', 'Observe: turn on debug for ONE scope and show that the others stay quiet — the whole point, and the assertion that fails if resolution regresses.', 'Track: hash the spec, run all suites, commit.']}
2026-07-28 13:02:56,087 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B72 CONFIRMED, OURS — the recorder\'s silence warning is a guaranteed false alarm: its "median gap" is computed over DUPLICATE receptions, so it is ~0 s, and every real interval looks like an outage. 1622 WARN lines, 107 of them about GARG.', 'notes': 'Found 2026-07-28 because Peter said "it is still logging errors watch it". He was right; they are\nnot errors, they are warnings that cannot ever be true.\n\n## What is in the log\n\n  WARN recorder: 987ab80f SILENT for 304s (median gap 0s)\n  WARN recorder: 987ab80f BACK after 899s silence\n\n987ab80f is GARG. 899 s is its 900 s beat. The unit is doing exactly what it is designed to do.\n\nCounted over the current pm2 buffer:\n\n  WARN [recorder] lines            1622\n  distinct units warned about       111     <- only TWO of them are ours\n  GARG (987ab80f)                   107     four an hour, forever, all false\n  BNCH (8cee336b)                    15\n  top foreign: 04c588a4 113, 336928d0 64, ea8fe024 59, 04ba78f1 51, aca9fff0 50\n\n## The root cause, which is one number\n\n  "median gap"   0s in 292 lines, 1s in 149, 2s in 89 ...\n\nA median inter-arrival gap of ZERO SECONDS is not a property of any transmitting node. It is what\nyou get when the gap is computed over RAW RECORDER ROWS: mesh-gw emits one event per RECEIVING\nRADIO, so the same packet is written twice, ~0 s apart. The statistic therefore describes OUR OWN\nDUPLICATE RECEPTION, not the unit\'s period.\n\nEverything downstream follows from that:\n  median gap ~0s  ->  any genuine interval looks like an enormous anomaly  ->  warn\nSo a unit on a 900 s beat trips a ~300 s silence check EVERY CYCLE. It is not a tuning problem;\nthe threshold is derived from a meaningless number.\n\n## Three separate defects, worth splitting\n\nB72a  the gap statistic must be computed over DISTINCT TRANSMISSIONS (dedupe by pkt_id), not rows.\nB72b  the silence threshold must respect a unit\'s BEAT. A 900 s sleeper is not silent at 300 s;\n      it is asleep, and we hold a measured beat for it in the model already. Warning about a\n      sleeping unit is the same error class as "never infer a physical fact from a config file",\n      inverted: inferring an outage from an absence we predicted.\nB72c  it warns about 109 units that are not ours. Same root as B47 (wake_log holds 82 distinct\n      units, 2 of them ours). Foreign nodes are not our business and cannot be actioned.\n\n## Why it matters beyond tidiness\n\nThis is the B60 failure mode again, at 20x the volume. On 2026-07-28 a real line —\n  WARN butler: delivery failed for !8cee336b (try 1/5)\nsat at the SAME SEVERITY, interleaved among this. An instrument that cries wolf 1622 times has\nmade its own real alarms unreadable, and the whole point of the debug work is that a prediction or\na failure whose evidence is invisible cannot be trusted or debugged.\n\n## Disposition\n\nOURS — trial-logger/recorder.js, loaded by pac-host. NOT fixed here: the active task is\nlog-levels-per-scope and its spec scope names mesh/lib/log.js, settings.js, host-module.js,\nhost.config.json and mesh/test/log.js. Reported, not fixed, per the rule. Deserves its own task\nand should be done BEFORE the per-scope levels are tuned, because otherwise the levels are being\ntuned to hide a false alarm rather than to reveal a real one.'}
