2026-07-28 13:03:15,353 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B72 CONFIRMED, OURS — the recorder\'s silence warning cannot ever be true: its "median gap" is computed over DUPLICATE receptions so it reads ~0 s, and every real interval then looks like an outage. 1622 WARN lines, 107 about GARG.', 'notes': 'Found 2026-07-28 because Peter said "it is still logging errors watch it". He was right; they are\nnot errors, they are warnings that cannot ever be true.\n\n## What is in the log\n\n  WARN recorder: 987ab80f SILENT for 304s (median gap 0s)\n  WARN recorder: 987ab80f BACK after 899s silence\n\n987ab80f is GARG. 899 s is its 900 s beat. The unit is doing exactly what it was designed to do.\n\nCounted over the current pm2 buffer:\n\n  WARN [recorder] lines            1622\n  distinct units warned about       111    <- only TWO are ours\n  GARG (987ab80f)                   107    four an hour, forever, all false\n  BNCH (8cee336b)                    15\n  top foreign: 04c588a4 113, 336928d0 64, ea8fe024 59, 04ba78f1 51, aca9fff0 50\n\n## The root cause, which is one number\n\n  "median gap":  0s in 292 lines, 1s in 149, 2s in 89, ...\n\nA median inter-arrival gap of ZERO SECONDS is not a property of any transmitting node. It is what\nyou get computing the gap over RAW RECORDER ROWS: mesh-gw emits one event per RECEIVING RADIO, so\nthe same packet is written twice ~0 s apart. The statistic describes OUR OWN DUPLICATE RECEPTION,\nnot the unit\'s period.\n\nEverything follows from that:\n  median gap ~0s -> any genuine interval looks like an enormous anomaly -> warn\nA unit on a 900 s beat therefore trips a ~300 s silence check EVERY CYCLE. Not a tuning problem —\nthe threshold is derived from a meaningless number.\n\n## Three defects, worth splitting\n\nB72a  compute the gap over DISTINCT TRANSMISSIONS (dedupe by pkt_id), not over rows.\nB72b  respect the unit\'s BEAT. A 900 s sleeper is not silent at 300 s, it is asleep, and we already\n      hold a measured beat for it in the model. Inferring an outage from an absence we ourselves\n      predicted is the same class of error as inferring a physical fact from a config file.\nB72c  it warns about 109 units that are not ours. Same root as B47 (wake_log holds 82 distinct\n      units, 2 of them ours). Foreign nodes cannot be actioned by anyone here.\n\n## Why it matters beyond tidiness\n\nThis is the B60 failure mode again at 20x the volume. Today a real line —\n  WARN butler: delivery failed for !8cee336b (try 1/5)\nsat at the SAME SEVERITY interleaved among this. An instrument that cries wolf 1622 times has made\nits own real alarms unreadable.\n\n## Disposition\n\nOURS — trial-logger/recorder.js, loaded by pac-host. NOT fixed in this turn: the active task is\nlog-levels-per-scope whose spec scope names mesh/lib/log.js, settings.js, host-module.js,\nhost.config.json, mesh/test/log.js. Reported not fixed, per the rule.\n\nShould be done BEFORE the per-scope levels are tuned — otherwise the levels get tuned to HIDE a\nfalse alarm rather than to reveal a real one.'}
2026-07-28 13:04:52,535 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'recorder-silence-truth', 'title': "B72: the recorder's silence alarm cannot ever be true — dedupe the gap statistic, respect the beat, and stop alerting on 109 nodes that are not ours", 'steps': ['Investigate: DONE. _liveness records a gap per ROW; mesh-gw emits one row per RECEIVING RADIO, and nowSec() is fractional, so gaps are duplicate-reception intervals (~0.4 s). median therefore ~0, so max(300, 5*med) is ALWAYS 300 s for every node regardless of beat.', 'Document: spec with the dedupe rule, the declared thresholds, and the ours-only alerting split (record whole-mesh, alert on declared units).', 'Implement: dedupe by pkt_id, declare the threshold constants, restrict alerting to declared units.', 'Observe: the median gap for GARG must read ~900 s, not 0 s, and no SILENT alert may fire for a unit inside its own beat.', 'Track: hash the spec, run all suites, commit.']}
2026-07-28 13:21:42,663 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B73 CONFIRMED, OURS, FIXED (5f8c69d) — the two device labels in host.config.json were SWAPPED: !8cee336b read "Garage alarm" while being the bench, !987ab80f read "Bench alarm" while being the garage', 'notes': 'Found 2026-07-28 within a minute of the first stats line going live, which is the entire argument\nfor Peter\'s "why dont we make more use of the pm2 logs".\n\n## The evidence, from one log line each\n\n  wake Bench alarm  err -1204ms | beat 900000 device | -119 dBm  -9.25 dB\n  wake Garage alarm err  -158ms | beat  60000 device |  -47 dBm  +6.25 dB\n\nA 900 s beat at -119 dBm is the unit 2.5 km away. A 60 s beat at -47 dBm is the one on the bench.\nThe names are exactly backwards.\n\nhost/host.config.json modules.mesh.devices, before:\n  "!8cee336b": { "label": "Garage alarm" }     <- BNCH. Bench 2-260727-121, at 51.02633,-3.158835\n  "!987ab80f": { "label": "Bench alarm"  }     <- GARG. Garage 2-260727-120, at 51.0146831,-3.128249\n\nFOUR independent facts contradict the file: the devices\' own long names, their short names\n(BNCH/GARG), their beats (15x apart), and their signals (70 dB apart).\n\n## Why it survived\n\nGET /v1/mesh/devices at index.js builds its label as\n  (u.short_name || (declared[id] && declared[id].label) || null)\nso the device\'s own short name always won and the wrong label was never rendered. It was live but\nunreachable — the same shape as the dead-code discriminator in the butler and the config knob that\nnothing read: a value that is wrong and invisible until something new happens to read it.\n\nThe new stats line read `declared[id].label` directly and surfaced it immediately.\n\n## Fixed\n\n- Labels corrected in host.config.json, with a comment recording the swap so it is not "tidied"\n  back.\n- The stats line now PREFERS the device\'s own short name and falls back to the config label. The\n  unit states its own identity; a config file only claims one. Same rule as "never infer a physical\n  fact from a config file", which this project bought expensively.\n\n## Two adjacent defects found in the same minute, both mine, both fixed in 5f8c69d\n\nB73a  The rotator\'s visible-skip line fired every 60 s for BNCH, forever — the exact flood I had\n      just spent the afternoon removing from the recorder (B72). The reason is STATIC, so it is a\n      once-per-unit fact and not a per-wake event. Now said once, and the message says so.\nB73b  `_roster` is an ARRAY, not a Map. My first _shortName wrote it as a Map lookup, which would\n      have returned null every time and fallen straight back to the wrong labels — a fix that\n      silently does nothing. Caught by checking the field\'s shape rather than assuming it.\n\n## Worth watching, NOT yet a defect\n\nThe first err values after a restart read -1204, -158, +792, -781 ms, against the +-8 ms measured\nover 26 settled intervals earlier today. These are the first wakes after a restart with restored\nstate, so a large error is expected there — but it is now VISIBLE per wake instead of recoverable\nonly by hand from the CSV, which is what the line is for. If it does not settle toward single-digit\nms over the next hour, that is a real finding. Not claimed either way yet.'}
