2026-07-28 14:19:32,299 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'fw-state-the-schedule', 'title': 'FIRMWARE: report beat/slp/rx.win in the wake telemetry, so the service never has to INFER the schedule from gaps', 'steps': ['Investigate: what the wake burst actually emits today (sendTelemetryVariant / RK_TELE_ENV / device_metrics) and where a few extra fields can ride without a new packet - the burst already costs airtime, so this should add bytes, not frames.', 'Decide the field set with Peter. beat and slp are certain; rx.win, fw and the wake counter are candidates. Every one must be a value the service currently GUESSES.', "Document: spec. The rule being applied is 'say only what you measured' - a stated schedule replaces an inferred one, and beatSource becomes 'device' every wake rather than once per lucky reply.", 'Implement device-side, then consume it in mesh/lib/model.js: a stated beat arriving every wake makes the alias guard (B75) almost irrelevant and removes the 3-wake relearn entirely.', "Observe: beatSource == 'device' continuously, and a commanded beat change reflected within ONE wake instead of never (B75) or three wakes (best case).", 'Track: hash, commit, and note this supersedes the model-side workaround in B75.']}
2026-07-28 14:19:47,199 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'task': 'fw-state-the-schedule', 'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-07-28: "the other simple one is that report it\'s beat in the heartbeat data, maybe a few others too. as far as I can see we are getting it\'s hearbeats, even if they are not precisiely on time."\n\nHe is right, and it is a better fix than the model-side workaround it replaces.\n\nTHE PREMISE IS MEASURED. GARG\'s wakes are heard 45 of 47 today, 93.8%, and 4/4 in every hour since 10:00 — so the heartbeat is the one channel that reliably works, in a day where 0 of 28 COMMANDS were delivered. Putting the schedule in the thing that arrives, rather than in a reply that does not, is the whole idea.\n\nWHAT IT REPLACES. Today the service INFERS the beat from inter-arrival gaps, and beatSource only becomes \'device\' on the rare occasion an `interval` reply gets through. That inference is the root of a family of defects:\n  B51  a stated 60 s beat overwritten by a measured 120 s (2x alias from burstGapMs)\n  B75  a commanded 900 -> 300 change can NEVER be learned, because 3 is a small-integer\n       divisor and the alias guard rejects every such gap outright\n  B42  a measured beat outliving the firmware it was measured under\nAll three are the same shape: guessing a SETTING from OBSERVATIONS. A device that states its own\nsetting every wake dissolves them.\n\nThis is the project\'s own rule — "never infer a physical fact from a config file", and "say only\nwhat you measured" — applied to the schedule, which is the one place it was never applied.\n\nFIELD SET, to agree with Peter: `beat` and `slp` are certain. Candidates: `rx.win` (the service has\nNEVER read it and publishes windowSource:\'assumed\'), `fw` (B42 keys off it and currently scrapes it\nout of the long name with a regex), and a wake counter (would make missed wakes countable rather\nthan inferred). Every candidate must be something the service currently guesses.\n\nCONSTRAINT: the wake burst already costs airtime on a battery unit. This should add BYTES to an\nexisting frame, not a new frame.\n\nFLASH-GATED. GARG is deployed and cannot be reflashed; it gets this at the next physical swap. The\nbench can take it now.'}
2026-07-28 14:19:56,439 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'node-page-rehash', 'title': 'PARKED — the node page needs a re-hash: it shows what a unit IS and almost nothing about whether we can REACH it. Our half is the API, node-dash owns the UI.', 'steps': ['PARKED 2026-07-28 by agreement - recorded now so it is not forgotten. Do not start without saying so.', 'OURS FIRST: per-radio best DIRECT reception with a `via`, plus direct/relayed counts per radio (B74/B59). Nothing useful about antennas can be shown until this exists - the page cannot display what we do not send.', 'OURS: the fields added in devices-answer-the-questions (txRadio, rotator point-and-hold state, acks summary, wakesSeen/Expected, wakeErrMs) - already built, needs http.md and a node-dash ack.', 'THEIRS: re-hash the page against those. Raise on the cross-session channel; never edit node-dash from here.', 'Re-screenshot with playwright afterwards and compare against the 2026-07-28 baseline.']}
2026-07-28 14:20:17,922 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'task': 'node-page-rehash', 'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PARKED 2026-07-28 at Peter\'s request: "dont forget about the dashboard / ui discussion we just had create a task for that and park it before we both forget."\n\nPage: node-dash http://192.168.10.205:8000/node/!987ab80f. Screenshot taken with playwright and\nread; baseline is services/garg-node-page.png.\n\nTHE RULE PETER SET: "the page needs to be informative and partly diagnostic, and not show info /\ndata for the sake of it. golden UI/UIX rule." And: "basically all of the questions I have asked\nyou, I should not need to."\n\nThat second line is the real specification. Nine questions he asked TODAY, and for seven the\nservice knew the answer and would not say it — which is why devices-answer-the-questions exists.\n\n## What the page gets WRONG (one thing, and it is an error not a preference)\n\n  tile "HOPS 0 — verified 14 Jul 17:51"\nA two-week-old value rendered with the same weight as live data. GARG has arrived at hop 1,\nRELAYED, for essentially all of today. The page asserts "direct" when it has not been direct in\nhours.\n\nTHE FIX PATTERN IS ALREADY ON THE PAGE: "SIGNAL -116 dBm · Fair · -6.5 dB · direct 45m ago" states\nthe KIND of reception and its AGE. HOPS should carry the same qualifier or be dropped.\n\n## Data for its own sake (Peter\'s rule)\n\n- LATITUDE and LONGITUDE as two large tiles. Raw coordinates diagnose nothing; "2.501 km @ 121 deg"\n  is the same data made informative, and BEARING 121 is already half of it.\n- BATTERY 79% AND VOLTAGE 4.02 V — one fact, two tiles. Voltage is the measured one.\n- CHAN UTIL / AIR UTIL TX are mesh health, not this node\'s.\n\nWALKED BACK, recorded honestly: I first argued for stripping toward pure diagnostics. Peter\'s rule\nis "informative AND PARTLY diagnostic", so battery, voltage and uptime earn their place. The rule\nbites on WRONG and DUPLICATED, not on informative.\n\n## Missing — and this is a page about a unit that stopped answering\n\n1. CAN WE REACH IT. Command outcomes: 0 of 28 delivered today. The single most diagnostic fact\n   available, and absent. Published now as `acks`.\n2. NEXT WAKE COUNTDOWN. Deaf ~99% of the time, so "next window in 13m 20s" matters far more than\n   "last heard 41s ago". Published: nextWake/beat/beatSource/windowMs/windowSource.\n3. WAKE RELIABILITY. 45 of 47 expected today = 93.8% — the number that says whether the alarm\n   works at all. Published now as wakesSeen/wakesExpected.\n4. POINT AND HOLD state — Peter asked for this explicitly. Published now under `rotator`.\n5. WHICH RADIO TRANSMITS — published now as `txRadio`.\n6. PER-RADIO RECEPTION — NOT published, and it is the blocker (B74/B59). We send ONE rssi/snr,\n   whichever radio reported last, and the same GARG packet reads -96 on the omni and -117 on the\n   yagi. A page showing "-96 dBm" is showing a relay\'s signal, not the garage\'s.\n\nTHE THROUGH-LINE: the page shows what the node IS and almost nothing about whether we can REACH it,\nwhich is the only reason anyone opens it.\n\n## Division of labour, per Peter: "you can fix our side, they can fix theirs"\n\nOURS   the API — B74 per-radio first, then http.md for the new fields.\nTHEIRS the page. Raise on the cross-session channel. node-dash is NEVER edited from here.'}
