2026-07-28 14:26:22,620 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B76 UNVERIFIED, HIGH — we may be jamming our own RX window: 3 sends/try at 3 s spacing = 9 s of transmissions into an 8-10 s window, each possibly x4 at the radio from want_ack retransmits', 'notes': 'Peter, 2026-07-28, before going AFK: "do acks potentially burden and / or reduce the likelyhood\nthat a message will be sent / received?"\n\nTwo different things wear the word "ack" and only one touches the air.\n\n## Consuming acks is FREE\n\nmesh-gw\'s `message_status` arrives over BLE from our own gateway. Zero airtime, zero RF. S1\nack-consumption cost nothing and buys the NEVER-ARRIVED vs ARRIVED-ANSWER-LOST distinction.\n\n## Requesting acks is NOT free\n\nOur sends are DMs (gw.js:281 sets `to` when not broadcast), so Meshtastic wants an ACK: the\nDESTINATION transmits one back, and OUR radio retransmits up to 3x if it does not hear one.\nbutler.js:30 already states the premise — "with want_ack on, silence across ~31 s of retransmits".\nAgainst a unit deaf ~99% of the time, most of those retransmits land in dead air: congestion with\nno possibility of arrival.\n\n## And we stack our own retries on top of that\n\n  timing.sendSpacingMs 3000, and the ledger shows:\n\n  verb        tries  sends  sends/try  ackState\n  config      1      1      1.0        delivered\n  ping        1      1      1.0        delivered\n  chunk       2      4      2.0        delivered\n  status      2      4      2.0        delivered\n  window      3      9      3.0        delivered\n  ping        5      15     3.0        unconfirmed\n  status      5      15     3.0        unconfirmed\n  status      5      15     3.0        unconfirmed\n  txp         5      13     2.6        unconfirmed\n\nThree sends per try at 3 s spacing is NINE SECONDS of our own transmissions into an 8-10 s RX\nwindow. Each may become up to four packets at the radio (original + 3 want_ack retransmits), so a\nsingle try can be ~12 transmissions, ~19 s of airtime, aimed at a ~10 s window.\n\n## The correlation, and the reason it is NOT yet a finding\n\nEverything that succeeded used <=2 sends/try; everything stuck at 3.0 failed.\n\nREVERSE CAUSATION IS THE OBVIOUS ALTERNATIVE and I am not dismissing it: we send three times\nBECAUSE the first two failed, so the arrow may run the other way entirely. Recorded as UNVERIFIED\nfor exactly that reason. I have been wrong twice today attaching a mechanism to a correlation\n(B68\'s bearing, then B71\'s tilt broadcasts) and this one gets the same treatment until tested.\n\n## The test, which is cheap\n\nCap transmissions to ONE per wake window and compare ack delivery over N commands. One change, one\nmeasurement. If delivery improves, self-congestion is real; if it does not, the arrow ran the other\nway and we have lost nothing. `timing.sendSpacingMs` and the butler\'s per-window send loop are the\nknobs; both are already declared.\n\nAlso worth establishing separately: whether mesh-gw sets want_ack on our behalf, and whether it is\nsettable per message. We POST {text, channel, to, reply_id} and never mention want_ack — so the\nretransmit behaviour is currently WHATEVER MESH-GW DEFAULTS TO, which nobody here has checked.\n\n## Why it matters now\n\nIt is the most plausible mechanism yet for 0 of 28 commands delivered today that does NOT require\nthe link to be broken — and the link demonstrably is not broken, since 45 of 47 heartbeats arrived\nin the same period (93.8%).'}
