2026-07-28 14:26:42,255 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B76 UNVERIFIED, HIGH — we may be jamming our own RX window: 3 sends/try at 3 s spacing = 9 s of transmissions into an 8-10 s window, each possibly x4 at the radio from want_ack retransmits', 'notes': 'Peter, 2026-07-28, before going AFK: "do acks potentially burden and / or reduce the likelyhood\nthat a message will be sent / received?"\n\nTwo different things wear the word "ack" and only one touches the air.\n\n## Consuming acks is FREE\n\nmesh-gw\'s message_status arrives over BLE from our own gateway. Zero airtime, zero RF. S1\nack-consumption cost nothing and buys the NEVER-ARRIVED vs ARRIVED-ANSWER-LOST distinction.\n\n## Requesting acks is NOT free\n\nOur sends are DMs (gw.js:281 sets `to` when not broadcast), so Meshtastic wants an ACK: the\nDESTINATION transmits one back, and OUR radio retransmits up to 3x if it does not hear one.\nbutler.js:30 already states the premise — "with want_ack on, silence across ~31 s of retransmits".\nAgainst a unit deaf ~99% of the time most of those retransmits land in dead air: congestion with no\npossibility of arrival.\n\n## And we stack our own retries on top\n\ntiming.sendSpacingMs 3000, and the ledger shows:\n\n  verb        tries  sends  sends/try  ackState\n  config      1      1      1.0        delivered\n  ping        1      1      1.0        delivered\n  chunk       2      4      2.0        delivered\n  status      2      4      2.0        delivered\n  window      3      9      3.0        delivered\n  ping        5      15     3.0        unconfirmed\n  status      5      15     3.0        unconfirmed\n  status      5      15     3.0        unconfirmed\n  txp         5      13     2.6        unconfirmed\n\nThree sends per try at 3 s spacing is NINE SECONDS of our own transmissions into an 8-10 s RX\nwindow. Each may become up to four packets at the radio (original + 3 want_ack retransmits), so one\ntry can be ~12 transmissions, ~19 s of airtime, aimed at a ~10 s window.\n\n## The correlation, and why it is NOT yet a finding\n\nEverything that succeeded used <=2 sends/try; everything stuck at 3.0 failed.\n\nREVERSE CAUSATION IS THE OBVIOUS ALTERNATIVE and I am not dismissing it: we send three times\nBECAUSE the first two failed, so the arrow may run the other way. Recorded UNVERIFIED for exactly\nthat reason. I have been wrong twice today attaching a mechanism to a correlation (B68\'s bearing,\nthen B71\'s tilt broadcasts); this one gets the same treatment until tested.\n\n## The test, which is cheap\n\nCap transmissions to ONE per wake window and compare ack delivery over N commands. One change, one\nmeasurement. If delivery improves, self-congestion is real; if not, the arrow ran the other way and\nnothing is lost. timing.sendSpacingMs and the butler\'s per-window send loop are the knobs, both\nalready declared.\n\nSEPARATELY WORTH ESTABLISHING: whether mesh-gw sets want_ack on our behalf and whether it is\nsettable per message. We POST {text, channel, to, reply_id} and never mention want_ack — so the\nretransmit behaviour is WHATEVER MESH-GW DEFAULTS TO, which nobody here has checked.\n\n## Why it matters now\n\nThe most plausible mechanism yet for 0 of 28 commands delivered today that does NOT require the\nlink to be broken — and it demonstrably is not broken: 45 of 47 heartbeats arrived in the same\nperiod (93.8%).'}
2026-07-28 14:27:31,761 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B75 CONFIRMED, OURS — a COMMANDED beat change to a small-integer divisor can never be learned: the alias guard rejects every such gap outright, so 900 -> 300 would leave the model permanently wrong', 'notes': 'Found 2026-07-28 because Peter asked "if we change the beat, the entire model breaks and cannot\ndetect a sudden change in beat patterns?" — after I had already QUEUED `interval 300` to GARG. He\nwas right and I had just created the hazard. Both commands cancelled before they could land.\n\n## The code\n\nmodel.js, the device-stated branch:\n\n  } else if (beatSource === \'device\' && !agrees(gap, beat)) {\n      if (!isFactorAlias(gap, beat, tol)) {          // <- only then may it re-seed\n          rejects = rejects.concat(gap).slice(-RESEED_AFTER);\n          ...\n  }\n\n  function isFactorAlias(gap, beat, tol) {\n    for (let k = 2; k <= 4; k++) {\n      if (Math.abs(gap - beat * k) <= beat * k * tol) return true;   // missed k-1 wakes\n      if (Math.abs(gap * k - beat) <= beat * tol) return true;       // split one wake k ways\n    }\n  }\n\nGARG\'s beatSource is \'device\' (published: beat 900000, beatSource device). 900/300 = 3, so\n`gap * 3 == beat` and every 300 s gap returns TRUE — classified as "we split one wake 3 ways" and\nREJECTED OUTRIGHT. Not counted toward a re-seed. The model would hold 900 s forever.\n\n## The guard is RIGHT; its scope is wrong\n\nIt exists because a real 60 s beat was mis-learned as 120 s when burstGapMs hid every second wake\n(B51). Rejecting small-integer ratios is exactly correct for MIS-OBSERVATION.\n\nIt is exactly wrong for a change WE COMMANDED. The service knows it sent `interval 300` — it should\nnot have to rediscover a value it chose, and it certainly should not refuse to.\n\n## Escape paths, and why neither saves us\n\n1. The `interval` REPLY arrives -> beatReported(300) sets it directly. That is the intended path.\n   GARG has answered 0 of 28 commands today, so betting on the reply is precisely the wrong bet.\n2. Nothing else. If the command LANDS and the reply is LOST, we are stuck wrong — and stuck wrong\n   means nextWake is wrong, so the butler cues into dead air and the rotator points at the wrong\n   time, on a unit we already cannot command.\n\n## Blast radius\n\nAny commanded beat that is a small-integer multiple or divisor (k=2..4) of the current one:\n  900 -> 300, 450, 225   and   900 -> 1800, 2700, 3600\nPeter\'s requested 5-minute beat (300) is in that set. So is the obvious 15 -> 30 min.\n\n## The fix, which is small\n\nThe service knows what it commanded. `model.beatCommanded(unit, ms)` records the intended value as\na HINT — never a truth, because commanding is not landing. The alias guard then does not reject a\ngap that AGREES with the commanded value; it is admitted as evidence and the normal RESEED_AFTER\nrun adopts it. The guard stays fully intact for gaps that agree with nothing we asked for.\n\nSUPERSEDED IN THE LONG RUN by fw-state-the-schedule (device states its beat every wake, which\narrives 93.8% of the time). But that is FLASH-GATED and GARG cannot be reflashed while deployed —\nso the model-side fix is the only one that can help the unit that needs it.'}
2026-07-28 14:27:41,954 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'commanded-beat-hint', 'title': 'B75: the model must not refuse to learn a beat WE commanded — a commanded value unblocks the alias guard, without weakening it', 'steps': ["Investigate: DONE. model.js device-stated branch gates re-seed behind !isFactorAlias(gap,beat,tol); 900->300 is k=3 so every gap is rejected as 'split one wake 3 ways'. GARG beatSource is 'device'. Peter's requested 5-min beat is inside the blast radius.", 'Document: spec. A commanded value is a HINT, never a truth - commanding is not landing. It may only ADMIT evidence the guard would otherwise discard; it must never set the beat directly.', "Implement: model.beatCommanded(unit, ms) + the guard exemption, called from the send path when verb === 'interval'.", "Observe: a simulated 900->300 with beatSource 'device' must re-seed to 300 after RESEED_AFTER agreeing gaps WITH the hint, and must still reject them WITHOUT it - the test fails if the exemption is removed.", "Track: hash, run all suites, commit. Then re-queue Peter's interval 300 and window 30 to GARG."]}
2026-07-28 14:32:29,539 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'fw-reply-ack-configurable', 'title': 'FIRMWARE: ping/status replies request an ACK the unit sleeps through — make it a config field so it can be switched off and measured', 'steps': ['Investigate: DONE. main.cpp:3138 hardcodes /*wantAck=*/true on the comfort-reply DM path, and sendReply passes it through at :1546. Telemetry is unaffected - it is broadcast, and MT never want_acks a broadcast. Not configurable anywhere.', 'Document: spec. A new CONFIG_FIELD reply.ack, default preserving CURRENT behaviour so the flash changes nothing on its own - one change per measurement, and the change is the A/B, not the flash.', 'Implement: reply.ack in CONFIG_FIELDS + applySet + a g_replyAck global feeding the sendReply call sites. Regenerate the schema so services picks the field up automatically (api-parity guards this).', "Observe: bench only. Flash, confirm `reply.ack` appears in the generated schema and in GET /v1/mesh/config, toggle it and confirm the reply DM's want_ack flag changes on the wire.", 'Track: hash, commit. GARG gets it at the next swap - it cannot be reflashed while deployed.']}
2026-07-28 14:54:21,706 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B77 CONFIRMED, OURS — the config route reports "unit did not answer" for a write that SUCCEEDED: the ledger shows done/2 tries/no error while the HTTP caller was told it failed', 'notes': 'Found 2026-07-28 while testing reply.ack end to end on the bench.\n\n  POST /v1/mesh/config/!8cee336b {"reply.ack":0}\n  -> 504 {"error":"unit did not answer",\n          "detail":"the unit is asleep or out of range; it answers only in its wake window"}\n\nThe ledger for the same command:\n\n  14:51:05  replyack [\'off\']  DONE  tries=2  err=null\n            result {"type":"replyack","on":0,"was":0,"ok":true}  ack=delivered  sends=4\n\nThe write LANDED. reply.ack is 0 on the bench right now. The caller was told the unit did not\nanswer.\n\n## Why\n\nThe write needed TWO wake windows. At a 60 s beat that is ~75 s+, which outgrows\ntiming.domainWaitMs (75000). dispatch\'s live path returns {queued:true,id} on expiry — correct —\nbut something between there and the route turns it into a timeout error instead of the queued\nshape config.js:296 was written to produce. The exact hop is not yet identified; config.set\'s\n`queued()` guard is applied to the needsCurrent READ, and whether the WRITE result gets the same\ntreatment is the first thing to check.\n\n## Why it matters more than it looks\n\nThis is the SAME CLASS as B67, which this route was supposed to have fixed: asserting a negative\noutcome for a command that is in flight or has already succeeded. B67\'s spec says it explicitly —\n"reporting ECONFIRM for it would be a worse lie than the old \'unit did not answer\', because it\nasserts a negative outcome for a command that may well succeed at the next wake window". We now\nemit the old lie itself.\n\nOperationally it is worse than a cosmetic error: an operator told "the unit did not answer" will\nRESEND. On a unit that is deaf 99% of the time, a needless resend is another 3 transmissions into\nthe next wake window — which is exactly the self-congestion suspected in B76.\n\n## Not yet fixed\n\nRecorded rather than chased mid-flight: the reply.ack A/B is the live experiment and I am not\nediting the config path underneath it. The evidence above is enough to fix it cleanly afterwards.\n\n## Adjacent, from the same ledger read\n\n`sleepmode [\'off\'] cancelled err={"code":"error","message":"gateway send failed: 500"}` at\n13:34:56. mesh-gw returned 500 on a send. Separate from B76\'s queue_failed/503s but the same\nfamily: a fraction of our attempts never reach the air, and nothing aggregates that.'}
