suspected in B76.\n\n## Not fixed yet, deliberately\n\nThe reply.ack A/B is the live experiment and I am not editing the config path underneath it. The\nevidence above is enough to fix it cleanly afterwards.\n\n## Adjacent, from the same ledger read\n\nsleepmode [\'off\'] cancelled err={"code":"error","message":"gateway send failed: 500"} at 13:34:56.\nmesh-gw returned 500 on a send. Distinct from B76\'s queue_failed/503s but the same family: a\nfraction of our attempts never reach the air, and nothing aggregates that anywhere.'}
2026-07-28 14:56:22,480 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'per-radio-reception', 'title': 'B74/B59: keep the best DIRECT reception PER RADIO with a via, so "is that coming from OMNI or YAGI?" is answerable and radio selection has a sound metric', 'steps': ['Investigate: DONE. The model keeps one reception per unit and /devices publishes a single rssi/snr - whichever radio reported last. The same GARG packet reads -96 on the omni (via relay 4) and -117 on the yagi, 21 dB apart, describing two different links.', 'Document: spec. DIRECT only for the comparison (hop_limit == hop_start); relayed is a different physical link and must be counted separately, never averaged in. No recent direct sample = UNKNOWN, never a stale winner.', 'Implement: per-addr best-direct retention in model.heard, published as `radios` on /devices.', 'Observe: /devices shows both radios for GARG with their own rssi/snr and direct/relayed counts, and says UNKNOWN where there is no recent direct sample.', 'Track: hash, run all suites, commit. Unblocks node-page-rehash and radio-selection part 2.']}
2026-07-28 14:58:11,392 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'fw-state-the-schedule', 'title': "Peter's addition: put the LAST PACKET ID THE DEVICE HEARD in the heartbeat — it is proof of arrival from the one channel that works, and it settles B69/B76 outright", 'notes': 'Peter, 2026-07-28: "here\'s a thought, maybe the beat should also include the last messge id heard?"\n\nThis is the highest-value field of the set, above beat and slp, and it deserves saying why.\n\n## What it settles\n\nToday the only arrival evidence is mesh-gw\'s `delivered` ack. That ack can come from a RELAY — the\nproject already knows this and butler.js says so: "message_status delivered ... a relay acking ONE\nHOP is evidence of movement, never of arrival". So `delivered` does NOT prove the device received\nanything, and `no_ack` does not prove it did not.\n\nA packet id ECHOED BY THE DEVICE ITSELF is proof of arrival, from the device, with no inference.\nIt converts the central open question of the whole day —\n\n  0 of 28 commands delivered: did they ARRIVE and the replies get lost, or never arrive at all?\n\n— from unanswerable into a lookup. B69 and B76 both hinge on exactly that distinction, and both are\ncurrently stuck on it.\n\n## Why it belongs in the HEARTBEAT specifically\n\nMeasured today: GARG\'s heartbeats arrive 45 of 47 (93.8%), while 0 of 28 command replies got\nthrough. The heartbeat is the channel that works. Putting arrival evidence in the reply — which is\nwhat we do now — puts it in the channel that does not.\n\nIt also survives the failure it is diagnosing: if a command arrives and the REPLY is lost, the next\nheartbeat still carries the id. That is precisely the case we cannot currently see.\n\n## Shape, to settle with Peter\n\nCheapest useful form is the LAST packet id heard plus how long ago:\n  {"rxid": <u32>, "rxage": <secs>}\nA ring of the last N ids would show a burst pattern (which of our 3 sends per try landed - B76) at\n4 bytes each. Start with one; N is a later question.\n\nNote the id is already available device-side: rx.id is used for reply correlation today\n(sendReply passes rx.id as both requestId and reply_id), so nothing new has to be captured.\n\n## Consumption, services side\n\nThe butler already records every packet id it sends, per attempt, in the ledger\'s sentIds[]. So a\nheartbeat carrying rxid can be matched against sentIds with no new bookkeeping at all — the join\nalready exists, it just has nothing to join to. That makes the services half small.\n\nFLASH-GATED like the rest of this task: bench can take it now, GARG at the next physical swap.'}
