2026-07-28 14:58:30,528 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'fw-state-the-schedule', 'title': "Peter's addition: put the LAST PACKET ID THE DEVICE HEARD in the heartbeat — proof of arrival from the one channel that works, and it settles B69/B76 outright", 'notes': 'Peter, 2026-07-28: "here\'s a thought, maybe the beat should also include the last messge id heard?"\n\nHighest-value field of the set, above beat and slp, and it deserves saying why.\n\n## What it settles\n\nToday the only arrival evidence is mesh-gw\'s `delivered` ack — and that ack can come from a RELAY.\nThe project already knows this; butler.js says it outright: a relay acking ONE HOP is "evidence of\nmovement, never of arrival". So `delivered` does not prove the device received anything, and\n`no_ack` does not prove it did not.\n\nA packet id ECHOED BY THE DEVICE ITSELF is proof of arrival, from the device, with no inference. It\nturns the central open question of the whole day —\n\n  0 of 28 commands delivered: did they ARRIVE and the replies get lost, or never arrive at all?\n\n— from unanswerable into a lookup. B69 and B76 both hinge on exactly that distinction and both are\nstuck on it.\n\n## Why the HEARTBEAT specifically\n\nMeasured today: GARG\'s heartbeats arrive 45 of 47 (93.8%) while 0 of 28 command replies got\nthrough. The heartbeat is the channel that works; the reply is the channel that does not, and\nputting arrival evidence in the reply is what we do now.\n\nIt also survives the failure it is diagnosing: if a command ARRIVES and the REPLY is lost, the next\nheartbeat still carries the id. That is exactly the case we currently cannot see.\n\n## Shape, to settle with Peter\n\nCheapest useful form is the last id plus its age:\n  {"rxid": <u32>, "rxage": <secs>}\nA ring of the last N ids would additionally show WHICH of our 3 sends per try landed (B76), at\n4 bytes each. Start with one; N is a later question.\n\nNothing new has to be captured device-side: rx.id is already in hand for reply correlation\n(sendReply passes it as both requestId and reply_id).\n\n## Services side is small, because the join already exists\n\nThe butler records every packet id it sends, per attempt, in the ledger\'s sentIds[]. A heartbeat\ncarrying rxid matches straight against that with no new bookkeeping — the join exists today and\nsimply has nothing to join to.\n\nFLASH-GATED like the rest of this task: bench now, GARG at the next physical swap.'}
