2026-07-28 15:08:02,182 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B78 CONFIRMED (the fact) — queue_failed is GARG-ONLY: 15 of 15 across BOTH radios, 0 for the bench in 94 sends. Our commands are refused by the radio and never reach the air.', 'notes': 'Found 2026-07-28 ~15:05 while watching the re-queued `interval 300` for GARG.\n\n## The fact, and it is not in doubt\n\n  interval [\'300\']  tries=1  sends=3\n    sentIds [1880293463, 2821562435, 4058345349]\n    acks    [\'queue_failed\', \'queue_failed\', \'queue_failed\']\n\nmesh-gw accepted all three over HTTP — journal shows POST /%212687afb1/messages 200 OK at\n15:02:17 / :27 / :38 — and the RADIO then refused every one. queue_failed is defined in\nbutler.js:TERMINAL_ACK as "the radio refused to queue it. It never left the device."\n\nSplit by destination, from the ledger:\n\n  BNCH before-switch  n=83   delivered=14  no_ack=52  failed=17  queue_failed=0\n  BNCH after-switch   n=11   delivered=6   no_ack=5   failed=0   queue_failed=0\n  GARG before-switch  n=128  delivered=15  no_ack=87  failed=14  queue_failed=12\n  GARG after-switch   n=3    delivered=0   no_ack=0   failed=0   queue_failed=3\n\n15 of 15 queue_failed events are GARG-bound. ZERO for the bench in 94 sends. It happens on BOTH\nradios (12 on the yagi before the switch, 3 on the omni after), so it is NOT the antenna, NOT the\nomni, and NOT a duty-cycle ceiling — any of those would hit both destinations.\n\nIT IS DESTINATION-SPECIFIC. That is the whole finding, and it reframes 0-of-28: a meaningful\nfraction of our commands to the garage are not weak, not lost in the air, and not mistimed against\nthe wake window. They are never transmitted at all.\n\n## Ruled out by measurement, not by argument\n\nMISSING PKI KEY. The firmware documents this exact refusal — "without a stored key for us it\nrefuses to send a DM at all (Router.cpp:750, \'refusing to send legacy DM\')" — and it would be\ndestination-specific, so it was the obvious candidate. CHECKED and it is WRONG:\n\n  !8cee336b BNCH: public_key PRESENT len=44  unmessagable=False\n  !987ab80f GARG: public_key PRESENT len=44  unmessagable=False\n\nBoth keyed, neither unmessagable.\n\n## HYPOTHESIS, explicitly not a finding\n\nA want_ack DM holds a slot in the radio\'s reliable-retransmit table until ACKed or expired (~31 s).\nWe send THREE times per try at 3 s spacing (B76). The bench ACKs promptly and frees its slots;\nGARG never ACKs, so its slots stay occupied and further sends to that destination are refused.\n\nThat would explain every feature: destination-specific, present on both radios, absent for the unit\nthat answers, and proportional to send volume.\n\nI have attached a mechanism to a correlation twice today and been wrong both times (B68\'s bearing,\nB71\'s tilt broadcasts). This one is recorded as a hypothesis until tested.\n\n## The test is the same one B76 already proposes\n\nCap transmissions to ONE per wake window and measure queue_failed. If the mechanism is right,\nqueue_failed should disappear along with the surplus sends. One change, one measurement — and it\ntests B76 and B78 together because they would be the same defect.\n\n## Why this outranks most of the open ledger\n\nIt is the first explanation for 0-of-28 that requires nothing to be wrong with the LINK — and the\nlink is demonstrably fine in the other direction, 45 of 47 heartbeats today (93.8%).'}
2026-07-28 15:09:03,007 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'queue-refused-sends', 'title': 'B78: the radio refuses our GARG commands and they never reach the air — 15 of 15 queue_failed are GARG-bound, 0 for the bench in 94 sends', 'steps': ['Investigate: establish WHICH send in a burst is refused and what state precedes it. Per-request, map send index -> ack outcome, and correlate with outstanding unacked DMs to the same destination. Do not touch code.', 'Document: spec with the mechanism the evidence actually supports, the fix, and what stays unproven.', 'Implement: the change the evidence indicates - almost certainly capping concurrent unacked sends per destination.', 'Observe: queue_failed rate for GARG before vs after, from the ledger. The number must come from the ledger, not from reasoning.', 'Track: hash the spec, run all suites, commit.']}
2026-07-28 15:18:06,668 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_switch args={'name': 'fw-state-the-schedule'}
2026-07-28 15:18:29,273 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'fw-state-the-schedule', 'title': "Peter: use OUR OWN ack/ok, not Meshtastic's want_ack — an application ack riding the heartbeat is proof of arrival; a routing ACK is not, and costs retransmits", 'notes': 'Peter, 2026-07-28: "maybe we should use our own ack/ok instead".\n\nThis is the natural partner to the last-message-id-heard idea and it replaces a mechanism the\nproject has repeatedly been misled by.\n\n## Why Meshtastic\'s want_ack is the wrong instrument for us\n\n1. IT DOES NOT PROVE ARRIVAL. A relay acking one hop produces `delivered`. butler.js says so\n   outright: a relay acking ONE HOP is "evidence of movement, never of arrival". We have been\n   treating `delivered` as arrival all day and it is not.\n2. IT IS NOT FREE. want_ack puts the transport on the reliable path and it retransmits until\n   ACKed. Against a unit awake ~1% of the time those retransmits are spent into dead air, and\n   B78 is exactly that bill arriving: 15 of 131 GARG sends refused by the radio outright.\n3. ITS ABSENCE PROVES NOTHING EITHER. `no_ack` is UNCONFIRMED, never "lost" — the butler already\n   documents this, and it is why 0-of-28 could not be diagnosed until today.\n\n## What an application-level ack gives instead\n\nThe device says, in a frame WE define, "I have message id X". That is:\n\n  - PROOF, from the device itself, with no inference and no relay in the middle;\n  - FREE of retransmit machinery — it rides a frame that is already being sent;\n  - CARRIED ON THE CHANNEL THAT WORKS. Measured today: heartbeats 45 of 47 (93.8%), command\n    replies 0 of 28. Arrival evidence belongs in the former, and it is currently in the latter.\n\nIt also survives the exact failure it diagnoses: if a command ARRIVES and the REPLY is lost, the\nnext heartbeat still carries the id.\n\n## How it composes with the rest\n\nSame field as step 8 (last message id heard) — one mechanism, two readings of it. With `rxid` in\nthe heartbeat we can drop want_ack on our own sends entirely (reply.ack already makes the DEVICE\nside switchable, fw-reply-ack-configurable) and stop paying for a guarantee that never proved\nwhat we wanted.\n\nSERVICES SIDE IS ALREADY BUILT: the ledger records every packet id we send per attempt in\nsentIds[]. A heartbeat carrying rxid joins straight onto it. The join exists and has nothing to\njoin to.\n\n## Sequencing\n\nDo NOT drop want_ack until rxid is landing and matching. Removing the only arrival signal we have,\nhowever flawed, before the better one is proven would leave us blind. Order: rxid in the heartbeat\n-> confirm it matches sentIds -> then reply.ack=0 and measure.'}
2026-07-28 15:18:51,381 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'sent-is-not-transmitted', 'title': "Peter: DO NOT ASSUME a packet the butler sent was transmitted — `sends` counts POSTs accepted by mesh-gw, and 15 of GARG's 131 never left the radio", 'steps': ["Investigate: DONE. timing.js:165-172 fires onSent as soon as gw.sendText returns an id - that is mesh-gw ACCEPTING the POST, not the radio transmitting. queue_failed arrives later and means it never left the device. So sentIds/sends is 'handed to the gateway', published everywhere as if it meant transmitted.", 'Document: spec. sends and transmitted are DIFFERENT NUMBERS and both must be published; transmitted is derived from the ack, and is UNKNOWN until the ack arrives - never assumed.', 'Implement: transmitted/notSent in the ack summary and the stats line, with unknown counted as unknown.', 'Observe: a GARG request with queue_failed must show transmitted < sends, and the difference must equal the queue_failed count.', 'Track: hash, run all suites, commit.']}
2026-07-28 15:20:15,921 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'task': 'fw-state-the-schedule', 'number': 9, 'text': 'Peter, 2026-07-28: "maybe we should use our own ack/ok instead", clarified: "basically an ok reply\nmessage with any relevant meta data".\n\n## The pattern ALREADY EXISTS — this extends it, it does not invent it\n\nEvery device reply already carries `ok`:\n\n  {"type":"replyack","on":0,"was":0,"ok":true}\n  {"type":"window","secs":8,"ok":true}\n  {"type":"interval","secs":900,"ok":true}\n\nSo "our own ok, with the relevant metadata" is the convention the firmware already follows. Two\nthings are missing, and both are small:\n\n1. WHICH MESSAGE IT IS ACKING. The reply threads reply_id at the protocol level (sendReply passes\n   rx.id as both requestId and reply_id), but the JSON does not SAY the id. Adding it makes the ack\n   self-describing rather than dependent on protocol correlation surviving.\n2. IT RIDES THE REPLY — the channel that does not arrive. Measured today: replies 0 of 28,\n   heartbeats 45 of 47 (93.8%). An ok that only travels in a reply is lost with the reply.\n\n## What "our own ack" therefore means concretely\n\n  a) keep the existing {"...","ok":true} reply shape, and add the id it answers;\n  b) ALSO carry the last id heard in the HEARTBEAT (step 8) — so arrival is provable even when the\n     reply is lost, which is precisely the case we cannot see today;\n  c) then stop asking Meshtastic for want_ack (reply.ack is already switchable,\n     fw-reply-ack-configurable) and stop paying retransmits for a signal that never proved arrival\n     anyway — a relay acking one hop produces `delivered`.\n\n## Why ours is better than Meshtastic\'s, in one line each\n\n  MT delivered  = someone forwarded it. Not proof of arrival (butler.js says so outright).\n  MT no_ack     = unconfirmed. Not proof of loss.\n  OUR ok+id     = the device itself, naming the message. Proof, with no inference.\n\n## Sequencing, unchanged and important\n\nDo NOT drop want_ack until the id is landing and matching sentIds. Removing the only arrival\nsignal we have, however flawed, before the better one is proven would leave us blind.\n\nSERVICES SIDE IS ALREADY BUILT: the ledger records every packet id per attempt in sentIds[], so an\nok carrying an id joins straight onto it. The join exists and has nothing to join to.'}
2026-07-28 15:20:28,321 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'task': 'fw-state-the-schedule', 'number': 9, 'context': 'fw-state-the-schedule', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-07-28: "maybe we should use our own ack/ok instead", clarified: "basically an ok reply\nmessage with any relevant meta data".\n\n## The pattern ALREADY EXISTS — this extends it, it does not invent it\n\nEvery device reply already carries `ok`:\n\n  {"type":"replyack","on":0,"was":0,"ok":true}\n  {"type":"window","secs":8,"ok":true}\n\nSo "our own ok, with the relevant metadata" is the convention the firmware already follows. Two\nthings are missing, and both are small:\n\n1. WHICH MESSAGE IT ACKS. The reply threads reply_id at the protocol level (sendReply passes rx.id\n   as both requestId and reply_id) but the JSON does not SAY the id. Putting it in the payload\n   makes the ack self-describing instead of dependent on protocol correlation surviving.\n2. IT RIDES THE REPLY — the channel that does not arrive. Measured today: replies 0 of 28,\n   heartbeats 45 of 47 (93.8%). An ok that travels only in a reply is lost with the reply.\n\n## What "our own ack" means concretely\n\n  a) keep the existing {"type":...,"ok":true} reply shape and add the id it answers;\n  b) ALSO carry the last id heard in the HEARTBEAT (step 8), so arrival is provable even when the\n     reply is lost — precisely the case we cannot see today;\n  c) then stop asking Meshtastic for want_ack (already switchable via reply.ack,\n     fw-reply-ack-configurable) and stop paying retransmits for a signal that never proved arrival.\n\n## Why ours beats Meshtastic\'s, one line each\n\n  MT delivered = someone forwarded it. NOT proof of arrival — butler.js says so outright.\n  MT no_ack    = unconfirmed. NOT proof of loss.\n  OUR ok + id  = the device itself, naming the message. Proof, with no inference.\n\n## Sequencing, unchanged and important\n\nDo NOT drop want_ack until the id is landing and matching sentIds. Removing the only arrival signal\nwe have, however flawed, before the better one is proven would leave us blind.\n\nSERVICES SIDE IS ALREADY BUILT: the ledger records every packet id per attempt in sentIds[], so an\nok carrying an id joins straight onto it. The join exists and has nothing to join to.'}
