2026-07-28 16:56:04,767 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B80 CONFIRMED (the drain) / STRONGLY INDICATED (the cause) — GARG has been discharging since 27 Jul 16:38, ~9.8 mV/h with NO daytime recovery, immediately after a burst of camera commands. 1.6 days to 3.6 V.', 'notes': 'Raised by Peter, 2026-07-28: "the battery drain I am seeing at the garage is inconsistent of a rak\nthat is asleep so much. my gut feeling is that esp32 is actually awake all of the time."\n\nHis gut is supported by the recorder.\n\n## THE DRAIN IS REAL AND IT STARTED ON A DATE\n\nGARG vbat, per day (median-based, from the recorder):\n\n  25 Jul   3.999 -> 4.211   net  +212 mV over 23.2 h  =  +9.1 mV/h   CHARGING\n  26 Jul   4.162 -> 4.167   net    +5 mV over 22.9 h  =  +0.2 mV/h   net flat\n  27 Jul   4.268 -> 4.141   net  -127 mV over 17.6 h  =  -7.2 mV/h   decline begins\n  28 Jul   4.132 -> 3.977   net  -155 mV over 15.8 h  =  -9.8 mV/h\n\nAnd on 28 Jul the DAYTIME median is 111 mV BELOW the night median — it is draining faster in\ndaylight, with no recovery at all. On 26 Jul daytime was +17.5 mV above night.\n\nSo this is not a slow flat battery. Something changed, and it changed between 26 and 27 July.\n\n## WHAT CHANGED, and the correlation is tight\n\nCamera commands to GARG — the ONLY ones ever sent, all within 41 minutes:\n\n  27 Jul 15:54:31  camu ping   delivered\n  27 Jul 16:06:25  cam  grab   no_ack\n  27 Jul 16:07:18  camu ping   delivered\n  27 Jul 16:35:14  camu ping   delivered\n\nUptime today runs 33849 -> 83349 s, MONOTONIC, so the unit booted ~27 Jul 16:38 — minutes after\nthe last camera command — and has not rebooted since. The discharge dates from that boot.\n\n## THE MECHANISM IS ALREADY DOCUMENTED IN THIS PROJECT\n\ncamera-rtc-recovery records it: the Timer Camera X firmware\n(pio/projects/timercam-chunk/src/main.cpp) "latches its own power with G33 in setup() and relies\non ext0 deep sleep, which only works while the chip still HAS power". M5\'s own docs add: "when\nusing external power, the device will remain powered on" — USB masks the whole problem, battery\ndoes not.\n\nA camera woken by `cam grab` / `camu ping`, latching G33 and never releasing it, is exactly a load\nthat appears at a known instant and never goes away. That fits every feature above.\n\n## OPERATIONAL — the number that matters\n\nAt -9.8 mV/h from 3.977 V:\n  to 3.6 V   38 h  = 1.6 days\n  to 3.5 V   49 h  = 2.0 days\n  to 3.4 V   59 h  = 2.5 days\n\n## NOT PROVEN, and I am not going to claim it\n\nThat the ESP32 is the load. The correlation is strong and the mechanism is documented, but I have\nno current measurement and no camera telemetry — the device tells us nothing about the camera\'s\npower state. I have attached a mechanism to a correlation twice today and been wrong both times.\n\nCHEAP DISCRIMINATOR, and it is free: the unit is about to be SWAPPED. If the returned unit\'s\ncamera is found powered/warm, that settles it. If the replacement unit — same firmware, no camera\ncommands sent — holds its charge, that settles it too.\n\n## Actions\n\n1. BEFORE/AT THE SWAP: check whether the returned camera is powered. One look, decisive.\n2. DO NOT send camera commands to the deployed unit until this is understood.\n3. The device should REPORT camera power state; today it reports nothing, which is why this took a\n   battery curve to find. Belongs with fw-state-the-schedule\'s "state it, do not infer it".\n4. The real fix is camera-rtc-recovery: the camera must be able to power itself DOWN, not only up.'}
2026-07-28 17:01:54,695 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B81 CONFIRMED, OURS — the ledger drops the FAILURE REASON mesh-gw already gives us (error_name/res), and pushes null into acks[] for non-terminal statuses', 'notes': 'Peter, 2026-07-28: "is there a problem with mesh-gw not correctly, or not with enough detail a\nmessage delivery status?"\n\nAnswer: the detail mostly EXISTS and WE DROP IT. Two distinct defects.\n\n## B81a — the reason is lifted, then thrown away\n\nmesh-gw\'s contract carries the reason, and gw.js:154-159 correctly lifts it:\n\n  { kind:\'status\', packetId, status, errorName, errorReason, res, raw }\n\nBut the LEDGER stores only the bare status string. So the ack array reads\n\n  [\'queue_failed\',\'queue_failed\',\'queue_failed\']\n\nwhen what mesh-gw actually knew was\n\n  routing_ack failed error=PKI_SEND_FAIL_PUBLIC_KEY\n\nTHAT IS WHY B79 COST A WHOLE DAY. Every layer said "queue_failed"; the reason lived only in a\nDEBUG line in mesh-gw\'s journal, which nothing consumes and nobody greps until desperate. The\nservice HAD the field in hand and discarded it before writing the row.\n\nFix: persist errorName/errorReason/res alongside the status, and surface the reason in the ack\nsummary and the cmd stats line. A refusal that says WHY is the difference between a day and a\nminute.\n\n## B81b — null in acks[]\n\nLive, this minute, on a bench ping:\n\n  ping trying tries=1 sends=1 acks=[None]\n\nThe status stream for that packet was sent -> queued -> relayed. `relayed` is DELIBERATELY not\nterminal — butler.js says so: "an intermediate node acking ONE HOP (from_num != to_num), so it is\nevidence of movement, never of arrival". Correct call. But the row still gets a null pushed into\nacks[], which:\n  - pollutes the array a consumer reads,\n  - makes acks.length disagree with the number of REAL outcomes, which\n    sent-is-not-transmitted (4b4d5fb) now relies on for transmitted/unknownSent.\nSo this quietly corrupts the numbers I published an hour ago.\n\nFix: do not push a non-terminal status into acks[]; keep it as progress (there is already a\n`relayed` concept) or record it in a separate field.\n\n## What IS genuinely thin on mesh-gw\'s side\n\nThe SUCCESS path is coarse: sent / queued / relayed / delivered, with `relayed` carrying only\nfrom_num. There is no per-hop detail and no reason on the good path — but that is far less\nimportant than the failure reason, which mesh-gw already provides and we discard.\n\n## Not fixed yet\n\nRecorded now because Peter asked and because a unit swap is imminent. B81b is the more urgent of\nthe two: it corrupts a number that is already published.'}
2026-07-28 17:14:11,653 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'no-accidental-public-broadcast', 'title': 'URGENT: an undefined `to` slips past the channel-0 broadcast guard and puts a DIRECTED command on the PUBLIC primary channel', 'steps': ['Investigate: DONE. gw.js BROADCAST = null; the guard is `to === BROADCAST && channel === 0`. _addressed() returns {to: num, channel: 0} for a directed send, and an UNDEFINED num passes `=== null`, then `body.to = undefined` is dropped by JSON.stringify - so the POST is byte-identical to a broadcast. Measured: one such send at 13:53:52 today, to=!ffffffff channel=0, right after the gateway switch when the roster was stale.', 'Document: spec. The guard must catch null AND undefined, and a DIRECTED send must REFUSE rather than degrade when it has no usable address.', 'Implement: `to == null` for the broadcast test, plus an explicit refusal when a directed send has a non-finite `to`.', 'Observe: assert all three cases - null blocked, undefined blocked, a real num sent. And confirm zero channel-0 broadcasts afterwards.', 'Track: hash, run all suites, commit.']}
2026-07-28 18:06:59,380 337709 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'wake-lifecycle-log', 'title': 'Peter: the log should read as a wake TIMELINE — window opens, beat received, window closes, and the pointer alongside', 'steps': ["Investigate: DONE. We log the wake (anchor moved), the butler's 'window open' ONLY when it has something to deliver, and rotator armed/restored. Missing: an unconditional window OPEN/CLOSE pair, and a line per BEAT received. The beat now arrives on port 260 and is learned silently.", "Document: spec. One line per boundary, each carrying a number; driven by the model's own nextWake+windowMs so the line says what we PREDICTED and the beat line says what actually arrived.", 'Implement: window OPEN/CLOSE from a timer, beat RECEIVED in the 260 handler with the reporting radio and signal.', 'Observe: a full wake reads as open -> beat -> close on both units, with the rotator interleaved for the garage.', 'Track: hash, run all suites, commit.']}
