2026-07-28 20:29:39,002 8170 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B83 CONFIRMED (services, IN SCOPE, NOT FIXED — outside the attached spec): 2 of 32 suites are red because I shipped the services half of `envevery`/`tele.env` and then reverted the firmware half', 'description': "**Measured 2026-07-28 ~20:30Z, all 32 suites run individually:**\n\n```\nFAIL test/api-parity.js  — api-coverage.js names verbs the firmware does not accept: envevery\nFAIL test/config.js      — schema: 23 fields from the file (got 22)\n```\n\n**Cause, and it is mine.** The beat-line firmware work added an `envevery` verb and a `tele.env`\nconfig field. I committed the SERVICES half — `mesh/lib/api-coverage.js:31` and\n`mesh/lib/config.js:60-61` — and then reverted the FIRMWARE half out (saved to `/tmp/main-beatline.cpp`,\nwhich will not survive a reboot). `device/docs/device-api.yaml` and `device/docs/config-schema.json`\nregenerated without it, and both parity tests correctly caught the mismatch.\n\nThe tests are RIGHT. They exist to catch exactly this — a service surface promising a verb no\nfirmware accepts — and they did their job. Nothing here is a test defect.\n\n**NOT FIXED, deliberately.** `mesh/lib/api-coverage.js` and `mesh/lib/config.js` are not in the\nscope of the attached spec (`per-unit-dm-fallback`), so under the standing rule this is reported,\nnot fixed. It also should not be fixed blind: removing the services references throws away work\nthat becomes correct the moment the firmware lands, and restoring the firmware is flash-gated and\nanother session's.\n\n**Two ways to close it, Peter's call:**\n1. Land the beat-line firmware (recover `/tmp/main-beatline.cpp` FIRST — a reboot loses it) and\n   both suites go green with the feature intact.\n2. Revert `envevery`/`tele.env` from `api-coverage.js` and `config.js` under their own task+spec.\n\n**Also record:** the red was not visible until every suite was run individually. `npm test` in\n`mesh/` runs `test/skeleton.js` ONLY — one file of 32 — and exits 0. A green `npm test` in this\nrepo means almost nothing, and I have quoted it as evidence before today."}
