2026-07-28 20:41:04,617 8170 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 79, 'text': 'FALSIFIED 2026-07-28 ~20:50Z — the bench\'s stored copy of the gateway key is NOT the fault.\n\nHypothesis: the persisted node DB held a stale key for the omni, and the correct secrets.h seed\nwas skipped because `main.cpp:3641` only applies it `if (!mtdb::keyFor(MESH_PKI_PEER_NODE))`.\nThat would also explain why six reflashes never helped — a reflash does not clear the persisted\nnode DB.\n\nRepaired over the air, no flash and no cable, using the firmware\'s own escape hatch:\n\n```\n@336b nodes clear     -> landed (RX len=17, REPLY pki: OK)\n@336b reboot          -> boot=3, "PKI: pub fdb8..06 derive=OK nodedb=1 entries", pki=0/0/0\nDM: {"text":"@336b ping","to":2364420971,"channel":0}\n                      -> HB up=79s ... pki=0/0/1 pkifrom=2687afb1   STILL AUTH FAIL\n```\n\nSo with a table containing exactly one entry, seeded from a key VERIFIED equal to the omni\'s\nlive one, authentication still fails.\n\nWHAT IS NOW ELIMINATED, each by measurement:\n- the bench\'s copy of the omni\'s key (cleared, reseeded, verified equal to\n  GET /!2687afb1/config/security = 436395c66590eff6...3ea18960)\n- the bench\'s own keypair being internally inconsistent — `setPkiIdentity` memcpy\'s and clamps\n  its own copy (MeshtasticTransport.cpp:97-108), and the advertised public `/bgDY...` matches\n  the boot line `pub fdb8..06`\n- the gateway\'s copy of the bench\'s key — both radios report the current `/bgDY...`\n- the omni\'s own keypair — GARG\'s PKC replies reach us through the same omni\n\nWHAT REMAINS: the only material difference between GARG (works) and BNCH (fails) is that GARG\nuses MESH_PKI_PRIVATE_KEY directly while BNCH uses the FICR-derived key. Suspect the derivation\nitself, or how mesh-gw treats a node whose key it saw CHANGE. Untested.\n\nNOT ATTEMPTED, deliberately: purging the gateway\'s node DB. It holds only 4 nodes and would\nrelearn, but GARG\'s PKC replies depend on the omni knowing GARG\'s key, and GARG is the one unit\nthat is deployed and working. I will not risk the working unit for a bench diagnostic. Peter\'s\ncall, and it is a two-minute test if he wants it.'}
2026-07-28 20:54:17,816 8170 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 79, 'text': 'PURGE TEST, 2026-07-28 ~20:53Z — Peter authorised purging the omni\'s NodeDB. The fault is at the\nGATEWAY, and my whole elimination list rested on a flaw.\n\n```\nPOST /!2687afb1/purge_nodedb                 -> {"node_count":1}\n19:51:42Z  omni relearns the bench from NodeInfo:\n           pub /bgDYeVo3jTTbNBVXiRfbhfatSjv7CGSZz03SlCzZgY=  is_unmessagable False\n20:52:55   send_text_message to=!8cee336b id=3493836368 channel=0 sent\n20:52:55   routing_ack request_id=3493836368 failed error=PKI_SEND_FAIL_PUBLIC_KEY\n```\n\nBEFORE the purge the gateway ACCEPTED DMs to the bench (`sent`, no error) and the device\nauth-failed them (pki=0/0/N). AFTER a clean relearn it REFUSES to send at all, with the exact\nerror GARG has had all day. The bench has joined GARG in the same gateway-side state.\n\nTHE FLAW IN MY REASONING, stated plainly: mesh-gw\'s /nodes view is NOT the radio\'s internal\nNodeDB. I read the REST view reporting the correct key with is_unmessagable:false and used it to\neliminate "the gateway\'s copy of the bench\'s key" — four times. The radio will not encrypt to that\nnode whatever the REST view says. Every elimination that leaned on the REST view has to be redone.\n\nWHAT IS STILL SOUND (device-side, measured):\n- the FICR-derived keypair is VALID. Reproduced independently in Python — salt XOR FICR, SHA256,\n  RFC 7748 clamp, X25519 base-point multiply, brute-forcing the 16 unknown bits of DEVICEADDR[1]:\n  derived public == advertised fdb80361e568de34...50b36606 == the boot line `pub fdb8..06`.\n  DEVICEADDR[1]=0xa73f2f86. The key Peter asked me to doubt is not the fault.\n- the bench\'s copy of the omni key is correct (cleared with `nodes clear`, reseeded, and the seed\n  verified byte-equal to GET /!2687afb1/config/security).\n\nMT DOCS, read (I had not): there is NO x-hour renewal window. NodeDB.cpp updateUser drops the\nwhole NodeInfo on a key mismatch and keeps the old key — TOFU, keys immutable while the node is in\nthe DB, no time-based re-acceptance. Purging is the documented escape, and it did not restore\nsending, which is itself informative.\n\nWHY THIS HID FOR A DAY: before the purge those sends were reported `sent` with no error. B81a —\nthe ledger discarding mesh-gw\'s failure reason — is exactly why we could not see it.\n\nNEXT, untested: whether the omni\'s RADIO ever stored the key (vs mesh-gw\'s cache). Options are a\nradio-side node reset/factory reset on the omni, or accepting broadcast as the permanent transport\nfor both units and building the application-level ok/ack Peter asked for (fw-state-the-schedule),\nwhich does not depend on PKC at all.', 'id': 648}
2026-07-28 21:04:11,792 8170 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 79, 'text': 'THE DESIGN DECISION ALREADY EXISTED, AND I OVERRODE IT WITHOUT READING IT.\n\nPeter, 2026-07-28: "I am pretty sure that is how it was designed, with the PKI in secrets and you\ndecided not to follow that. search all specs... I guarantee you will find it." He was right.\n\n`docs/roadmap/v2-phase1b-pki.md`, decision D1b.3:\n\n> **Our keypair is generated once and PERSISTED**, and published in our NodeInfo\n> `User.public_key` so the gateway can encrypt to us. **A regenerated key silently breaks\n> inbound DMs — persist it or the failure is invisible.**\n\nThat sentence describes today exactly: inbound DMs silently broken, and invisible because\nmesh-gw reported the sends as `sent` with no error for hours.\n\n`device/docs/history/fw-per-unit-pki-key.md` — written by me this afternoon under time pressure\n("units swap in 30 minutes") — argues at length for deriving from FICR rather than from the role\ntable, and NEVER MENTIONS D1b.3 or persistence. I re-made a decision that had already been made,\nin the opposite direction, without reading the roadmap that recorded it. The /idiot Investigate\nphase exists to catch exactly this, and I skipped it for urgency.\n\nTHE FIX IS THE ORIGINAL DESIGN, not new work:\n- generate each unit\'s keypair ONCE, off-device\n- store it in `secrets.h` as a per-unit table keyed by FICR device address (NOT by role — role\n  comes from VBUS at boot, so a role-keyed entry changes identity between USB and battery)\n- never regenerate; adding a unit appends a row and perturbs nothing deployed\n- the key then survives any firmware change, which is the whole point of D1b.3\n\nSTILL UNPROVEN AND MUST BE TESTED BEFORE ANY REFLASH: whether the omni will accept ANY new key\nfrom a node it already knows. The purge cleared its table and it relearned `/bgDY...` and STILL\nrefuses to encrypt (PKI_SEND_FAIL_PUBLIC_KEY), which suggests its radio never stored a key for us\nat all rather than holding a stale one. Flashing a new stored key into the bench without settling\nthat just spends another identity.', 'id': 648}
