2026-07-28 21:22:28,343 8170 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'fw-key-repair-on-authfail', 'title': 'FIRMWARE: a WRONG PKI key can never be repaired — the key-request bootstrap is wired to pkiRxNoKey, and every failure we get is pkiRxAuthFail', 'steps': ['Investigate: DONE. main.cpp:4329 triggers maybeRequestKey only when pkiRxNoKey changes. Measured all evening on the bench: pki=0/0/N — noKey is ZERO, every failure is authFail, all from pkifrom=2687afb1. The trigger watches a counter that never moves. And maybeRequestKey:1937 early-returns on mtdb::keyFor(node), so a WRONG key blocks its own repair. Survived 6 reflashes, an OTA `nodes clear`, a reboot, and a gateway NodeDB purge. Proved by restoring the link BY HAND with `@336b name BNCH` -> sendNodeInfo -> routing_ack delivered from=!8cee336b at 21:19:59, first PKC DM success since 15:52.', 'Document: spec written at device/docs/history/fw-key-repair-on-authfail.md — trigger on pkiRxAuthFail as well as pkiRxNoKey, and mtdb::forget(peer) before requesting so the keyFor guard cannot veto the repair. Scope is src/main.cpp only. D1b.3 (persist the key in secrets.h) is DELIBERATELY separate — one change per measurement.', 'Implement: replace the trigger block at main.cpp:4329-4333 with the two-counter version plus forget()+saveNodeDb() and a KEYBAD: log line.', 'Observe: induce a key mismatch, confirm KEYBAD: and KEYREQ: appear, then confirm a gateway PKC DM reaches routing_ack delivered with NO human command, and that pki= shows a non-zero FIRST field (a real decrypt). Today that recovery needed a hand-typed name command.', 'Track: hash the spec with ../scripts/spec-hash.py, run the device build, commit, and raise the behaviour change with the services session.']}
2026-07-28 21:22:31,854 8170 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_file_attach args={'file_path': 'device/docs/history/fw-key-repair-on-authfail.md', 'kind': 'plan', 'label': 'spec: repair a WRONG PKI key on authFail'}
2026-07-28 21:28:48,151 8170 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': "B84 CONFIRMED (device/, NOT FIXED — outside the spec's scope): mtdb::forget() does not evict the transport's peer key, so the over-the-air `nodes forget` / `nodes clear` escape hatch does not stop decryption", 'description': 'Found 2026-07-28 ~21:35Z while trying to INDUCE a key failure to test fw-key-repair-on-authfail.\n\n`@336b nodes forget 646426545` then a PKC DM from the gateway: the counters went\n`pki=1/0/0 -> 2/0/0`. **Both DMs decrypted normally.** The forget had no effect on decryption.\n\nCause: `syncPkiPeers` (`src/main.cpp:1447`) only ever ADDS —\n\n```c\nfor (uint8_t i = 0; i < mtdb::NODEDB_MAX; i++) {\n    const mtdb::Entry *e = mtdb::at(i);\n    if (e && e->nodeNum && e->haveKey) mesh.addPkiPeer(e->nodeNum, e->publicKey);\n}\n```\n\n— and `MeshtasticTransport` (`transport/src/MeshtasticTransport.h:152`) exposes `addPkiPeer` with\nno corresponding remove. The key therefore stays live in `_pkiPeers` until something calls\n`addPkiPeer` again for that node number. `mtdb::forget()` edits the persisted DB and the running\nsession keeps using the old key.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE TEST: `nodes clear` / `nodes forget` are described in the firmware as\n"the no-reflash escape hatch: inspect and repair the key table over the air... what makes a\nlearned-key design safe on a unit we cannot revisit." For a DEPLOYED unit that is the only repair\ntool there is, and it half works — it fixes what survives a reboot, not what is in use now. I used\nit tonight believing it had taken effect.\n\nFIX (device/, not ours to make): give the transport a `removePkiPeer(nodeNum)` (or a\n`clearPkiPeers()` + re-sync) and call it from the `nodes forget` / `nodes clear` handlers.\n\nDOES NOT INVALIDATE fw-key-repair-on-authfail (commit a859876): that path calls\n`mtdb::forget(peer)` to unblock the `keyFor` guard, and the transport\'s stale copy is REPLACED by\n`mesh.addPkiPeer(...)` inside handleNodeInfo\'s learnKey-success branch when the solicited NodeInfo\narrives. The chain completes without a reboot. But it does mean the authFail branch could not be\nexercised by hand tonight — recorded as NOT DEMONSTRATED in that spec rather than claimed.'}
