2026-07-28 21:30:14,527 8170 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'fw-forget-evicts-transport-key', 'title': "B84: `nodes forget` / `nodes clear` edit the persisted DB but do not stop decryption — the transport's peer table has no remove", 'steps': ['Investigate: read mtdb::forget/clear, the transport peer table and every caller of addPkiPeer/syncPkiPeers; establish exactly what survives a forget and for how long. Do not modify files.', 'Document: spec in device/docs/history/fw-forget-evicts-transport-key.md with the exact before/after and the scope.', 'Implement: only what the spec names.', 'Observe: static, functional and regression checks with real output, and verify the fix is REACHED by removing it and confirming the check fails.', 'Track: hash with ../scripts/spec-hash.py, build, commit, report SHA.']}
2026-07-28 21:31:13,092 8170 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': "B85 CONFIRMED (device/, same root as B84, NOT FIXED): the transport's 4-slot PKI peer table is add-only, so once full it permanently refuses every new peer until reboot", 'description': 'Found 2026-07-28 ~21:45Z during the B84 investigation. Distinct failure mode, same root cause\n(no remove), recorded separately because it fails differently.\n\n`transport/src/MeshtasticTransport.h:479-482`:\n\n```c\nstatic const uint8_t PKI_PEERS_N = 4;\nstruct PkiPeer { uint32_t node; uint8_t pub[32]; };\nPkiPeer  _pkiPeers[PKI_PEERS_N]{};\nuint8_t  _pkiPeerCount = 0;\n```\n\n`MeshtasticTransport.cpp:110-126` — `addPkiPeer` refreshes a known node, else appends, else:\n\n```c\nif (_pkiPeerCount >= PKI_PEERS_N)\n    return false;\n```\n\n`_pkiPeerCount` is **never decremented anywhere** — there is no remove, no eviction, no compaction.\nSo the table is monotonic: four distinct nodes ever seen, and every subsequent new peer is refused\nfor the rest of the boot. `mtdb` (the persisted DB) is larger and has eviction; the transport copy\nhas neither, so they diverge silently.\n\nCONSEQUENCE: a fifth peer — a replacement gateway, a second radio, a relay we start DMing — can\nnever be PKC-addressed, and `sendPki` fails its `!pkiPeerKey(to)` guard at `:143` with no\ndiagnostic. Recovery is a reboot, which on a deployed unit means a site visit.\n\nNOT YET OBSERVED IN THE FIELD: our units talk to one gateway, so the table holds 1-2 entries. This\nis a latent defect, recorded as CONFIRMED-by-reading rather than measured, and it is exactly the\nkind that surfaces on the day a gateway is swapped.\n\nFIX: the same `removePkiPeer(nodeNum)` / compaction that B84 needs, plus LRU eviction on a full\ntable rather than a silent refusal. Both belong to the `fw-forget-evicts-transport-key` task.'}
