2026-07-28 21:31:35,643 8170 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': "B85 CONFIRMED-BY-READING (device/, same root as B84, NOT FIXED): the transport's 4-slot PKI peer table is add-only, so once full it permanently refuses every new peer until reboot", 'description': 'Found 2026-07-28 ~21:45Z during the B84 investigation. Distinct failure mode, same root cause\n(no remove), recorded separately because it fails differently.\n\n`transport/src/MeshtasticTransport.h:479-482`:\n\n```c\nstatic const uint8_t PKI_PEERS_N = 4;\nstruct PkiPeer { uint32_t node; uint8_t pub[32]; };\nPkiPeer  _pkiPeers[PKI_PEERS_N]{};\nuint8_t  _pkiPeerCount = 0;\n```\n\n`MeshtasticTransport.cpp:110-126` — addPkiPeer refreshes a known node, else appends, else:\n\n```c\nif (_pkiPeerCount >= PKI_PEERS_N)\n    return false;\n```\n\n`_pkiPeerCount` is NEVER decremented anywhere — no remove, no eviction, no compaction. The table\nis monotonic: four distinct nodes ever seen, and every subsequent new peer is refused for the rest\nof the boot. `mtdb` (the persisted DB) is larger and HAS eviction; the transport copy has neither,\nso the two diverge silently.\n\nCONSEQUENCE: a fifth peer — a replacement gateway, a second radio, a relay we start DMing — can\nnever be PKC-addressed. `sendPki` then fails its `!pkiPeerKey(to)` guard (MeshtasticTransport.cpp:143)\nand returns false with no diagnostic. Recovery is a reboot, which on a deployed unit is a site visit.\n\nSTATUS IS CONFIRMED-BY-READING, NOT MEASURED. Our units talk to one gateway so the table holds 1-2\nentries today; I have not observed this in the field and have not forced it. It is latent, and it\nis the kind that surfaces on the day a gateway is swapped.\n\nFIX: the same removePkiPeer(nodeNum) + compaction that B84 needs, plus LRU eviction on a full table\ninstead of a silent refusal. Both belong to task `fw-forget-evicts-transport-key`.'}
2026-07-28 21:46:31,288 8170 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'fw-answer-broadcast-by-broadcast', 'title': 'A command that ARRIVED by broadcast must be ANSWERED by broadcast — remove PKI from the command loop entirely, so no key state can silence a unit 8000 km away for 6 months', 'steps': ['Investigate: trace sendReply/handleCommand and every reply path; establish exactly how the reply transport is chosen today and what decides PKC vs broadcast. Read, do not modify.', 'Document: spec at device/docs/history/fw-answer-broadcast-by-broadcast.md - reply on the transport the command arrived on, with the acceptance number stated first.', 'Implement: only what the spec names.', 'Observe: send a broadcast command, confirm the reply arrives at pac-host with PKI unusable; verify the fix is REACHED by reverting it and confirming the reply is lost again.', 'Track: hash, build, flash, commit, report SHA.']}
