2026-07-28 21:51:49,873 8170 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'B86 CONFIRMED, OURS, HIGH: one reply settles MULTIPLE pending requests, and a `pong` is accepted as the answer to `status` — the correlator never checks the reply type against the verb', 'description': 'Peter, 2026-07-28 20:50Z: "I just sent status to bench, it replied with a ping answer." Confirmed\nfrom the ledger — my three concurrent test pings exposed it, they did not cause it.\n\n```\n20:50:23  ms54q4by.k  command ping    -> done  {"type":"pong","upt":134,"rssi":-42,"snr":7.5}\n20:50:27  ms54r16g.l  command status  -> done  {"type":"pong","upt":134,"rssi":-42,"snr":7.5}\n20:48:51  ms54odzd.h  command ping    -> done  {"type":"pong","upt":41,"rssi":-41,"snr":6}\n20:48:55  ms54oedw.i  command ping    -> done  {"type":"pong","upt":41,"rssi":-41,"snr":6}\n```\n\nByte-identical result objects on DIFFERENT request ids, including one whose verb was `status`. So:\n\n1. a single inbound reply can settle MORE THAN ONE pending request, and\n2. the correlator does not require the reply\'s `type` to correspond to the request\'s `verb` —\n   `{"type":"pong"}` was accepted as the answer to `status`.\n\nWHY THIS IS HIGH, not cosmetic. It is the exact failure class the project charter names as the\nreason this repo exists: "a reply matched against the wrong request." With the unit 2.5 km away\n(and 8000 km from Peter for 6 months from ~2026-08), the consequence is that a `config set` or a\n`status` can report SUCCESS on the strength of an unrelated ping reply, and we would believe a\nsetting had landed when it had not. Every remote diagnosis rests on this correlation being sound.\n\nNOT YET ESTABLISHED (do not assume in the fix):\n- whether the match is on packet/replyId, on unit+pending-order, or a fallback "first pending\n  request for this unit" path. The code has not been read yet — this entry records the OBSERVED\n  behaviour only.\n- whether the duplicate settle happens at the ledger layer or in the mesh reply handler.\n- whether a reply arriving for an ALREADY-settled request can resurrect or overwrite it.\n\nSCOPE: services, `mesh/` — ours to fix, under its own task and spec.\n\nRELATED: B81b (non-terminal `relayed` statuses push null into acks[]) touches the same settle path.\nCheck whether one fix covers both before writing two.'}
2026-07-28 21:59:43,448 8170 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'B87 CONFIRMED (device/, HIGH — would have gone to the garage tomorrow): the reply buffer is sized to the FRAME cap, not the frame cap MINUS the protobuf envelope, so any long reply (`status`) is refused at the sender and never reaches the air', 'description': 'Peter, 2026-07-28 ~20:5xZ: "I just sent status to bench, it replied with a ping answer" and then\n"so basically the only thing the device now responds to is a ping".\n\nMEASURED ON THE DEVICE CONSOLE — one `status` by broadcast, nothing else in flight:\n\n```\nRX: from=0x2687afb1 id=0xba15b407 port=1 len=12 rssi=-41 snr=6.0\nREPLY  : id=0xf71e353f FAILED\nRX: from=0x2687afb1 id=0x1d6e4983 port=1 len=12 rssi=-42 snr=7.2\nREPLY  : id=0xf71e353f FAILED\n```\n\nLedger, same command isolated: `verb=status state=failed tries=5 result=null`, sentIds 5 entries.\n`ping` answers every time; `status` never does.\n\nCAUSE, from the code rather than inference:\n- `src/main.cpp:2329` — `static char reply[237]; // sized to the send() payload cap`\n- `transport/src/MeshtasticTransport.h:357` — `MAX_PAYLOAD = 237`\n- `MeshtasticTransport.cpp:178-181` — the reply is placed in a `meshtastic_Data` together with\n  `portnum`, `request_id`, `reply_id` and `want_response`, and THE WHOLE PROTOBUF is encoded into\n  `static uint8_t plain[MAX_PAYLOAD]`:\n\n```c\nif (!pb_encode(&os, meshtastic_Data_fields, &data))\n    return false;          // -> send() false -> sendReply reports FAILED\n```\n\nSo the text cap must be MAX_PAYLOAD **minus** the Data envelope (field tags + the three uint32s +\nthe length prefix, ~15-20 bytes), and minus a further 12 for PKC (PKI_OVERHEAD 8 + extraNonce 4 —\nthe roadmap already states 231 -> 219 effective for PKI). `reply[237]` can therefore build a reply\nthat can never be encoded, and the failure is at the SENDER: nothing is transmitted, no retry\nhelps, and from the service it is indistinguishable from a unit that did not hear us.\n\nWHY HIGH: `status` is the primary remote-diagnosis verb, and this unit is swapped into the garage\n2026-07-29 with Peter 8000 km away for 6 months from ~2026-08. A unit that answers `ping` and\nnothing else is a unit you cannot diagnose.\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED YET: the exact byte length of the status reply and therefore the exact overflow\nmargin; which other verbs exceed the cap (`config`, `schema`, `nodes` listing and `status mem` are\nall candidates). Only `ping` and `status` have been measured. Do NOT assume the rest are fine.\n\nFIX (device/, own task+spec): derive the text cap from the transport rather than hard-coding 237 —\nexpose the usable text budget for plain and PKC, size `reply[]` to the smaller, and make an\noversize reply VISIBLE (truncate with a marker or emit a short error reply) instead of failing\nsilently at the sender.\n\nRELATED: B86 (a reply settling a request it does not belong to) is SEPARATE and still open — it is\nwhat made a `status` appear to be answered by a `pong` while pings were concurrent.'}
