2026-07-29 06:40:02,229 18559 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 85, 'text': 'SCOPE CORRECTED 2026-07-28 ~21:40Z — Peter: "too long to encode? they are supposed to be\nchunked over port 261." He was right that I had mis-scoped this, and two of the four verbs I\nlisted are NOT defects at all.\n\nNOT DEFECTS — no text reply BY DESIGN, verified in the firmware, my sweep harness was wrong:\n\n- `debug` (main.cpp:2407): "On-demand diagnostics. The DEBUG frame IS the answer, so no text\n  reply — a text ack would spend the airtime this change exists to save (same reasoning as\n  `chunk pull`)." followed by `reply[0] = 0;`. The sweep\'s "GOT None / MISMATCH want debug" is\n  the harness expecting a text reply that is deliberately not sent. **B88 IS INVALID** — see\n  its own entry, disproved with evidence, not deleted.\n- `sch` (main.cpp:3089): "one positional-array page on PAC_ALARM_APP (port 260); the frame IS\n  the reply (like debug/config), so no text reply" followed by `reply[0] = 0;`. The sweep\'s\n  "failed / NO REPLY" is correct behaviour.\n\nSTILL REAL, and the only one MEASURED at the device rather than inferred:\n\n- `status` — device console, one command, nothing else in flight:\n  `RX: from=0x2687afb1 port=1 len=12` then `REPLY  : id=0xf71e353f FAILED`, twice. The send is\n  refused at the sender; nothing reaches the air.\n\nUNVERIFIED, do not assume either way: `config` and `env` build text into `reply[]`\n(`buildConfig`, main.cpp:2116) and read `failed` in the sweep, but I have NOT watched either on\nthe console, and the sweep\'s poll window was shorter than the retry cycle so a `failed` row there\nis not trustworthy evidence. Measure before fixing.\n\nTHE MECHANISM CLAIM ALSO NEEDS RE-TESTING. I asserted "reply too long to encode": the text goes\ninto a `meshtastic_Data` whose payload type is PB_BYTES_ARRAY_T(233) (generated/meshtastic/mesh.pb.h:846)\nwhile `reply[237]` (main.cpp:2329) and `MAX_PAYLOAD` (MeshtasticTransport.h:357) are both 237 —\nso a reply between 234 and 236 bytes cannot be encoded. That is consistent with the observed\nfailure but I have NOT measured the actual length of the status reply, so the mechanism is\nPLAUSIBLE, not proven. Measure the length first.\n\nPeter\'s design point stands regardless: a payload that does not fit a frame belongs on the chunk\npath (port 261) or a paged port-260 reply like `sch`, not silently refused at the sender.'}
