2026-07-29 08:14:03,447 18559 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'verified-hops-traceroute', 'title': 'Own traceroute and publish hopsVerified — MOVE it out of node-dash rather than duplicate it, because header hops are claimed and some nodes lie', 'steps': ["Investigate: mesh-gw exposes POST /{node_id}/traceroute (seen in its openapi). Establish what it returns, what it costs in airtime, and how a route maps to a hop count. Read node-dash's approach via the xsession channel FIRST - they have run this for months and know which nodes misreport. Do not reimplement blind.", 'Document: spec. hopsVerified{value, at, path, method} as ADDITIVE data alongside the claimed `hops`, never replacing it - one is an assertion, the other a measurement. State the cadence and the airtime budget BEFORE writing code: on a duty-cycled link where our units are deaf ~99% of the time, a traceroute is a real cost.', 'Implement: only what the spec names.', 'Observe: hopsVerified matches an independently run traceroute for at least one node whose header hops is WRONG - that node is the whole point, and a test against an honest node proves nothing.', 'Track: hash, suites, commit, and tell node-dash it can retire its own traceroute - the move is not complete while two implementations are transmitting.']}
2026-07-29 08:15:51,877 18559 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'verified-hops-traceroute', 'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'TRIGGERS, NOT A SCHEDULE. Peter, 2026-07-29: "we cannot just keep running traceroute, it needs\ntriggers, i.e. has the rssi or snr suddenly changed... I dont want to start flooding the mesh with\ntraceroutes."\n\nSCOPE FIRST — the biggest saving of all. We care about the route to OUR UNITS and the relays in\nthat path. Tracerouting the other ~200 mesh nodes is pure airtime for data no consumer reads.\nThat alone reduces "the mesh" to two targets.\n\nTRIGGERS — take a route measurement when something INDICATES the route changed. Every one of these\nis detectable from data we already keep:\n- SIGNAL STEP CHANGE (Peter\'s example). A step, not drift: e.g. >10 dB against the recent median\n  for that radio. radios.<addr>.direct.{rssi,snr,at} already exists per radio with timestamps.\n- THE RELAY CHANGED. A frame arrives `via` a different node than last time — that is a route\n  change by definition, and detecting it is free.\n- DIRECT <-> RELAYED TRANSITION. We already keep counts.direct and counts.relayed per radio.\n- CLAIMED `hops` CHANGED. Cheap to watch; and disagreement between `hops` and `hopsVerified` is\n  itself the signal that a node misreports.\n- DELIVERY GOT WORSE. A run of failed/unacked commands to a unit that was previously fine — route\n  change is one of the few explanations, and the acks summary already carries it.\n- FIRST SIGHTING. No verified route held yet.\n- KNOWN TOPOLOGY EVENTS. Gateway switched, antenna moved, rotator repointed, unit swapped. WE\n  cause these, so no detection is needed.\n\nBUDGET, so triggers cannot stampede:\n- per-node cooldown: nothing more often than ~30 min however many triggers fire\n- global ceiling per hour across all targets\n- NEVER while an image transfer is running or commands are queued for that unit — those matter\n  more, and a traceroute competes for the same airtime\n- for our SLEEPING units, fire only just after we have HEARD them, when the window is open. A\n  traceroute into a deaf radio wastes the transmission and the wait.\n- staleness BACKSTOP, not a schedule: if unverified for ~24 h, take one. Bounds drift without\n  polling.\n\nTHE NUMBERS ABOVE ARE GUESSES AND MUST NOT BE SHIPPED AS FACTS. The airtime cost of a traceroute\non this link (SF11/BW250, ~1.6 s per frame) is NOT measured. FIRST ACTION: run ONE traceroute,\ntime it, count the frames it puts on air. Every threshold and ceiling here is provisional until\nthat exists — including whether 30 min is generous or absurd.\n\nALL THRESHOLDS CONFIGURABLE, per the standing rule that every timing value is declared in\nsettings.js and never hard-coded.'}
