2026-07-29 09:11:41,836 27975 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'CONFIRMED: fmtAgo() silently clamps FUTURE timestamps to "0s ago" — a scheduled instant renders as "now"', 'description': '**Status: CONFIRMED** (static read, `src/format.js:95-102`, verified 2026-07-29)\n\n```js\nexport function fmtAgo(ts, nowSec = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000)) {\n  if (!n(ts) || ts <= 0) return null;\n  const s = Math.max(0, nowSec - ts);\n  ...\n  return `${s}s ago`;\n```\n\n`Math.max(0, nowSec - ts)` means **any timestamp in the future produces `"0s ago"`** — not null, not an error, not "in Xs". A plausible-looking wrong answer, which is the class of defect the whole reachability work order exists to eliminate.\n\n**Why it matters now:** pac-host publishes `nextWake`, a **future** instant. Feeding it to `fmtAgo` would render an upcoming wake window as "0s ago" — i.e. "the unit is awake right now" — which is the exact opposite of the truth and unfalsifiable from the page.\n\n**Not yet known:** whether any *existing* caller already passes a future timestamp. Not audited. Every current caller passes `last_heard`-family values, which are historical, so no live defect is claimed — only the latent trap.\n\n**Disposition:** `fmtAgo` returning null (or a distinct "in Xs" via a separate `fmtUntil`) for future instants is the fix. `fmtUptime(sec)` at `src/format.js:31-41` already formats a bare duration and is reusable for the countdown half.\n\nFound during Phase 1 of `node-page-reachability`. Guarded *within* that task at the call site; the shared helper is left alone because changing it touches every page.'}
2026-07-29 09:11:51,820 27975 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': "UNVERIFIED: pac-host _statusesEqual JSON-compares units[] — new per-fact At timestamps may make the 'change' event fire on nearly every 30s poll", 'description': '**Status: UNVERIFIED** — mechanism read from source, frequency NOT measured.\n\n`src/pac-host.js:30-35`:\n\n```js\nfunction _statusesEqual(a, b) {\n  return a.status === b.status\n    && a.available === b.available\n    && JSON.stringify(a.modules) === JSON.stringify(b.modules)\n    && JSON.stringify(a.units) === JSON.stringify(b.units);\n}\n```\n\nThe whole `units` array is JSON-compared. **Any** byte differing anywhere makes `changed` true, which emits `change` (`:83`), which `src/ws-relay.js:330` turns into a full `connectMessage()` **broadcast to every connected browser**.\n\n**Why this may now be different:** services added per-fact age siblings on 2026-07-29 (`hopsAt`, `rssiAt`, `snrAt`, `positionAt` — commit `10fb7d9`). Those instants advance whenever a packet is heard. Previously the comparison was over slower-moving fields, so `change` fired rarely; it may now fire on most 30 s polls.\n\n**What is NOT known — do not report this as a live defect:**\n- the actual before/after `change` rate. Not measured.\n- whether the resulting broadcast volume matters at all. Two units and a handful of browsers may make it irrelevant.\n\nThis is recorded as a *thing to measure*, not a fault found. Log line to count: `pac-host` `status: ...` at `src/pac-host.js:82`, which prints on every `change`.\n\n**Interaction with `node-page-reachability`:** that task deliberately uses this same `change` event to hint `node_status` refreshes (spec §6). If the event is noisier than assumed, the existing 1 s per-node throttle in `_hintNodeStatus` (`src/ws-relay.js:120-131`) absorbs it — but that assumption is untested and is the reason this entry exists.'}
