2026-07-29 11:08:52,686 28111 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B91 CONFIRMED — wakesExpected is a FENCEPOST off-by-one; the ratio can exceed 100% forever.\n  mesh/index.js:999 vs :1010. Raised by node-dash (xsession #30), confirmed live by me, not taken\n  from a note:\n      BNCH  wakesSeen 6  wakesExpected 5   120%\n      GARG  wakesSeen 2  wakesExpected 5    40%\n  CAUSE: the two sides count DIFFERENT THINGS from the same instant.\n      wakesSeen     = wake ANCHORS observed since service start (EVENTS)\n      wakesExpected = floor((now - this._stats.since) / m.beat)   (ELAPSED INTERVALS)\n  N events span N-1 intervals, so a unit that wakes as we start reads 1 of 0, 2 of 1, 6 of 5 —\n  permanently exactly one ahead. NOT the 110% fixed earlier today: that was round() + a changed\n  beat + counting an always-listening unit\'s telemetry. floor() fixed those three and left this.\n  GARG\'s 2-of-5 is NOT affected — that is a genuine miss and the signal is correct.\n  FIX (mine, next): anchor wakesExpected on the unit\'s FIRST OBSERVED WAKE, not service start, and\n  count opportunities inclusive; wakesSince becomes that first-wake instant so the basis of the\n  ratio is the ratio\'s own anchor. Both sides must count the same fenceposts from the same instant.\n  node-dash is rendering "12 of 11" VERBATIM on the node page until fixed, deliberately, rather\n  than cover our defect with reconciling wording. Agreed — leave it ugly.\n\nB92 CONFIRMED — txRadio.state is read ONCE AT BOOT and never refreshed. Worst-aged field we publish.\n  mesh/index.js:242 _txRadio() reads this._radios; :1561 assigns it; the ONLY caller of\n  _refreshRadios() is :235, in startup. Never called again for the life of the process.\n  So txRadio.state answers "what was true when pac-host booted", republished as current — in the\n  one field whose stated purpose (per its own comment, B49) is "so a consumer can see a dead\n  transmitter". It would still report READY for a radio that died an hour ago.\n  This is the exact defect class B49 recorded: a config file\'s opinion of a radio vs the wire.\n  We fixed it by publishing mesh-gw\'s state, then froze that state at boot.\n  Found while answering node-dash\'s COMPUTED-vs-REMEMBERED question. They declined to classify it\n  ("READY reads like a live probe but might be a cached connection state") and were right to.\n  Told them not to render it as a live readiness claim until fixed. Queued 2nd, ahead of the\n  beat/slp At siblings, because a stale READY is an actively WRONG answer to "is there anything to\n  send through", whereas an undated beat is merely undated.\n\nB93 NOT OURS — CMD_DROP implemented on the camera, absent from the RAK. Raised with device\n  session (xsession device--services #10), not fixed here.\n  Camera implements CMD_DROP 0x07. device/lib/mt-chunk/src/M5CameraSource.h implements\n  CMD_INFO 0x01, CMD_CAPTURE 0x02, CMD_SEEK 0x03, CMD_SLEEP 0x04 — and omits it.\n  CONSEQUENCE: nothing frees a captured image, the camera queue never returns to 0, so CMD_SLEEP\n  lands in LIGHT sleep (RAM+PSRAM retained) not DEEP (~10 uA). Only escape is MAX_HOLD_MS = 2 HOURS.\n  A command implemented on one side and unknown to the other, with no document describing the\n  pair — the failure class the project charter names.\n\nB94 UNVERIFIED — the camera\'s ~22 mA is ~20x what light sleep should cost. Mechanism UNKNOWN.\n  Measured on the LTC4150, battery only, USB disconnected: ~30 mA total with camera vs ~7.8 mA\n  without. B93 explains why it is not in DEEP sleep; it does NOT explain the magnitude — light\n  sleep with PSRAM retained should be ~1 mA.\n  HYPOTHESIS, NOT A FINDING: the camera ext0-wakes on its UART RX going LOW (pullup enabled,\n  timercam-chunk/src/main.cpp:355-357). That pin is wired to the RAK\'s UART TX. If the RAK\'s TX is\n  deinitialised or floats/drives LOW while the RAK sleeps, the camera is woken the instant it\n  sleeps, forever. Read from the CAMERA side only — I have NOT verified what the RAK\'s pin does\n  and will not assert it. Asked the device session (#10).\n  DISCRIMINATOR NOW EXISTS: the camera\'s boot LED is patterned by wake cause (1 flash = ext0,\n  2 = RTC timer, 3 = cold boot), so a repeated SINGLE flash IS a wake loop, steady-on is\n  never-sleeping, and one long pulse then dark clears the camera entirely — in which case the\n  22 mA is elsewhere and this hypothesis dies. Peter observing. DEPLOYMENT-BLOCKING: 30 mA is\n  ~3 days on the pack against a six-month deployment.'}
