2026-07-29 11:28:43,136 28111 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B95 CONFIRMED — mesh/test/api-parity.js FAILS, and has been failing before today\'s work.\n  "api-coverage.js names verbs the firmware does not accept: envevery"\n  PROVEN PRE-EXISTING: git stash\'d my wake-ratio changes and it failed identically on the stashed\n  tree, so it is not mine. Found while running all 32 suites for wake-ratio-fencepost.\n  WHAT IT MEANS: we publish a verb (`envevery`) in api-coverage.js that the firmware does not\n  accept. That is an API promising something nothing implements — the exact defect class the\n  project charter names, and the reason api-parity.js exists at all. The test is doing its job;\n  nobody is reading it.\n  NOT FIXED — outside the scope of the attached spec. Needs its own task: either the firmware\n  gained/lost the verb, or api-coverage.js was edited without the firmware.\n\nB96 CONFIRMED — mesh/test/config.js FAILS, also pre-existing.\n  "schema: 23 fields from the file (got 22)" at mesh/test/config.js:91\n  PROVEN PRE-EXISTING by the same stash test. The config schema declares 23 fields and the loader\n  produces 22, so one declared setting is not being read. Given this repo\'s rule that a declared\n  setting exists so it can be changed without a code edit, a field that never loads is a setting\n  that silently does nothing.\n  NOT FIXED — out of scope. Own task.\n\n  NOTE ON BOTH: 30 of 32 suites pass. These two were RED before this session\'s changes and are RED\n  after, unchanged. They are recorded here rather than left in a terminal scrollback because a\n  failing suite that everyone has learned to ignore is worse than no suite.\n\nB97 CONFIRMED — the camera draws 44.6 mA ISOLATED ON USB, which is MORE than the 22 mA attributed\n  to it while on the RAK. Measured on the LTC4150, camera alone, USB powered, RAK disconnected:\n      avg_ma 44.6 | used_mah 4.1 | in_mah 0.0 | run_secs 330 | mah_day 1070\n  in_mah 0.0 confirms this is consumption, not charging.\n  DO NOT TREAT 44.6 AS THE DEPLOYMENT FIGURE. This rig includes the TimerCAM\'s USB-serial bridge\n  chip, which is absent on battery. The absolute number is contaminated; the rig\'s value is that\n  the USB offset is CONSTANT, so a before/after DELTA across a sensor-standby change is valid even\n  though the baseline is not. Peter, 2026-07-29: "45ma includes the USB circuitry".\n  ALSO UNKNOWN AND MUST NOT BE ASSUMED: whether the camera was awake, light-sleeping or\n  deep-sleeping for this 5.5 min. Nothing was attached to tell us. On USB with no RAK the ext0 wake\n  line (Grove G4) is disconnected, and USB serial is a DIFFERENT uart — so once it deep sleeps\n  nothing can wake it but the 4 h RTC alarm or a reset. Establish the state before quoting this\n  number as any kind of sleep current.'}
