2026-07-29 11:35:39,808 28111 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B98 UNVERIFIED (reported by node-dash, not yet reproduced by me) — the images endpoint HANGS while\n  a pull is in flight. Their measurement, quoted: "your images endpoint currently HANGS — 25s,\n  http 000, zero bytes, while a pull is in flight."\n  http 000 with zero bytes after 25s is a connection that produced no response at all, not a 5xx.\n  WHY IT IS NOW CRITICAL rather than annoying: Peter\'s mesh-ownership directive closes port-stream\n  reads, which kills node-dash\'s plan to reassemble the port-261 push frames passively themselves\n  (the way their working predecessor did). With that off the table the Camera page has EXACTLY ONE\n  path to an image and it runs through this endpoint. Their words: "That moves your images work\n  from \'the last blocker\' to \'the only path\'."\n  NOT DIAGNOSED. I have not reproduced it and will not assert a cause. The obvious suspect is the\n  request awaiting the same path the in-flight pull holds, but that is a hypothesis and is written\n  here as one. A read endpoint that blocks for the duration of a transfer is wrong however it\n  happens.\n  REPRIORITISED because of it: images progress source + this hang now go AHEAD of B92 (txRadio\n  staleness). Told node-dash (#37).\n\nB99 NOTED, low priority — the camera prints a core-dump checksum error on every boot:\n      E (664) esp_core_dump_... Core dump data check failed:\n      Calculated checksum=\'470fabe2\'  Image checksum=\'2255368e\'\n  Seen on every reset captured 2026-07-29 while establishing the sleep state. Most likely an\n  uninitialised/never-written core dump partition rather than a real recorded crash, but that is a\n  GUESS and is recorded as one. Worth one look if the camera ever reboots unexpectedly in the\n  field, because if it IS a real dump it is the record of the crash that would explain it.\n  NOT investigated — outside the scope of any current task.\n\nCORRECTION TO B94, recorded because I had the mechanism wrong and the correction matters:\n  The camera\'s draw is NOT explained by it failing to reach deep sleep. MEASURED 2026-07-29 on the\n  LTC4150, camera alone, USB, RAK disconnected — the camera\'s OWN console says which branch it took:\n      === timercam-chunk === wake=0 (cold boot / button / reset)\n      idle 3004ms — sleeping\n      SLEEP deep (qn=0)\n  and the board still drew avg_ma 44.8 over 9 minutes (used_mah 6.66, in_mah 0.0, run_secs 534).\n  So the ESP32 IS in deep sleep — printed by the device, not inferred from a meter — and the draw\n  is entirely peripheral. CONTROL RUN, so the silence was not a dead port: with no reset the console\n  was silent and unresponsive to \'?\', and a reset via the adapter EN line immediately produced the\n  banner above. USB serial cannot wake it because ext0 watches the GROVE pin (G4), a different uart.\n  My earlier ext0-wake-loop hypothesis is DEAD, killed by Peter\'s observation of no LED flashes —\n  a wake loop would flash once per wake. The B93 missing-CMD_DROP light-sleep story does not\n  explain this either, because this measurement was taken with qn=0 in DEEP sleep.\n  WHAT REMAINS: the OV3660 sensor, powered continuously and never deinit\'d, with pin_pwdn = -1\n  ("not wired on this board", main.cpp:261) so it cannot be dropped by a pin. Independently\n  corroborated by users who moved the sensor to an AI Thinker board where PWDN IS wired: 22-24 mA\n  with it active, 2.7 mA with it powered down, "the real culprit of power consumption is OV3660".\n  FIX TO TEST: SCCB software standby via register 0x3008 (SYSTEM_CTROL0, bit[6] = software power\n  down) using esp_camera_sensor_get()->set_reg(). Value to be determined EMPIRICALLY — canonical\n  OV5640/OV3660 standby is 0x42, one forum report says 0x01 works; the rig can settle it.\n  NOTE ON THE RIG: 44.8 mA includes the TimerCAM USB-serial bridge, absent on battery. The absolute\n  figure is NOT a deployment number. The USB offset is CONSTANT, so a before/after DELTA is valid.'}
