2026-07-29 12:45:34,797 28111 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B102 CONFIRMED (observation) — EVERY failed image transfer today is pid 1, and pid 1 is\n  TEST_IMAGE_PID. The "GARG stalls at 6/32 chunks" investigation may have been chasing a test\n  pattern, not a photograph.\n  COUNTED from pac-host\'s log, 2026-07-29:\n      13 x  auto-adopt push pid 1 from !987ab80f   (GARG)\n      13 x  image pull: pid 1 from !987ab80f\n       1 x  image pull: pid 35688 from 336b        (BNCH — the ONLY transfer that SUCCEEDED)\n  OUTCOMES: every EDEADLINE and EXFER carries pid 1, at 1/32, 2/32, 4/32, 6/32, 6/32, 8/32, and\n  once "1/0" (count 0 = no manifest ever arrived, and we drove it for the full 900 s anyway).\n  Nearly all show "0 repairs" — the repair machinery never even engaged. Several show\n  "stream 0.0s" — no chunk ever flowed after adoption.\n  WHY pid 1 IS THE TELL, read from timercam-chunk/src/main.cpp:180-186 to understand the interface:\n      // ... TEST_IMAGE_PID there is 1.\n      uint16_t pid = (uint16_t)(crc ^ (crc >> 16));\n      if (pid == 0 || pid == 1) pid ^= 0x8000;\n  A REAL capture can never be pid 1 BY CONSTRUCTION — 0 and 1 are folded away deliberately. So the\n  thing GARG has pushed 13 times is a test image.\n  NOT OURS TO CONCLUDE and explicitly not asserted: whether GARG is deliberately pushing a test\n  pattern, whether it has a camera attached at all, and what re-triggers it 13 times in a day are\n  all firmware questions. RAISED with the device session as xsession #11 (garg-test-pid), phrased\n  as questions.\n  WHAT IT COSTS: each runs to the full 900 s pushDeadlineMs before giving up. 13 of those is 3+\n  hours of driver time plus the progress queries and repairs we put ON THE AIR chasing an image\n  that may not exist — airtime taken from the unit about to be deployed.\n  CONSEQUENCE FOR OTHER LEDGER ENTRIES: B89 and the "GARG image stall" line of investigation should\n  NOT be treated as camera/link defects until this is answered. They may be observations of a test\n  fixture behaving as designed.\n\nB103 CONFIRMED — we auto-adopt a push and drive it for 900 s even when NO MANIFEST ever arrives.\n  Evidence: "image EDEADLINE: pid 1 1/0 chunks, 0 repairs, 1 dupes, stream 597.3s total 900.3s".\n  count 0 means the manifest never came, so we never knew the total, never knew what to repair, and\n  could not have completed under any circumstances — yet the driver ran the full deadline and put\n  progress queries on the air throughout.\n  images.js onFrame() adopts on MANIFEST *or* CHUNK, so a stray CHUNK alone starts a transfer that\n  has no chance of finishing. A transfer with no manifest after some bounded time is unwinnable and\n  should give up early rather than burn 15 minutes of wake windows.\n  NOT FIXED — own task. Note the interaction with B90\'s give-up guards: those fire on stalled\n  PROGRESS, not on "we never learned the size", so this case slips past them.\n\nCLOSED TODAY, with the measurement that closed each:\n  B91  wake ratio fencepost      1/1 -> 2/2 live on both units          commit e85c8d4\n  B92  txRadio boot snapshot     60 s refresh + txRadioAt               commit a466453 (unverified live)\n  B98  images endpoint hang      75.02 s -> 0.0015 s                    commit 677aee4\n  B100 unvalidated pid           75.02 s -> 0.0017 s, 502 -> 400        commit 495da69\n  B101 unowned promise           bare node exit 1 -> survives           commit 104ca5a\n       (severity DOWNGRADED from "potentially fatal": pm2 absorbs it, no restart follows either\n        rejection in the log. I had rated it too high on the mechanism alone.)'}
2026-07-29 14:22:03,967 28111 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B104 CONFIRMED, AND IT IS THE MOST WASTEFUL DEFECT FOUND TODAY — the image push control path\n  BYPASSES THE BUTLER, so its retries are blind to the wake schedule and ~59 of every 60\n  transmissions are sent at a device that cannot hear them.\n  FOUND BY PETER, from instinct, not from the code: "have you been hammering the bench with\n  @b80f push q 1 every few minutes for the last 1 hour?" and "I thought it pushes at it\'s own pace\n  and we listen until the end and then ask for missing frames, this looks to me like we are\n  pestering it". He was right on both counts.\n  MEASURED LIVE, a transfer in flight at the time of writing:\n      GET /v1/mesh/images/!987ab80f/progress\n      {"pid":1,"received":1,"count":32,"percent":3,"deviceCursor":null,"repairsSent":0,\n       "startedAt":1785330757865,"firstRxAt":1785330757864,"lastRxAt":1785330757892}\n  ONE chunk, lastRxAt 28 ms after firstRxAt, then silence. deviceCursor null = GARG has never\n  answered a single progress query. Running 396 s at the time of measurement => ~26 `push q 1`\n  already sent, capping at 30 (maxUnanswered) over 450 s. 13 such transfers today.\n  THE MISMATCH, and it is the whole defect:\n      GARG wakes  every 15 MINUTES\n      we query    every 15 SECONDS   (quietMs, once a manifest exists)\n  So ~59 of every 60 queries land while the unit is DEAF and are simply lost. Not a lost packet —\n  a transmission that never had a chance.\n  ROOT CAUSE, located exactly:\n      mesh/index.js:107   send: (node, text) => this._sendRaw(node, text)   // fire-and-forget DM\n      mesh/lib/images.js:32  this.send = deps.send;   // "fire-and-forget over the DM path"\n  The BUTLER is the component that knows nextWake, aims transmissions at wake windows and retries\n  ACROSS them — CLAUDE.md: "The butler\'s cross-window retry is the entire delivery guarantee."\n  isAwake is wired into the butler (butler.js:61, used :264) and NOT into Images. The image\n  receiver therefore fires on a fixed timer with no idea whether anyone can hear it.\n  WHAT IS CORRECT AND SHOULD NOT BE CHANGED: the LISTEN half. tick() gates on\n  (now - lastRxMs), so while the device is streaming we stay silent and only speak after quiet.\n  Peter\'s description of the intended protocol is what the code does. The defect is purely the\n  CADENCE of the asking afterwards.\n  COST: 13 transfers x ~30 queries = ~390 transmissions today, at ~2.156 s airtime each on\n  SF11/BW250 = ~14 MINUTES of channel occupancy, almost all of it aimed at a deaf radio. Plus\n  whatever it costs GARG\'s battery on the few that DO land — and GARG was originally calculated at\n  1-1.5 years, which this undermines. It also corrupts our own beat measurement: GARG\'s beat reads\n  302388 ms "measured" when Peter says it should be 900 s, and a unit responding to our pestering\n  is not transmitting on its own schedule.\n  FIX DIRECTION (not yet implemented, needs Peter\'s call): gate the push receiver\'s queries on\n  isAwake/nextWake, or route the control frames through the butler as commands do. The second is\n  more work and more correct — there should be ONE thing in this service that decides when a\n  transmission is aimed at a unit, and it already exists.\n  IMMEDIATE MITIGATIONS available without that work: (a) stop the autonomous image listener until\n  B102 answers why pid 1 is pushed at all, (b) raise quietMs so the cadence is far cheaper.'}
