2026-07-30 13:59:30,200 88716 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'power-levers-dcdc-gpio-nfc', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'F11 HAS NEVER BEEN ON SILICON — caught by the device session, 2026-07-30, and they are right.\n\nI had been treating F11 as "committed and unmeasured". It is weaker than that: it has never been\nFLASHED OR BOOTED at all.\n\nThe sequence, checked: 336B was flashed with v2 at ~10:00 UTC from a build made BEFORE\nNRF_POWER->DCDCEN = 1 was added; 92a588f was committed after. And the 1FC4 unit currently on the\nLTC is running the SEPARATE rak-sleep-floor project\'s firmware, not device/src/main.cpp. So no\nhardware anywhere has ever executed that line.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN "UNMEASURED": the documented risk on F11 is that the nRF52840\'s DC/DC\nneeds external inductors on the DCC pins, which I have NOT verified are fitted on the RAK4631. If\nthey are absent the regulator cannot hold the rail and the chip browns out. An unmeasured change\nis a gap in knowledge; an unbooted one with a brownout risk is a change that could stop a unit\ncoming back.\n\nDEVICE SESSION\'S RECOMMENDATION, ADOPTED: flash the bench unit ON USB POWER FIRST. That is the\nsafest possible failure mode — a brownout simply means it fails to enumerate, which is visible\nimmediately and recoverable with a double-tap RESET into the bootloader. Only after it is proven\nto boot does it go anywhere near the LTC, a battery, or a soak.\n\nDO NOT let F11 reach a deployed unit on the strength of "it builds and the write is in the\nbinary". Static verification proves the instruction exists; it proves nothing about whether the\nsilicon survives executing it.'}
2026-07-30 13:59:49,366 88716 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'sleep-floor-unexplained', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'QSPI CANDIDATE (step 4) — RESCOPED by the device session, 2026-07-30. My "one command, not a pin\naudit" framing was wrong and would have under-estimated the work.\n\nWHAT THEY CHECKED: no SPIFlash / Adafruit_SPIFlash object is instantiated anywhere in src/ or\nlib/. Nothing in the firmware ever drives the QSPI peripheral or sends the flash chip a command.\n\nWHAT THAT DOES AND DOES NOT MEAN:\n  - LOWERS the odds that firmware is the cause — we are not holding the peripheral awake, because\n    we never touch it.\n  - DOES NOT clear it. The external flash chip\'s POST-RESET STANDBY current is unknown, and\n    nobody has ever sent it a deep-power-down command. Standby is not DPD; a serial flash that has\n    never been told to sleep sits in standby indefinitely.\n\nTHE CORRECTION TO SCOPE: because there is NO existing driver object, sending a DPD is not a\none-liner on existing code. It means either\n  (a) adding a minimal Adafruit_SPIFlash begin() followed by a deep-power-down call, or\n  (b) a raw NRF_QSPI register poke.\nBoth are new code on a path the firmware currently does not use at all. Scope it accordingly\nbefore committing to it as "the cheap next lever" — which is how I described it, wrongly.\n\nConsequence for ordering: this is no longer obviously cheaper than F7. Both are now real work, and\nthe base-board-alone measurement (step 2) stays the cheapest thing that could explain the floor,\nbecause it costs no code at all.'}
