2026-07-30 14:18:21,365 88795 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'B2 [CONFIRMED, high severity] src/main.cpp -- PIN_3V3S_EN (the WisBlock sensor rail: SHTC3 + BME680 + PIR, per its own comment) is dropped LOW in doSleep() on every sleep and NEVER restored to HIGH afterward. Only 2 occurrences of PIN_3V3S_EN exist in the whole file (grep -n "3V3S" src/main.cpp, exhaustive, done twice, same result both times): the initial HIGH in mtRails.begin() (setup(), boot-only) and the digitalWrite(...,LOW) in doSleep(). setup() never re-runs after the first sleep (System-ON tickless, confirmed by the file\'s own comments at the Wire.begin()/Serial.begin() wake-restore lines, which explicitly restore Wire and Serial but do NOT restore the rail). doSleep()\'s wake section (after g_loopTop = millis()) restores SERIAL_CAM, Wire, and Serial -- never the 3V3S rail.\n\nCONSEQUENCE: after the FIRST sleep cycle in the unit\'s life, SHTC3/BME680 (readEnv(), main.cpp:1716) and the PIR sensor are running unpowered. readEnv() would return whatever an unpowered I2C device returns (garbage/failure, per the code\'s own "RAIL FIRST, THEN THE BUS... a read issued into an unpowered BME680 returns garbage" comment). PIR detection likely stops working entirely, since the PIR module itself has no power -- this is the ALARM\'S PRIMARY FUNCTION.\n\nORIGIN: commit 926d2c7 (peripherals-off-before-sleep, task 852) added the LOW write. Its own commit message says "Restore is RAIL FIRST, then the bus, with a 5 ms settle" -- describing intent that was never actually implemented in the wake path. The setup()-time restore (now mtRails.sensorRailOn(), moved by lib-rails) satisfies that description ONLY for the first boot, not for any wake after a sleep.\n\nSTATUS: task 852 currently shows steps 1-3 (Investigate/Document/Implement) marked done by my earlier audit, based on the 3 commits existing. That audit did not catch this gap -- flagging here since 852\'s Implement is not actually complete for the 3V3S sub-fix. Found while investigating lib-rails (879) as part of the pure-move; NOT fixed there -- adding a wake-time restore call is new logic, not a move, and belongs to 852\'s own scope. Not yet verified whether this has reached the deployed unit -- 926d2c7\'s commit message does not state flash/measurement status either way, unlike its two sibling commits which explicitly said "NOT FLASHED, NOT MEASURED".'}
2026-07-30 14:18:29,016 88795 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_switch args={'name': 'peripherals-off-before-sleep'}
2026-07-30 14:18:37,110 88795 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': "CORRECTION to my 2026-07-30 audit, which marked steps 1-3 done based on 3 commits existing without verifying wake-path correctness. Found while investigating lib-rails (879), logged in full as bug ledger B2: the 3V3S sensor-rail sub-fix (926d2c7) drops the rail LOW in doSleep() but never restores it HIGH on wake -- setup() never re-runs (System-ON tickless), and doSleep()'s wake section restores SERIAL_CAM/Wire/Serial but not the rail. After the first sleep, SHTC3/BME680/PIR run unpowered. PIR is the alarm's primary function.\nThis means step 3 (Implement) is NOT actually complete for the 3V3S portion, despite being marked done -- Wire.end/Serial guard portions ARE correct, only the rail restore is missing. A wake-time mtRails.sensorRailOn() call (mt-rails now owns the rail after lib-rails) needs adding to doSleep()'s wake section, alongside the existing Wire.begin()/Serial.begin() restores. That is new logic, not this task's job to add via lib-rails' pure-move scope -- it belongs here."}
