2026-07-30 19:55:52,385 1041 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'soak-test-2-v2-operating-floor', 'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PRODUCE THE ONE NUMBER THAT MAKES v2\'s ~1 mA ATTRIBUTABLE.\n\nPeter\'s design, 2026-07-30: "the soak mode should emulate what v2 does / supposed to do and hold\nit\'s memory etc etc as the next test. the current test proves that if it is truly in deep sleep\nwith everything off, its consumption is tiny, but that will only tell us it\'s possible, but wont\nhelp with the v2 design."\n\nThat is exactly right and it is a better test than the one I built.\n\nTHE SUBTRACTION THIS EXISTS TO ENABLE:\n\n    test 1  System-OFF, everything off        absolute floor        (running, <91 uA at 1h52m)\n    test 2  System-ON + RAM + RTC + radio     v2\'s OPERATING floor  <- THIS TASK\n            asleep, NO application\n    test 3  v2 itself                         ~1 mA, already known\n\n    v2 - test2 = THE COST OF OUR APPLICATION CODE.\n\nNothing measured today is attributable. This makes one thing attributable, which is worth more\nthan another bound.\n\nWHY TEST 1 CANNOT ANSWER IT: System-OFF loses RAM and can only be revived by reset. v2 must retain\nstate and wake ITSELF on a schedule, so it can never operate that way. Test 1\'s number is a floor\nBENEATH v2, not a target FOR it.\n\nAND THE MODE DIFFERENCE IS NOT THE PROBLEM — a correction I had to make twice today. On nRF52840,\nSystem-ON idle with RAM retained and RTC running is ~2 uA against System-OFF\'s ~0.4 uA. About\n1.5 uA apart. So v2 running the shallower mode explains none of its milliamp. RAK\'s own published\n40 uA reference is ITSELF System-ON sleep with a live LoRaWAN app — the deeper mode is not required\nto hit spec.\n\nBUILD IT TO MATCH v2, NOT TO WIN:\n  - System-ON tickless (FreeRTOS tickless idle is already the Adafruit core default)\n  - RAM retained\n  - RTC/timer wake on 900 s, v2\'s beat\n  - radio begin() then sleep(TRUE) — WARM, exactly what v2 calls at src/main.cpp:366.\n    Do NOT use sleep(false) here even though it is marginally lower. The point is to reproduce\n    v2\'s conditions, not to produce the best number.\n  - wake, do nothing at all, sleep again\n  - no mesh, no sensors, no camera, no logging\n\nDO NOT DELETE TEST 1. Same project, separate env or build flag. Test 1 is the lower bound and its\nown result is still pending as of this writing.\n\nACCEPTANCE: at least TWO clean pulses. Peter\'s standard, and correct — one interval is one sample,\nand the FIRST pulse can carry pre-run charge because the LTC\'s integrator holds up to one quantum\n(/CLR unsoldered on this build). Quote the interval-derived figure, never avg_ma, never used_mah.\n\nCAVEAT TO STATE WITH THE RESULT: even a perfect number here is "below X", not X. The LTC4150 cannot\nresolve 40 uA from 80 uA inside a day — one pulse is 4.3 hours at 40 uA. A definitive figure needs\na microamp-resolution instrument (Nordic PPK2 or similar).\n\nRELATED, NOT PART OF THIS TASK: with a BENCH SUPPLY rather than a cell (Peter, 2026-07-30), the\nsupply voltage can be swept deliberately. A forum report has RAK4631 current climbing below ~3.4 V.\nSweeping 3.9 -> 3.4 V on a rig whose radio is provably asleep would confirm or kill that on our own\nhardware, with no code change. Worth its own task.'}
