2026-07-30 21:13:47,039 1041 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'image-send-catch-regression', 'title': "MY REGRESSION from 14287f1: images.js calls .catch() on butler.enqueue's synchronous return — image retrieval 502s even when the transfer succeeded", 'steps': ['Investigate: DONE 2026-07-30. Reported by the device session after an end-to-end cam grab+push: pid 40939, 12 chunks, ready:true on GET /v1/mesh/images/336b — but GET /v1/mesh/images/336b/40939 returns 502 {"error":"this.send(...).catch is not a function"}. Reproduced twice by them. ROOT CAUSE: mesh/index.js:119 injects send as (node,text) => this.butler.enqueue(...), which is SYNCHRONOUS and returns no promise. mesh/lib/images.js:145-146 still does this.send(node, text).catch(...), written when send was the raw DM path that DID return a promise. I changed the injection in 14287f1 (B104 push-via-butler) and never checked the call site.', 'Document: spec the fix. Candidate: Promise.resolve(this.send(...)).catch(...) — safe whether or not enqueue ever becomes async. Alternative: drop the .catch entirely since enqueue is sync and throws synchronously. Decide which, and check EVERY other this.send call site in images.js (line 386 uses await this.send(...), which also needs checking against a sync return).', 'Implement: mesh/lib/images.js only.', 'Observe: reproduce the 502 FIRST (it must fail before the fix, or the test proves nothing), then GET /v1/mesh/images/<node>/<pid> must return bytes. A cached ready:true transfer already exists on 336b pid 40939 per the device session, so no mesh round-trip is needed to test.', 'Track: hash, commit, and tell the device session on xsession — they found it and are blocked on it.']}
