2026-07-30 22:00:53,887 1041 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'title': 'B105 CONFIRMED, FIXED (9fcfe8a): image control frames went out UNADDRESSED for 30 hours — the real reason no image could be retrieved', 'description': 'mesh/index.js:119. 14287f1 (B104 push-via-butler) replaced _sendRaw with a bare butler.enqueue and dropped _sendRaw\'s _addressed() call with it. host.config.json sets units["!8cee336b"].dm = false (B79 PKI workaround), so _addressed returns a BROADCAST whose only addressing is the leading `@token` — and we transmitted `push 43238` on channel 2, naming nobody.\n\nEVIDENCE, measured 2026-07-30: unfixed, 6 START attempts -> 0 chunks, run TWICE (short name `336b` AND full id `!8cee336b`, the second specifically to kill the short-name-keying theory — it is dead). Meanwhile GET /v1/mesh/images/336b answered in 4.9 s, so the command path was never implicated, and the pulls that DID get chunks (11/12 at 20:07) were the device\'s own push, which we only adopt. Fixed: HTTP 200, 2696 bytes, JPEG 320x240, 12/12 chunks, 2 repairs.\n\nPaired defect, same commit, same two lines: .catch() on butler.enqueue\'s synchronous return -> TypeError on every pull. Promise.resolve().catch() would NOT have sufficed — enqueue also throws EQUEUEFULL synchronously, before Promise.resolve is reached.\n\nLESSON WORTH KEEPING: I changed a producer and never checked the consumer, and the visible symptom (TypeError) masked the expensive one (lost addressing). Fixing the first made the second visible; had I stopped at the first I would have reported success on a path that still returned no image.'}
2026-07-30 22:07:08,514 1041 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'progress-key-normalise', 'title': 'B106: /images/:target/progress matches the target by RAW STRING, so a transfer is findable only under the key whoever started it happened to use', 'steps': ['Investigate: DONE 2026-07-30, reported by node-dash with a measurement. They polled GET /v1/mesh/images/!8cee336b/progress every 2s across my pid 43238 pull (20:55:51-20:58:35Z) and got {"transfers":[]} continuously - 200 every time, through 1/12, 12/12 and the save. ROOT CAUSE: images.js progress() filters with raw string equality `if (node && e.node !== node) continue`, and e.node is whatever string reached _startTransfer. A REQUESTED pull stores the caller\'s path segment verbatim (GET /images/336b/43238 -> \'336b\'); an ADOPTED push stores `from || String(f.pid)`, the gateway sender id, i.e. the full \'!8cee336b\'. Same unit, two keys, decided by origin. There is no correct key a consumer can pass, which is why node-dash could not find one.', 'Document: spec it. Resolve BOTH sides to a canonical node id before comparing, via a SYNC resolver - progress() is deliberately synchronous (B98: it must answer while listImages() blocks for minutes) so it must do no I/O. index.js _resolve() shows resolveTarget(node, this._roster, this._knownIds()) is already sync; the await is only the roster-refresh fallback. Also decide whether to publish a canonical `unit` field on each progress row so a consumer never has to guess.', 'Implement: mesh/lib/images.js + mesh/index.js. Do NOT change where payloads are stored - payloads/336b/ vs payloads/!8cee336b/ both exist and unifying them is a separate change with its own measurement.', 'Observe: the failure must be reproduced FIRST. Start a pull keyed one way, poll /progress the OTHER way, and confirm [] before the fix and a populated transfers[] after. Both key forms plus the bare 8-hex must find the same live transfer.', 'Track: hash, commit, and tell node-dash on xsession - they raised [progress-target-key] and are waiting on it. Their poller should need no change.']}
