2026-07-31 06:30:25,139 65290 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'ltc-pol-honesty', 'title': "POL honesty: 'charge' is the default answer — armed line, help wording, trust floor (awaiting run end to flash)", 'steps': ["Amend SPEC + api_help: state verbatim that undriven POL classifies as charge — 'the value it returns when it has nothing to say'; armed line shows 'N charge pulses seen'; trust-floor convention (sign <~0.5-1 mA, magnitude <~0.2 mA at 50 mOhm, set against WORST drift: in-situ offset ~55 uA apparent, x2 over 8 h, from services' 12h record)", 'Implement + flash + verify ONLY after Peter ends the rak-floor-radi run']}
2026-07-31 06:34:32,689 65290 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'ltc-pol-honesty', 'text': "DATASHEET-CORRECTED FLOOR (services pulled 4150fb/fc, sources on the xsession board): VOS spec MAX +/-100 uV at 25C/VDD 4V, +/-150 uV over temp, NO typical specified. On 50 mOhm: sign untrustworthy below ~2 mA (25C) / ~3 mA (over temp) BY SPEC — my drift-inferred 0.5-1 mA was 2-6x optimistic and is superseded. THIS part measures ~2.75 uV actual (36x better than spec) which is why 0.44 mA classified correctly — a good part at a hopeless task; the PUBLISHED floor must be spec-based. Help wording per services' proposal: express the floor as a FORMULA (sign needs |I| >> VOS/Rsense; 2 mA at 25C, 3 mA over temp on 50 mOhm) so it scales with any future shunt/set_quanta and cannot go stale. VSENSE input range +/-50 mV confirmed (1 Ohm clips at 50 mA; by-spec sign at 40 uA needs >=2.5 Ohm which clips at 20 mA — a 1 Ohm floor rig requires characterising the actual part's offset first; the no-load test IS that characterisation). GVF 32.55 Hz/V confirms quanta 0.17067759 (50 mOhm) and 0.00853388 (1 Ohm) exactly."}
2026-07-31 06:37:42,847 1041 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_switch args={'name': 'floor-rig-really-sleeps'}
2026-07-31 06:39:34,211 1041 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': "DOCUMENTED 2026-07-31, commit 90a1c3d in rak-sleep-floor. SPEC-really-sleeps.md now carries an AMENDMENT stating plainly that this test CANNOT BE MEASURED BY THE LTC4150, and the firmware that actually ran the soak (LittleFS stripped) is committed alongside it — it had been running uncommitted, so the spec had been describing a result produced by code not under version control.\n\nTHE NUMBER THAT SETTLES IT — LTC4150 datasheet 4150fb/fc, Electrical Characteristics, VOS Differential Offset Voltage: MAX +/-100 uV at VDD 4.0V/25C, +/-150 uV over temperature, +/-200 uV across 2.7-8.5V over temperature. MIN and TYP columns are BLANK — only a MAX is published, so no small offset may be assumed on an uncharacterised part. On the fitted 50 mOhm shunt that is +/-2.0 mA equivalent at 25C. Our 40-80 uA signal is 2-4 uV. The permitted offset is 25-75x the whole signal.\n\nTHE ONLY DEFENSIBLE FIGURE THIS RUN SUPPORTS: I < ~1.94 mA (25C spec), < 2.94 mA over temp. Derived properly: 4 pulses in 12.4 h fixes |V_signal + V_offset| = 2.75 uV charge-signed, so I_true x R = V_offset - 2.75 uV, and bounding V_offset by spec gives the above. USELESS FOR OUR PURPOSE — a true 1 mA needs V_offset = 52.75 uV, well inside spec, so this run cannot separate a sleeping board from the ~1 mA that is the entire open question. It rules out the broken 1.99 mA rig and barely that.\n\nWHAT SURVIVES: the radio genuinely sleeps, attested by begin=0 sleep=0 read back FROM THE CHIP, not from the counter. And earlier mA-scale readings are probably sound — this individual part's ACTUAL effective offset is ~2.75 uV (from the 55 uA apparent rate), about 36x better than its spec limit, which is exactly why yesterday's 22 uV runs classified direction correctly and this one did not.\n\nROUTES TO A REAL MEASUREMENT, recorded in the spec: (1) PPK2 - the right instrument class; (2) NO-LOAD SUBTRACTION on this rig, measuring V_offset with the DUT side open then subtracting - ~24 h gives ~8 pulses and roughly +/-7 uA, but the limiting error is DRIFT not counting (apparent rate doubled over 8 h overnight) so the runs must be back-to-back under matched ambient, and the first no-load pulse must be discarded because the tester's /CLR pin is unsoldered; (3) a >=2.5 Ohm shunt - and note 1 Ohm is NOT sufficient, giving only 40 uV against a 100 uV spec offset, while 2.5 Ohm clips at 20 mA against the +/-50 mV VSENSE range.\n\nOWNERSHIP FLAG FOR PETER: rak-sleep-floor is a separate PlatformIO project at /usr/share/pac/dev/pio/projects/rak-sleep-floor. The device session has now flagged TWICE that task 863 targeting it is mis-homed and that neither device nor services owns it. I created the repo and authored this spec, and I have committed to it again here on Peter's explicit instruction to document the test. Needs an owner decided rather than defaulting to whoever last touched it."}
2026-07-31 06:54:14,028 1041 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_show args={'name': 'soak-test-2-v2-operating-floor'}
2026-07-31 06:58:23,185 1041 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'soak-test-2-v2-operating-floor', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PETER REDIRECTED THIS TEST, 2026-07-31, and his framing is better than the one in the goal note above. Verbatim: "what I want to see first, is with the soak device having identical settings to what the live device has / should have (including new discoveries you have made and used in this soak test) does it still show 1.1ma as per the live device, or do we drop down into micro amps where the soak test board is". Also: "the testing method is a different matter" — the duty-cycle sweep I proposed is DEFERRED, not part of this.\n\nSO THIS IS A BISECTION, NOT A MEASUREMENT. It separates "inherent to this hardware in this operating mode" from "caused by our application code". That reframing matters because it changes what instrument is needed.\n\nTHE ACCEPTANCE IN THE GOAL NOTE ABOVE IS NOW WRONG AND MUST BE REPLACED. "At least two clean pulses" is UNACHIEVABLE if the board behaves: at 40-220 uA the signal is 2-11 uV, below the LTC4150\'s floor (VOS max +/-100 uV = +/-2 mA equivalent on the fitted 50 mOhm — see floor-rig-really-sleeps and commit 90a1c3d). The run would sit armed for hours returning charge-signed offset pulses, exactly as the night of 2026-07-30 did.\n\nNEW ACCEPTANCE — TWO SIGNATURES, and they are unmistakably different. This is the whole reason the test is decisive with the instrument we already have:\n  STILL ~1.1 mA -> 55 uV -> clean DISCHARGE pulses roughly every 9 min, used_mah CLIMBING, run clock starts.\n  DROPPED TO uA -> 2-4 uV -> offset-dominated: CHARGE-signed pulses hours apart, used_mah stuck at 0.00, run_secs never starts.\nWe only know how to read the second signature BECAUSE of the failed run on 2026-07-30. That run\'s real value was teaching us the null case.\n\nReport the signature, NOT a current figure, unless the discharge case fires — in which case the interval-derived figure is legitimate because 55 uV is far above this part\'s actual ~2.75 uV offset (that is why yesterday\'s 0.44 mA / 22 uV runs classified correctly).\n\nBLOCKED ON: [v2-power-settings-authoritative], raised with the device session 2026-07-31 as device--services #77. I asked for the definitive list of power-relevant configuration in the SHIPPING v2 image — rails, GPIO states entering sleep, peripheral teardown, radio call and mode, DCDC, NFC, QSPI — plus, separately, anything they think SHOULD be set but is not, since Peter said "has / should have" and the gap between those two lists is itself a finding. I must NOT infer this from reading their code; that rule has cost this project days.\n\nItems I asked them to confirm or correct: radio sleep(TRUE) WARM at src/main.cpp:366 (the rig used COLD - match v2, do not win); F11 DCDCEN=1 committed but to my knowledge NEVER BOOTED ON SILICON; F4 CONFIG_NFCT_PINS_AS_GPIOS, suspected no-op per B4; F7 lib-gpio 1242fa0; B2 3V3_S restore 10ded12; System-ON tickless with RTC wake at 900 s; QSPI never deep-power-downed on either side.\n\nALSO ASKED, and possibly more important than any of it: what the unit draws WHILE AWAKE, and for how long. Never measured here. 8 s awake in every 900 s at 20 mA contributes 178 uA - 71% of the entire 251 uA budget for a 1-year life on 2200 mAh, before any sleep current at all. If the awake cost dominates, the sleep hunt is aimed at the wrong half of the problem.'}
2026-07-31 07:09:22,719 1041 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'device-registry-removable', 'title': 'B108: _markOurs is append-only with no removal path, so a bench board flashed once is in every node-dash page forever — and stale roster fields make it look alive', 'steps': ['Investigate: DONE 2026-07-31, reported by Peter (\'it shows up everywhere in node-dash\'). mesh/index.js:912 _markOurs() adds any node that speaks our protocol to this._ours and persists via store.saveDevices(); index.js:260 reloads it on boot. THERE IS NO REMOVAL PATH ANYWHERE. Persisted registry right now: ["!8cee336b","!987ab80f","!18a01fc4"]. !18a01fc4 is the SOAK BOARD - flashed with v2 once, heard at 2026-07-30 15:36, silent since 17:00:42 when the floor-rig firmware took over. node-dash builds its UI from /v1/mesh/devices exactly as http.md instructs, so it renders a dead bench board on every page. SECOND HALF: it looks ALIVE, not merely present - live payload has lastHeardMs null and awake null (from our model) but hops 0, rssi -57, snr 6.2 (from the GATEWAY ROSTER, which still holds a node it last heard 14.5 h ago), plus a junk beat 57408 learned from sparse sightings. That is item (4) of devices-honest-fields (832), now with a victim.', 'Document: spec it. Two parts: (a) a removal path so a device can leave the registry, (b) honest staleness so a silent unit cannot present a fresh RSSI. DECIDE: does removal need to be sticky? _markOurs will re-add !18a01fc4 the moment it speaks again, which it WILL during the T3/T4 radio rungs of the soak ladder - so a plain delete is temporary by construction. Consider a suppress/ignore list that survives re-hearing, or an explicit opt-in registry. Keep this SEPARATE from 832, which is much broader.', 'Implement: mesh/index.js, mesh/lib/store.js, mesh/host-module.js.', 'Observe: the failure must be reproduced FIRST - confirm !18a01fc4 is present in GET /v1/mesh/devices and carries a non-null rssi with a null lastHeardMs. After: it must be absent (or explicitly marked stale), AND must NOT silently return after a simulated re-hearing if the spec chose sticky removal. Re-query live, do not infer.', 'Track: hash, commit, tell node-dash on xsession - they consume /devices and a shape change or a new field is a contract change under docs/api/http.md, not binding until they ack.']}
2026-07-31 07:10:22,078 1041 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_file_attach args={'file_path': 'services/docs/history/device-registry-removable.md', 'kind': 'plan', 'label': 'Spec: sticky removal via an ignore list that survives re-hearing'}
2026-07-31 07:15:51,522 1041 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'soak-ladder-4-tests', 'title': "Soak ladder: 4 builds on the 1FC4 rig, each adding ONE thing, to attribute where v2's ~1.1 mA actually goes", 'steps': ['Document: write the spec. Four builds T1-T4 on pio/projects/rak-sleep-floor, selected by build flag, test 1 (System-OFF) preserved untouched. T1 sleep only / T2 +wake 8 s do nothing / T3 +radio RX for the window / T4 +transmit one packet. Record the v2-vs-soak-NOW-vs-soak-NEXT table. HARD CONSTRAINT: T3/T4 use RAW RadioLib on the private sync word, NO Meshtastic stack, NO NodeInfo, NO advertising - 1FC4 shares the BNCH identity with !8cee336b (one identity per firmware image) and rejoining the mesh would pollute the roster, the wake model and the recorder AND change the power profile being measured.', 'Implement T1+T2: System-ON tickless with RAM retained and RTC wake, replacing System-OFF. Keep the radio status readback and the refuse-to-sleep-on-failure guard - that guard is what caught the last false result.', 'Implement T3+T4: radio RX for the window, then TX one packet per wake, raw RadioLib only.', "Observe: run the ladder. T4 AT THE REAL 900 s BEAT FIRST - that is Peter's actual question and it resolves in ONE HOUR (1.1 mA = a discharge pulse every 9.3 min, so ~6 in an hour; zero discharge pulses in an hour rules it out outright). Then T2/T3/T4 at a COMPRESSED 30 s period where each rung is 1.4-3.4 mA and comfortably measurable; scale each delta by 30/900. Sleep current and LTC offset are both constant across the four runs so they CANCEL in every subtraction - the deltas are trustworthy even though T1 itself is not.", 'Track: hash, commit, record the subtraction chain (T2-T1 = wake cost, T3-T2 = RX window cost, T4-T3 = transmit cost, v2-T4 = application cost) and report which rung the current actually appears at.']}
